---
<p>Every diagnostic the compiler reports has a code, <code>BYN</code> and four digits. This page lists each code once: what it means, a small program that triggers it with the compiler's real message, and how to fix it. The long form of any entry is one command away: <code>bayan explain BYN2001</code>.</p>
<h2 id="reading">Reading a diagnostic</h2>
<p>A diagnostic has a severity (<code>error</code> or <code>warning</code>), the code, a one-line message, the file position, the source line with the span marked, and often a <code>help</code> line with a fix you can apply. Errors stop the build. Warnings do not. <code>--all</code> shows every error instead of the first 20. <code>--diagnostics json</code> prints one JSON object per diagnostic on standard error, with the code, severity, file, span, message, label, help and fixes, for editors and scripts.</p>
<p>The first two digits of a code name the area. The sections below follow that order.</p>
