<h2 id="g00">Source text (BYN00xx)</h2>
<p>Problems reading the file before any grammar applies: bad characters, unterminated strings, invalid UTF-8.</p>
<h3 id="byn0001">BYN0001: bidirectional control character outside a string literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bidi controls (U+202A–U+202E, U+2066–U+2069) are invisible and reorder how text displays. In code or comments they can make source read differently from how it compiles ("Trojan Source", CVE-2021-42574). Bayan allows them only inside string literals, where they are data.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Delete the character. If you need it in text, put it in a string literal, ideally as an escape: <code>"\u{202E}"</code>.</p>

<h3 id="byn0002">BYN0002: unexpected character</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The character cannot start any token: it is not an operator, punctuation, digit, quote or identifier letter.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the character, or put it inside a string or comment.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = 1 ` 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0002:</span> unexpected character ```
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ let a = 1 ` 2
    │           ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0003">BYN0003: unterminated string literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A regular string must close on the line it opens. A raw <code>"""</code> string must close before the end of the file. An interpolated string's <code>{</code> must be closed before the string ends.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the closing <code>"</code>. For text spanning lines use a raw string:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = """
    several
    lines
    """</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = "open</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0003:</span> unterminated string literal
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let a = "open
    │         ^^^^^ string ends at the end of the line
    │
  help: close it with `"`; for text spanning lines use a raw `"""` string</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0004">BYN0004: unterminated block comment</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>/*</code> comment runs until <code>*/</code>. Without one it swallows the rest of the file.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>*/</code> where the comment should end, or use <code>//</code> line comments.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">/* started here
let a = 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0004:</span> unterminated block comment
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ /* started here
    │ ^^ comment starts here
    │
  help: close it with `*/`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0005">BYN0005: identifier not in normalization form C</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Unicode can encode the same visible letter in more than one way (<code>é</code> as one scalar or as <code>e</code> + combining accent). Two identifiers that look identical but differ in bytes would be different names. Bayan requires NFC so that names that look the same are the same.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the composed form. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix; most editors also have a "normalize to NFC" command.</p>

<h3 id="byn0006">BYN0006: invalid escape sequence</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only a fixed set of escapes exists, so a typo cannot silently become a different character.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of <code>\n \r \t \0 \\ \" \'</code> or <code>\u{…}</code> with a hex scalar value. To write a backslash, use <code>\\</code> or a raw <code>"""</code> string.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let s = "bad \q escape"</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0006:</span> invalid escape sequence `\q`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:14

  1 │ let s = "bad \q escape"
    │              ^^
    │
  help: use a valid escape: \n \r \t \0 \\ \" \' and \u{…}</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0007">BYN0007: empty char literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>rune</code> is one Unicode scalar; <code>''</code> holds none.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Put the character between the quotes, e.g. <code>' '</code>, or use an empty string <code>""</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let c = ''</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0007:</span> empty char literal
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let c = ''
    │         ^^
    │
  help: write the character between the quotes, e.g. `' '`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0008">BYN0008: unterminated char literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A char literal must close with <code>'</code> on the same line.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the closing <code>'</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let c = 'x</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0008:</span> unterminated char literal
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let c = 'x
    │         ^^
    │
  help: close it with `'`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0008</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0009">BYN0009: char literal holds more than one scalar</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>rune</code> is exactly one Unicode scalar value. Text with several characters, including letters built from combining marks or emoji sequences, is a string.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a string literal: <code>"ab"</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let c = 'ab'</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0009:</span> a char literal holds exactly one Unicode scalar
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let c = 'ab'
    │         ^^^^
    │
  help: use a string literal ("…") for text</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0009</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0010">BYN0010: invalid numeric literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The literal breaks one of the number rules: <code>_</code> separators must sit between digits; <code>0x</code> and <code>0b</code> need at least one digit of the right base; an exponent needs digits.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>1_000</code>, <code>0x0</code>, <code>1e3</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = 1__000
let b = 0x
let c = 1e</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0010:</span> invalid numeric literal: expected digits after `0x`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ let b = 0x
    │         ^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0010</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0011">BYN0011: unit suffix on a hex or binary literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Unit suffixes such as <code>dec</code> or <code>s</code> attach meaning to a decimal quantity. Hex and binary literals are bit patterns, so only the built-in width suffixes <code>u</code>, <code>l</code>, <code>ul</code> are allowed on them.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the value in decimal (<code>255s</code>), or drop the suffix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = 0xFFs</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0011:</span> unit suffix `s` is not allowed on a hexadecimal literal
  --&gt; main.byn:1:13

  1 │ let a = 0xFFs
    │             ^
    │
  help: write the value in decimal, or convert it with T(x), where T is the unit type</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0011</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0012">BYN0012: reserved word used as identifier</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every keyword is reserved, including those kept for future features (<code>actor</code>, <code>macro</code>, <code>select</code>, <code>become</code>), so adding a feature later cannot break existing code. The C# primitive type names (<code>sbyte</code>, <code>byte</code>, <code>short</code>, <code>ushort</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>uint</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>ulong</code>, <code>nint</code>, <code>nuint</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>double</code>) are reserved too: they name the sized types in every project, so none can be declared as a name (<code>i32 int</code>, <code>let long = 4</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Choose another name, e.g. <code>actorId</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let actor = 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0012:</span> `actor` is reserved and cannot be used as an identifier
  --&gt; main.byn:1:5

  1 │ let actor = 1
    │     ^^^^^ reserved for a future version of Bayan
    │
  help: choose another name</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0012</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0013">BYN0013: escape is not a Unicode scalar value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>\u{…}</code> must name a Unicode scalar: U+0000–U+D7FF or U+E000–U+10FFFF. Surrogates and values above U+10FFFF cannot be encoded in UTF-8.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the scalar value you meant. For characters outside the BMP write the code point directly (<code>\u{1F600}</code>), not a surrogate pair.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let c = '\u{D800}'</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0013:</span> `\u{D800}` is not a Unicode scalar value
  --&gt; main.byn:1:10

  1 │ let c = '\u{D800}'
    │          ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: use a scalar value in U+0000–U+D7FF or U+E000–U+10FFFF</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0013</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0014">BYN0014: single <code>}</code> in an interpolated string</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> In <code>$"…"</code> strings <code>{</code> opens an expression and <code>}</code> closes it. A lone <code>}</code> in the text is ambiguous, so literal braces are written doubled.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>}}</code> (and <code>{{</code> for a literal <code>{</code>). The diagnostic carries this fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let s = $"stray } here"</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN0014:</span> `}` in an interpolated string must be doubled
  --&gt; main.byn:1:17

  1 │ let s = $"stray } here"
    │                 ^
    │
    │ 1 │ let s = $"stray }} here"
    │   │                  +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN0014</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn0015">BYN0015: source is not valid UTF-8</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan source files are UTF-8. An invalid byte sequence usually means the file was saved in another encoding (ISO-8859-1, Windows-1252, UTF-16).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Re-save the file as UTF-8. The byte offset in the diagnostic shows the first bad byte on each line.</p>

<h3 id="byn0016">BYN0016: interpolated strings nested too deeply</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An interpolated string may contain an expression that is itself an interpolated string. The lexer bounds this nesting (16 levels) so pathological input cannot exhaust it. Only the first occurrence in a file is reported.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Build the inner text in a local first:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let inner = $"{a}-{b}"
let outer = $"[{inner}]"</code></pre>

<h2 id="g10">Syntax (BYN10xx)</h2>
<p>The text does not fit the grammar, or it uses a form Bayan rewrites to its one canonical spelling.</p>
<h3 id="byn1001">BYN1001: expected token</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The parser needed a specific token at this point (a closing brace, a parenthesis, a name) and found something else. When a block is not closed, the help line names the line where it was opened.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Insert the missing token. For an unclosed block, add <code>}</code> where the block should end.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void f() {
    let x = 1

void g() {}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1001:</span> expected `}`, found `void`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:1

  4 │ void g() {}
    │ ^^^^
    │
  help: close the block opened on line 1</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1002">BYN1002: expected an expression</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An operator, initializer or argument list needs a value here, but the next token cannot start an expression.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Supply the missing value, or remove the operator that expects one.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = </code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1002:</span> expected an expression, found end of file
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ 
    │ ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1003">BYN1003: expected a type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A declaration, cast or generic argument needs a type here, but the next token cannot start one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the type, such as <code>i32</code>, <code>string</code> or a struct name.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void f(, i32 x) {}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1003:</span> expected a type, found `,`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ void f(, i32 x) {}
    │        ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1004">BYN1004: expected a struct member</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Inside a struct body, only fields, properties, constructors and methods may appear. The token shown cannot start any of them.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the stray tokens, or complete the member declaration.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct S {
    i64 a
    + 
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1004:</span> expected a field, property, constructor or method, found `+`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:5

  3 │     + 
    │     ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1005">BYN1005: expected a line break after the statement</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A statement ends at the end of its line, at <code>}</code> or at the end of the file. A second statement on the same line has nothing to end the first, so the two would read as one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Put the next statement on its own line, at the same indent. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = 1 let b = 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1005:</span> expected a line break after the statement
  --&gt; main.byn:1:10

  1 │ let a = 1 let b = 2
    │          ^ found keyword `let`
    │
  help: put the next statement on its own line
    │ 1 │ let a = 1
    │ 2 │ let b = 2</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1005</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1006">BYN1006: <code>unless</code> cannot have an <code>else</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>unless c A else B</code> reads as a double negative. When both branches matter, the positive form says the same thing directly.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>if ready { go(); } else { wait(); }</code>. The diagnostic offers this rewrite.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unless ready { wait(); } else { go(); }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1006:</span> `unless` cannot have an `else` branch
  --&gt; main.byn:1:26

  1 │ unless ready { wait(); } else { go(); }
    │                          ^^^^ `else` after `unless`
    │
  help: use `if` with the opposite condition, so both branches read positively</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1007">BYN1007: <code>unless</code> with a negated condition</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>unless (!x)</code> is a double negative and means <code>if (x)</code>. Bayan rejects it so conditions read directly. From design round 3 the same holds for the word spelling <code>not</code>, in both the block and the postfix form; BYN1007 covers both spellings (it absorbed the retired [BYN1049](BYN1049.md)).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use <code>if</code> with the condition un-negated (<code>return 0 if ok</code>). The diagnostic carries the rewrite.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unless !done { step(); }
return 0 unless !ok
return 0 unless not ok</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1007:</span> `unless` with a negated condition is a double negative
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ unless !done { step(); }
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^ `unless` already negates
    │
  help: use `if` with the condition as written
    │ 1 │ if done { step(); }
    │   │ +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1008">BYN1008: postfix condition on a compound statement</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A postfix <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code> (<code>return x if c;</code>) is meant for one short simple statement: a return, break, continue, assignment or call. On a block, loop or declaration it hides control flow at the end of a long construct, and on a declaration it would leave the name unassigned.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a prefix <code>if ready { … }</code>, or declare the variable first and assign it conditionally.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let y = 2 if ready</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1008:</span> a postfix `if` applies only to a simple statement
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ let y = 2 if ready
    │           ^^
    │
  help: declare the variable, then assign it conditionally; a declaration cannot be conditional</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1008</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1009">BYN1009: chained postfix conditions</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>x if a if b;</code> is ambiguous to readers (which condition is checked first?) and adds nothing over <code>&amp;&amp;</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Combine the conditions: <code>return 0 if a &amp;&amp; b;</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">return 0 if a if b</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1009:</span> postfix conditions cannot be chained
  --&gt; main.byn:1:15

  1 │ return 0 if a if b
    │               ^^ a second condition
    │
  help: combine the conditions with `&amp;&amp;` in one postfix `if`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1009</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1010">BYN1010: <code>goto</code> is not supported</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan has no <code>goto</code>. Structured exits cover its legitimate uses: labeled <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> leave nested loops, and <code>defer</code> handles cleanup. <code>goto case</code> is not supported either; switch sections never fall through.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Label the loop and use <code>break label;</code> or <code>continue label;</code>. For shared switch code, move it into a function.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">goto done</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1010:</span> `goto` does not exist in Bayan
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ goto done
    │ ^^^^ not a keyword in Bayan
    │
  help: label the loop (`outer: for x in xs { … }`) and use `break outer` to leave it early
    │ 1 │ break done
    │   │ +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1010</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1011">BYN1011: invalid assignment target</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only variables, fields, properties and indexed elements can be assigned. Calls, literals and operator results are values, not storage.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign to a variable or member instead.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">f() = 3
a + b += 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1011:</span> cannot assign to this expression
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ f() = 3
    │ ^^^
    │
  help: assign to a variable, a field or an indexed element</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1011</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1012">BYN1012: expression has no effect as a statement</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A statement that is only an expression must do something: call a function, <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>, or jump with <code>?? return</code>. Expressions such as <code>a + b;</code> compute a value and throw it away, which is almost always a mistake.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign the value (<code>y = x + 1;</code>), use it, or remove the statement.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">x + 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1012:</span> this expression does nothing as a statement
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ x + 1
    │ ^^^^^ the value is computed and discarded
    │
  help: write `_ = f()` to discard a call's result; only calls, assignments and `++`/`--` can stand
        alone</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1012</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1013">BYN1013: conditional expression in a collection literal</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Inside <code>[ … ]</code>, a bare <code>c ? a : b</code> is easy to misread as several elements. Bayan asks for parentheses so element boundaries stay obvious.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Parenthesize it: <code>[1, (ready ? 2 : 3)]</code>. The diagnostic carries this fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let xs = [1, ready ? 2 : 3]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1013:</span> a conditional expression inside a collection literal must be parenthesized
  --&gt; main.byn:1:14

  1 │ let xs = [1, ready ? 2 : 3]
    │              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: wrap it in parentheses
    │ 1 │ let xs = [1, (ready ? 2 : 3)]
    │   │              +++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1013</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1014">BYN1014: stacked case labels</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> C-style <code>case a: case b:</code> relies on fall-through, which Bayan switch sections do not have. One label with an <code>or</code> pattern states the intent directly.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>case .sat or .sun:</code>. The parser merges stacked labels this way to keep checking the rest of the file.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">switch day {
    case .sat:
    case .sun:
        rest()
        break
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1014:</span> stacked `case` labels
  --&gt; main.byn:3:5

  3 │     case .sun:
    │     ^^^^ a second label for the same statements
    │
  help: combine the patterns into one label, e.g. `case .sat or .sun:`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1014</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1015">BYN1015: C-style <code>for</code> loop</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan's <code>for</code> iterates over a range or collection; there is no <code>for (init; cond; step)</code> form. Ranges cover counting loops and make off-by-one bounds explicit.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>for i in 0..n { … }</code>, or use <code>while</code> for anything else.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for (i32 i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1015:</span> C-style `for` loops do not exist in Bayan
  --&gt; main.byn:1:12

  1 │ for (i32 i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) { … }
    │            ^ expected `in`
    │
  help: loop over a range instead: `for i in 0..n { … }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1015</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1016">BYN1016: modifier not allowed here</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>{0}</code> is not allowed here"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "put <code>@sealed</code> on a struct" (on anything but a struct); "write <code>@sealed</code> with no arguments and no <code>because</code>" (with arguments or <code>because</code>); "put <code>@positional</code> on a struct" (on anything but a struct); "write <code>@positional</code> with no arguments and no <code>because</code>" (with arguments or <code>because</code>); "remove <code>@positional</code>: a struct with states is built with state literals" (on a struct with a <code>states</code> block).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each modifier applies to certain declarations only: <code>public</code> to functions, structs and members, <code>readonly</code> to fields and structs, <code>static</code> to members, and so on. An import, local or top-level function cannot take the modifier shown. <code>@sealed</code> and <code>@positional</code> belong on a struct and take no arguments and no <code>because</code>; a struct with states is built with state literals, so it cannot be <code>@positional</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the modifier, or put <code>@sealed</code> or <code>@positional</code>, bare, on a struct without states.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public import money
readonly void f() {}

@sealed
void run() {}

@positional(strict)
struct Point {
    i64 x
    i64 y
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1016:</span> `public` is not allowed here
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ public import money
    │ ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1016</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1017">BYN1017: parenthesized condition</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> Parentheses around the whole condition of <code>if</code>, <code>unless</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>switch</code> or <code>do … while</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan conditions are bare and their bodies are blocks, so the parentheses carry nothing. One spelling keeps code uniform. They are still allowed when the condition has a struct literal at its top level, where the <code>{</code> would otherwise open the body.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the parentheses (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if count &gt; 0 {
    print("some")
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if (count &gt; 0) {
    print("some")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1017:</span> parentheses around a condition are an input form
  --&gt; main.byn:1:4

  1 │ if (count &gt; 0) {
    │    ^^^^^^^^^^^ not needed
    │
  help: remove the parentheses
    │ 1 │ if count &gt; 0 {
    │   │    +++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1017</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1018">BYN1018: body needs braces</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A single statement as the body of <code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>unless</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>do</code>, or a declaration as the body of <code>defer</code> (<code>defer stmt;</code> is otherwise legal, spec §7.6; <code>defer let x = 1;</code> declares a local nothing can see).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A braceless body invites the classic bug where a second indented line looks guarded but is not. Every body is a block.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the statement in <code>{ }</code> (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if done { return; }</code></pre>
<p>When the header has its own fix (BYN1017, BYN1007, BYN1019, …), that fix also adds the braces, so it is valid applied alone.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if (done) return</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1018:</span> the body of `if` must be a block
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ if (done) return
    │           ^^^^^^ a single statement
    │
  help: wrap the statement in `{ }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1018</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1019">BYN1019: parenthesized <code>for</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>for (let x in xs)</code>, <code>for (var x in xs)</code> or <code>foreach (var x in xs)</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan has one loop over a sequence: <code>for x in xs { … }</code>. The loop variable is a fresh binding each iteration, so <code>let</code>/<code>var</code> adds nothing. A <code>(</code> after <code>for</code> followed by a top-level <code>let</code>, <code>var</code> or <code>in</code> is this input form; <code>for (a, b) in pairs</code> is a tuple destructure.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the bare form (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for order in orders {
    ship(order)
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">foreach (var order in orders) {
    ship(order)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1019:</span> `for (let x in xs)` is an input form; write `for x in xs`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ foreach (var order in orders) {
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write the loop variable and iterable without parentheses
    │ 1 │ for order in orders {
    │   │    ++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1019</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1020">BYN1020: <code>errdefer</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> The keyword <code>errdefer</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Cleanup kinds are spelled after <code>defer</code>: <code>defer</code>, <code>defer failing</code>, <code>defer panicking</code>. <code>errdefer</code> was the old spelling of <code>defer failing</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>defer failing</code>; the <code>(e)</code> binding stays (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">defer failing (e) log($"rolled back: {e}")</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">errdefer (e) log($"rolled back: {e}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1020:</span> `errdefer` is now `defer failing`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ errdefer (e) log($"rolled back: {e}")
    │ ^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write `defer failing`
    │ 1 │ defer failing (e) log($"rolled back: {e}")
    │   │ +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1020</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1021">BYN1021: <code>=</code> in a literal</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>name = value</code> inside a struct literal or a <code>with</code> literal.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A literal names its fields with <code>:</code>, the same as a manifest and a property pattern. <code>=</code> is assignment, which a literal does not do.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>:</code> (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
let q = p with { y: 3 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let p = Point { x = 1, y = 2 }
let q = p with { y = 3 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1021:</span> `x = …` in a literal is an input form; write `x: …`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:19

  1 │ let p = Point { x = 1, y = 2 }
    │                   ^ the input form
    │
  help: write `:` after the field name
    │ 1 │ let p = Point { x: 1, y = 2 }
    │   │                  +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1021</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1022">BYN1022: compound <code>unless</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>unless</code> with a condition joined by <code>&amp;&amp;</code> or <code>||</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>unless a &amp;&amp; b</code> means "if not (a and b)", which readers get wrong. <code>unless</code> is for one plain condition.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use <code>if</code> with the negated condition in parentheses (the machine fix does this), and simplify it if you like:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if !(ready &amp;&amp; open) {
    wait()
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unless ready &amp;&amp; open {
    wait()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1022:</span> `unless` with `&amp;&amp;`/`||` reads as a double negative
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ unless ready &amp;&amp; open {
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a compound condition
    │
  help: use `if` with the negated condition
    │ 1 │ if !(ready &amp;&amp; open) {
    │   │ +++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1022</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1023">BYN1023: <code>unless x is null</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>unless x is null</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> It is a double negative; <code>if x is not null</code> says the same thing directly.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>if … is not null</code> (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if user is not null {
    greet(user)
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unless user is null {
    greet(user)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1023:</span> `unless x is null` is not allowed; write `if x is not null`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ unless user is null {
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a negated null test
    │
  help: use `if` with `is not null`
    │ 1 │ if user is not null {
    │   │ ++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1023</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1025">BYN1025: <code>catch (e)</code> binding</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>(name)</code> binding after <code>catch</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A handler that needs the error matches it: each <code>catch switch</code> arm binds the error at the type it matched, so the binding always has a precise type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>(e)</code> (the machine fix does this when <code>switch</code> follows) and bind the error in the arms that need it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = parse(s) catch switch {
    .empty =&gt; 0,
    ParseError e =&gt; fallback(e),
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = parse(s) catch (e) switch {
    .empty =&gt; 0,
    _ =&gt; fallback(e),
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1025:</span> `catch (e)` is an input form; bind the error in a `catch switch` arm
  --&gt; main.byn:1:24

  1 │ let n = parse(s) catch (e) switch {
    │                        ^^^ the input form
    │
  help: remove `(e)` and bind the error in each arm: `E e =&gt; …`
    │ 1 │ let n = parse(s) catch (e) switch {
    │   │                        ----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1025</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1028">BYN1028: <code>delegate</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan writes function types directly: <code>Order -&gt; bool</code>. A name for one is a type alias. The C# <code>delegate</code> declaration is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>delegate</code> is not Bayan; write a function type <code>type {0} = {1}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> declare it as <code>type {0} = {1}</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write a type alias for the function type:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order { i64 total }

type OrderFilter = Order -&gt; bool</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it. A generic delegate (<code>delegate bool F&lt;T&gt;(T x)</code>) gets no fix: aliases take no type parameters.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order { i64 total }

delegate bool OrderFilter(Order order)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1028:</span> `delegate` is not Bayan; write a function type `type OrderFilter = Order -&gt; bool`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:1

  3 │ delegate bool OrderFilter(Order order)
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ a C# delegate
    │
  help: declare it as `type OrderFilter = Order -&gt; bool`
    │ 3 │ type OrderFilter = Order -&gt; bool
    │   │ ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1028</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1029">BYN1029: <code>error set</code> in place of <code>union error</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> The round-2 <code>error set S = A | B</code>, or <code>|</code> between the members of a <code>union error</code> composition.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>union error</code> declares, composes and narrows error sets with one head-first spelling, so <code>set</code> is gone. A composition lists its members like any other list, separated by <code>,</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>union error</code>, with <code>,</code> between the members (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error SignupError = EmailError, DbError</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">error set SignupError = EmailError | DbError</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1029:</span> an error set is declared `union error SignupError = A, B`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ error set SignupError = EmailError | DbError
    │ ^^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write `union error`, with `,` between the members
    │ 1 │ union error SignupError = EmailError, DbError
    │   │ +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1029</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1031">BYN1031: <code>T!E</code> return type</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A function declared <code>T!E name(…)</code> or <code>T!(A, B) name(…)</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> What a function can fail with is written once, after the parameters, with <code>fails</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the errors to a <code>fails</code> clause (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 parse(string s) fails ParseError { … }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64!ParseError parse(string s) { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1031:</span> `T!E` is an input form; write `T f() fails E`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:4

  1 │ i64!ParseError parse(string s) { … }
    │    ^^^^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write `fails ParseError` after the parameters
    │ 1 │ i64 parse(string s) fails ParseError { … }
    │   │    +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1031</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1033">BYN1033: likelihood word after <code>unless</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>unless unlikely …</code> or <code>unless never …</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A likelihood word belongs to <code>if</code>, where it describes the branch that is written. After <code>unless</code> it would describe the negation.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Drop the word (the machine fix does this), or write <code>if unlikely</code> with the negated condition:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if unlikely !ok {
    recover()
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unless unlikely ok {
    recover()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1033:</span> `unless` takes no likelihood word; write `if unlikely !…` or drop `unlikely`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ unless unlikely ok {
    │        ^^^^^^^^ a likelihood word
    │
  help: remove the word, or write `if unlikely` with the negated condition
    │ 1 │ unless unlikely ok {
    │   │        ---------</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1033</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1040">BYN1040: <code>;</code> is an input form</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A statement ends at the newline, so <code>;</code> adds nothing. The line-end rules decide where a statement ends: a line ending in a binary operator, <code>,</code>, <code>=</code>, <code>=&gt;</code> or <code>{</code> continues on the next line, and so does a line starting with <code>.</code>, <code>?.</code>, <code>??</code>, <code>catch</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, <code>and</code> or <code>or</code>. A declaration header continues onto lines starting with its clauses: <code>fails</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>requires</code>. A line starting with <code>(</code>, <code>[</code> or <code>-</code> always starts a new statement, and outside brackets a blank line always ends one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the <code>;</code>. When another statement follows on the same line, the fix puts it on its own line instead. <code>bayan format</code> applies the fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a = 1
let b = 2; print(a + b)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1040:</span> `;` is an input form; a statement ends at the newline
  --&gt; main.byn:2:10

  2 │ let b = 2; print(a + b)
    │          ^ remove `;`
    │
  help: put the next statement on its own line
    │ 2 │ let b = 2
    │ 3 │ print(a + b)</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1040</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1041">BYN1041: <code>else</code> on a new line</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A newline after the <code>}</code> of an <code>if</code> ends the statement, so an <code>else</code> on the next line has no <code>if</code> to belong to. Keeping <code>else</code> on the line of <code>}</code> makes the two branches read as one statement.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move <code>else</code> up to the line of <code>}</code>: <code>} else {</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if a &gt; 0 {
    print("pos")
}
else {
    print("other")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1041:</span> `else` must follow `}` on the same line
  --&gt; main.byn:4:1

  4 │ else {
    │ ^^^^ on a new line
    │
  help: move `else` up to the line of `}`
    │ 3 │ } else {
    │   │  +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1041</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1042">BYN1042: a value or modifier on the next line</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> and <code>fail</code> take their operand on the same line, and a postfix <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code> sits on the line of its statement. A newline ends the statement, so a value or condition left on the next line would otherwise be a separate statement. One code covers both forms: "<code>{0}</code> takes its value on the same line" for an operand, and "a postfix <code>{0}</code> must be on the line of its statement" for a modifier. <code>fail</code> is in scope (revision 0002), but <code>fail</code> statements do not parse yet (step 5.3f), so only <code>return</code>, <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code> raise it today.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the value to the line of <code>return</code>, or the <code>if</code> clause up to the statement's line. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix that joins the lines.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 f(i64 x) {
    return
        x + 1
}

void g(bool done) {
    stop()
        if done
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1042:</span> `return` takes its value on the same line
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │         x + 1
    │         ^ on the next line
    │
  help: move the value to the line of `return`
    │ 2 │     return
    │ 3 │         x + 1
    │   │ -------</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1042</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1043">BYN1043: declaration and local binding in the wrong order</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Declarations put the type first (<code>Sku sku</code>, <code>Grams weight(Order order)</code>); local bindings put the name first (<code>let w: Grams = 0g</code>). One code covers both directions, with one message form each. A name-first declaration reads "<code>{0}: {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>{1} {0}</code>", and a name-first return reads "<code>{0}(…) -&gt; {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>{1} {0}(…)</code>". A type-first local reads "<code>{0} {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>let {1}: {0}</code>". A typed loop variable follows the local rule: <code>for T x in e</code> reads "<code>for {0} {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>for {1}: {0}</code>".</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}: {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>{1} {0}</code> (a name-first declaration); <code>{0}(…) -&gt; {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>{1} {0}(…)</code> (a name-first return); <code>{0} {1}</code> is an input form; write <code>let {1}: {0}</code> (a type-first local).</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> put the type before the name; declarations are type-first (declaration); put the return type before the name (return); write <code>{2} {1}: {0}</code>; local bindings put the name first (local, <code>{2}</code> is <code>let</code> or <code>var</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> For a declaration, write the type first: <code>i32 f()</code>, <code>f(i32 x)</code>, <code>string name</code>, <code>timeout(Millis after)</code>. A missing return type becomes <code>void</code>. For a loop variable, write <code>for x: T in e</code>. For a local binding, write <code>let w: Grams = 0g</code>; the local gains <code>let</code>, or <code>var</code> when it has no value or is assigned later in its block. A <code>let</code>, <code>var</code> or <code>const</code> already written is kept: <code>const i32 n = 1</code> becomes <code>const n: i32 = 1</code>. A function type used as a field or parameter type is parenthesized so the name follows it: <code>(Order -&gt; void uses net) onPaid</code>. The diagnostic carries the rewrite, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">f() -&gt; i32 { … }
f(x: i32) { … }
struct P { name: string }
union Event { timeout(after: Millis) }
void run() {
    Grams w = 0g
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1043:</span> `f(…) -&gt; i32` is an input form; write `i32 f(…)`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:5

  1 │ f() -&gt; i32 { … }
    │     ^^ return type after the parameters
    │
  help: put the return type before the name
    │ 1 │ i32 f() { … }
    │   │ +++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1043</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1044">BYN1044: declaration keywords in the wrong order</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A declaration names its kind first and its modifier after it: <code>struct unique</code>, <code>type distinct</code>, <code>union error</code>. The modifier-first spellings <code>unique struct</code>, <code>distinct type</code>, <code>error enum</code> and <code>error union</code> are input forms. The round-2 <code>error set</code> is BYN1029.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Put the kind first:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct unique File {
    i64 fd
}
union error StockError {
    outOfStock
}
type distinct Sku = string</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">unique struct File {
    i64 fd
}
error enum StockError {
    outOfStock
}
distinct type Sku = string</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1044:</span> `unique struct` is an input form; write `struct unique`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ unique struct File {
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: put the kind first: `struct unique`
    │ 1 │ struct unique File {
    │   │ +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1044</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1045">BYN1045: bracket attribute</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan writes an attribute as <code>@name</code> or <code>@name(args)</code> before the declaration, with a camelCase name, so <code>[</code> only ever opens a list or an index. The C# spelling <code>[Name(args)]</code> is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the attribute with <code>@</code> and a camelCase name:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases(1, 2, 3)
test "positive" (i32 n) {
    assert(n &gt; 0)
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">[Cases(1, 2, 3)]
test "positive" (i32 n) {
    assert(n &gt; 0)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1045:</span> `[Cases(1, 2, 3)]` is an input form; write `@cases(1, 2, 3)`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ [Cases(1, 2, 3)]
    │ ^
    │
  help: write the attribute with `@` and a camelCase name
    │ 1 │ @cases(1, 2, 3)
    │   │ +++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1045</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1046">BYN1046: <code>pattern</code> outside a <code>const</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A named pattern is a <code>const</code> value of pattern type, so <code>except</code>, <code>is</code> and <code>switch</code> can share it (spec §6.5). <code>pattern</code> is a contextual keyword, valid only after <code>=</code> in a <code>const</code> declaration; anywhere else it is an ordinary name.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the pattern as a <code>const</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">const inactive = pattern Line { status: .removed or .backordered }</code></pre>
<p>There is no machine fix: the <code>const</code> may need to move out of the function.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let inactive = pattern Line { status: .removed or .backordered }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1046:</span> `pattern` is allowed only after `=` in a `const`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:20

  2 │     let inactive = pattern Line { status: .removed or .backordered }
    │                    ^^^^^^^ `pattern` outside a `const`
    │
  help: declare the pattern as `const name = pattern T { … }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1046</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1047">BYN1047: block comment</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan has one comment form, <code>//</code> to the end of the line (and <code>///</code> for documentation), so a comment never hides code across lines and never nests. <code>/* */</code> is an input form. An unclosed <code>/*</code> is BYN0004.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write each line with <code>//</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// Prices are in cents.
// Discounts round down.
i64 total(i64 price) =&gt; price</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix (one <code>//</code> line per comment line, at the indent of the <code>/*</code> line), and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">/* Prices are in cents.
   Discounts round down. */
i64 total(i64 price) =&gt; price</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1047:</span> `/* */` is an input form; write `//` lines
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ /* Prices are in cents.
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ block comment
    │
  help: write each line with `//`
    │ 1 │ // Prices are in cents.
    │   │  ++++++++++++++++++++++
    │ 2 │ // Discounts round down.
    │   │ ++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1047</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1048">BYN1048: <code>char</code> is now <code>rune</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A Bayan character is a Unicode scalar value, not a UTF-16 unit as in C#, so it is named <code>rune</code>. <code>char</code> is an input form; a character literal such as <code>'a'</code> is a <code>rune</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename the type to <code>rune</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bool isVowel(rune c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bool isVowel(char c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1048:</span> `char` is now `rune`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:14

  1 │ bool isVowel(char c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'
    │              ^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: rename the type to `rune`
    │ 1 │ bool isVowel(rune c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'
    │   │              ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1048</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1050">BYN1050: <code>value</code> in <code>requires</code> is now <code>it</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> In a <code>requires</code> rule the value being checked is <code>it</code>, as in a lambda argument (spec §7.9). <code>requires value…</code> is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename <code>value</code> to <code>it</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Percent = i32 requires it in 0..=100</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Percent = i32 requires value in 0..=100</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1050:</span> `value` in `requires` is now `it`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:38

  1 │ type distinct Percent = i32 requires value in 0..=100
    │                                      ^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: rename `value` to `it`
    │ 1 │ type distinct Percent = i32 requires it in 0..=100
    │   │                                      ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1050</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1051">BYN1051: <code>except</code> is not allowed in a <code>case</code> pattern</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>except</code> removes cases from a value ([grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md)); it is an expression, not part of a pattern. A <code>case</code> label or a <code>switch</code> arm takes a pattern, and <code>not</code> already says "anything but this".</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>not</code> before the pattern:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">        not .circle =&gt; return s</code></pre>
<p><code>_ except p</code> carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it. Any other left side has no fix: the parser reads it as <code>left and not p</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Shape pick(Shape s) {
    switch s {
        case _ except .circle: return s
        default: return s
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1051:</span> `except` is not allowed in a `case` pattern
  --&gt; main.byn:3:16

  3 │         case _ except .circle: return s
    │                ^^^^^^ not allowed here
    │
  help: write the arm as `case not .circle`
    │ 3 │         case not .circle: return s
    │   │              ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1051</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1052">BYN1052: <code>except</code> takes a pattern, not a condition</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The right side of <code>except</code> is a pattern (<code>.timeout</code>, <code>"if"</code>, <code>0..=9</code>). A comparison, <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>is</code> or <code>in</code> after a constant makes it a condition, which <code>except</code> does not accept.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the condition to <code>where</code>, or write the pattern alone:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    if n is 0 where n &gt; 3 {</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void check(i32 n) {
    if n is 0 except n &gt; 3 {
        print("x")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1052:</span> `except` takes a pattern, not a condition
  --&gt; main.byn:2:22

  2 │     if n is 0 except n &gt; 3 {
    │                      ^^^^^ a condition
    │
  help: use `where` for a condition</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1052</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1053">BYN1053: cases separated by commas</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Declarations are separated by newlines: each case of an <code>enum</code>, <code>union</code> or <code>union error</code> sits on its own line, like a field. Commas separate entries of literals and argument lists only.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write each case on its own line, without commas:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f64 r)
    square(f64 side)
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape { circle(f64 r), square(f64 side) }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1053:</span> `Shape` declares its cases on one line; write one per line
  --&gt; main.byn:1:28

  1 │ union Shape { circle(f64 r), square(f64 side) }
    │                            ^ the first `,`
    │
  help: write each case on its own line
    │ 1 │ union Shape {
    │ 2 │     circle(f64 r)
    │   │ +++++++++++++++++
    │ 3 │     square(f64 side)
    │   │ ++++++++++++++++++++
    │ 4 │ }</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1053</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1054">BYN1054: <code>try</code> covers more than the call that fails</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>try</code> covers the whole expression to its right, down to <code>??</code> (spec §8): one scope rule, with no exceptions to memorize. When the part that can fail is only a piece of that expression, the reader would take <code>try</code> to apply to the whole, so the compiler asks for parentheses around the failing piece.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the part that fails in parentheses, with <code>try</code> inside:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 total(string s) fails ParseError {
    return (try parse(s)) + 1
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix. <code>bayan format</code> does not apply it: the code is a binder error, not an input form.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    bad
}

i64 parse(string s) fails ParseError {
    fail .bad if s.isEmpty
    return s.length
}

i64 one() =&gt; 1

i64 total(string s) fails ParseError {
    return try parse(s) + 1
}

i64 wrapped(string s) fails ParseError {
    return try one() + parse(s)
}

i64 fine(string s) fails ParseError {
    return (try parse(s)) + try parse(s)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1054:</span> `try` covers `parse(s) + 1`, which cannot fail
  --&gt; main.byn:13:16

  13 │     return try parse(s) + 1
     │                ^^^^^^^^^^^^ cannot fail
     │
  help: wrap the part that fails in parentheses: `(try parse(s))`
     │ 13 │     return (try parse(s)) + 1
     │    │            +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1054</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1055">BYN1055: <code>=&gt;</code> in a function type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function type is written with <code>-&gt;</code>: <code>i32 -&gt; bool</code>, <code>(i32, string) -&gt; i32</code>, <code>() -&gt; void</code>. <code>=&gt;</code> is the lambda arrow and the expression-body arrow (<code>bool isBig(i32 x) =&gt; x &gt; 2</code>); in a parameter type, a <code>type</code> alias target, a local annotation or a type argument it is the older spelling and an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>=&gt;</code> in a type is an input form; write <code>-&gt;</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> write the function type with <code>-&gt;</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>-&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type Pred = i32 -&gt; bool

bool check((i32 -&gt; bool) p) =&gt; p(3)</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type Pred = i32 =&gt; bool

bool check((i32 =&gt; bool) p) =&gt; p(3)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1055:</span> `=&gt;` in a type is an input form; write `-&gt;`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:17

  1 │ type Pred = i32 =&gt; bool
    │                 ^^ the input form
    │
  help: write the function type with `-&gt;`
    │ 1 │ type Pred = i32 -&gt; bool
    │   │                 +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1055</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1056">BYN1056: <code>return .x</code> where <code>.x</code> is an error</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>return</code> always returns a value and <code>fail</code> always leaves with an error (spec §8), so a reader tells the two exits apart by the keyword alone. In a function that fails, <code>return .x</code> where the return type has no case <code>.x</code> but the <code>fails</code> clause does reads as a value and is an error; it is the older spelling and an input form. When the return type has a case <code>.x</code>, <code>return .x</code> returns that value and no diagnostic is raised.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>return .{0}</code> returns an error; write <code>fail .{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> leave with the error: <code>fail .{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>fail</code> in place of <code>return</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 find(string key) fails LookupError {
    fail .missing if key.isEmpty
    return 1
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix. <code>bayan format</code> does not apply it: the code is a binder error, not an input form the parser sees.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error LookupError {
    missing
}

i64 find(string key) fails LookupError {
    return .missing if key.isEmpty
    return 1
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1056:</span> `return .missing` returns an error; write `fail .missing`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:5

  6 │     return .missing if key.isEmpty
    │     ^^^^^^ this leaves with an error
    │
  help: leave with the error: `fail .missing`
    │ 6 │     fail .missing if key.isEmpty
    │   │     ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1056</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1057">BYN1057: <code>(T)x</code> is an input form</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A conversion is a call on the target type: <code>u8(x)</code> is checked and traps in every build mode when <code>x</code> is out of range, and <code>u8.tryFrom(x)</code> returns <code>u8?</code> (spec §6.2). The C-style cast <code>(u8)x</code> is the older spelling and an input form; it also made <code>if (i32)big &gt; 3 {</code> ambiguous with a parenthesized condition.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>({0})x</code> is an input form; write <code>{0}(x)</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> call the conversion: <code>{0}(x)</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call the type with the value:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let big: i64 = 300
let small = u8(big)</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let big: i64 = 300
let small = (u8)big</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1057:</span> `(u8)x` is an input form; write `u8(x)`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:13

  2 │ let small = (u8)big
    │             ^^^^ a cast
    │
  help: call the conversion: `u8(x)`
    │ 2 │ let small = u8(big)
    │   │             +++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1057</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1058">BYN1058: <code>params</code> or <code>T...</code> for a variadic parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A variadic parameter is written <code>rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt; name</code>: the caller's extra arguments arrive as a read-only span. C#'s <code>params T[]</code> and C's <code>T...</code> are input forms.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 sum(rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;i32&gt; xs) { … }</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 sum(params i32[] xs) { … }
i32 total(i32... xs) { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1058:</span> `params i32[] xs` is an input form; write `rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;i32&gt; xs`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ i32 sum(params i32[] xs) { … }
    │         ^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: declare the variadic parameter with `rest`
    │ 1 │ i32 sum(rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;i32&gt; xs) { … }
    │   │         ++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1058</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1059">BYN1059: <code>state x</code> member lines</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A struct declares its typestates in one <code>states { … }</code> block after its fields, one state per line, each with an optional named payload. Per-line <code>state x</code> members are an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Gather the states into one block:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 total
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Every <code>state</code> line carries the same machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it: the lines leave their places and the block takes the place of the last one. There is no fix when the struct already has a <code>states</code> block (move the state into it by hand), or when a <code>state</code> line shares its line with other code or a comment.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 total
    state draft
    state paid(i64 payment)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1059:</span> `state draft` is an input form; declare the states in one `states { … }` block
  --&gt; main.byn:3:5

  3 │     state draft
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: move each state into the struct's `states` block
    │ 3 │     states {
    │   │          +++
    │ 4 │         draft
    │   │ +++++++++++++
    │ 5 │         paid(i64 payment)
    │   │ +++++++++++++++++++++++++
    │ 6 │     }
    │   │ +++++
    │ 7 │ }</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1059</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1060">BYN1060: <code>{ get }</code> on an interface member</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A field-shaped interface member, <code>Type name</code>, is already a read-only property requirement. C#'s <code>Type name { get }</code> is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>{ get }</code>: <code>i64 length</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface Sized {
    i64 length { get }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1060:</span> `i64 length { get }` is an input form; write `i64 length`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:16

  2 │     i64 length { get }
    │                ^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: remove `{ get }`: a field-shaped interface member is a read-only property
    │ 2 │     i64 length { get }
    │   │               --------</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1060</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1061">BYN1061: <code>this</code> receiver without a state</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A method reads its receiver by default, so a bare <code>this</code> first parameter says nothing. Write <code>this</code> only with a state (<code>this is .placed</code>), or as <code>ref this</code> or <code>move this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>this</code>: <code>i64 read() =&gt; n</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 n

    i64 read(this) =&gt; n
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1061:</span> `this` without a state is the default receiver
  --&gt; main.byn:4:14

  4 │     i64 read(this) =&gt; n
    │              ^^^^ the default receiver
    │
  help: remove `this`, or add a state: `this is .s`
    │ 4 │     i64 read(this) =&gt; n
    │   │              ----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1061</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1062">BYN1062: <code>trust(x, reason: …)</code> is now <code>x.trustUnchecked() because "…"</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Clearing the <code>Untrusted</code> mark bypasses a safety rule, and every bypass in Bayan states its reason in a <code>because</code> clause, so a reviewer finds them all with one search. <code>trust(x, reason: "…")</code> is the older spelling and an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call <code>trustUnchecked()</code> on the value and give the reason in <code>because</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let name: Untrusted&lt;string&gt; = Untrusted("x")
print(name.trustUnchecked() because "checked against the allow list")</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix when the reason is a string literal, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let name: Untrusted&lt;string&gt; = Untrusted("x")
print(trust(name, reason: "checked against the allow list"))</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1062:</span> `trust(x, reason: …)` is now `x.trustUnchecked() because "…"`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:7

  2 │ print(trust(name, reason: "checked against the allow list"))
    │       ^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: call `trustUnchecked()` on the value and give the reason in `because`
    │ 2 │ print(name.trustUnchecked() because "checked against the allow list")
    │   │       ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1062</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1063">BYN1063: <code>requires</code> on a function</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function's precondition is written <code>expects condition</code>; <code>requires</code> belongs to field and type rules. <code>requires</code> on a function is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>expects</code>: <code>i64 half(i64 n) expects n &gt;= 0 { … }</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 half(i64 n) requires n &gt;= 0 { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1063:</span> `requires` on a function is an input form; write `expects`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:17

  1 │ i64 half(i64 n) requires n &gt;= 0 { … }
    │                 ^^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: use `expects` for a precondition; `requires` is checked at construction
    │ 1 │ i64 half(i64 n) expects n &gt;= 0 { … }
    │   │                 ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1063</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1064">BYN1064: payload without a name</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each payload of a union variant or a state is a named field, read as <code>shape.radius</code> once the variant is matched. A type alone gives the field no name.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the payload: <code>circle(f64 radius)</code>. There is no automatic fix, because the name is yours to choose.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f64)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1064:</span> payload `f64` of `.circle` needs a name
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │     circle(f64)
    │            ^^^ no name
    │
  help: name the payload: `.circle(f64 name)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1064</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1065">BYN1065: function clauses out of order</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The clauses after a function's parameters have one order, so every signature reads the same way: <code>where</code>, <code>expects</code>, <code>uses</code>, <code>becomes</code>, <code>fails</code>. The code is raised once, at the first clause out of order.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Put the clauses in order: <code>i64 parse(string s) uses io fails ParseError { … }</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix that sorts the clauses, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 parse(string s) fails ParseError uses io { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1065:</span> `uses` must come before `fails`; the order is `where`, `expects`, `uses`, `becomes`,
`fails`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:38

  1 │ i64 parse(string s) fails ParseError uses io { … }
    │                                      ^^^^ out of order
    │
  help: move `uses` before `fails`
    │ 1 │ i64 parse(string s) uses io fails ParseError { … }
    │   │                     ++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1065</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1066">BYN1066: prefix pointer type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A pointer type is written after its pointee, as in C#: <code>T*</code>, and <code>readonly T*</code> for a pointee that cannot be written through it. Rust's <code>*const T</code> and <code>*mut T</code>, and a bare <code>*T</code>, are input forms.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the pointer after the type: <code>readonly u8* from</code>, <code>u8* to</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it; a nested <code>**T</code> is fixed from the inside out. Pointer types are allowed only in <code>extern</code> declarations (BYN1067).</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">extern void copy(*const u8 from, *mut u8 to)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1066:</span> `*const u8` is an input form; write `readonly u8*`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ extern void copy(*const u8 from, *mut u8 to)
    │                  ^ the input form
    │
  help: write the pointer after the type: `readonly u8*`
    │ 1 │ extern void copy(readonly u8* from, *mut u8 to)
    │   │                  ++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1066</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1067">BYN1067: pointer type outside <code>extern</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Pointers exist only to call C. A pointer type may appear only in an <code>extern</code> declaration; everywhere else, use a reference (<code>ref</code>), a span or an index.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass a <code>ref</code> or a span, or wrap the C call in an <code>extern</code> declaration. There is no automatic fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void fill(u8* buffer) { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1067:</span> pointer types are allowed only in `extern` declarations
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ void fill(u8* buffer) { … }
    │           ^^^ a pointer type
    │
  help: wrap the C call in an `extern` declaration</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1067</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1068">BYN1068: compact format spec in an interpolation hole</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An interpolation hole takes its format specs as named arguments after the value: <code>{n, as: .hex}</code>, <code>{x, digits: 2}</code>, <code>{id, width: 6, fill: '0'}</code>. The C#-style compact forms <code>{n:hex}</code>, <code>{n:binary}</code>, <code>{n:octal}</code>, <code>{x:.2}</code>, <code>{d:iso}</code> and <code>{x:debug}</code> are input forms.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the spec as named arguments: <code>print($"{n, as: .hex} {x, digits: 2}")</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it. Other compact specs (<code>{x:N2}</code>) are not recognized and are a syntax error.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">print($"{n:hex} {x:.2}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1068:</span> `:hex` is an input form; write `, as: .hex`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ print($"{n:hex} {x:.2}")
    │           ^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write the spec as named arguments: `, as: .hex`
    │ 1 │ print($"{n, as: .hex} {x:.2}")
    │   │           +++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1068</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn1069">BYN1069: attribute not allowed before a statement</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A statement may carry attributes from a short allow-list, written on the line before it and applying to that statement only. The list holds one attribute: <code>@unobservable because "…"</code> or <code>@unobservable(operator) because "…"</code>, which tells mutation testing that a change there cannot be observed. Every other attribute belongs on a declaration.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the attribute, or move it to the declaration it was meant for. There is no automatic fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 f(i32 x) {
    @inline
    return x
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1069:</span> attribute `@inline` is not allowed before a statement
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     @inline
    │     ^^^^^^^ before a statement
    │
  help: remove the attribute; only `@unobservable` may come before a statement</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1069</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g20">Names, modules and declarations (BYN20xx)</h2>
<p>A name that does not exist, a declaration that clashes with another, or a module that is set up wrongly.</p>
<h3 id="byn2001">BYN2001: undefined name</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A name must be declared before it can be used: as a local, parameter, function, type, constant or imported module. Bayan has no implicit globals.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Check the spelling (the help line suggests a close match), declare the name, or <code>import</code> the module it lives in.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total = cout + 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2001:</span> `cout` is not defined
  --&gt; main.byn:1:13

  1 │ let total = cout + 1
    │             ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2002">BYN2002: unknown type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Types are primitives, built-ins (<code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Arena</code>, <code>StrBuf</code>), and structs, error enums and aliases declared in the module or an imported one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Check the spelling, or import the module that declares the type and qualify it: <code>money.Money</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var price: Mony</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2002:</span> unknown type `Mony`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:12

  1 │ var price: Mony
    │            ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2003">BYN2003: unknown member</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Members are fixed by the type's declaration; Bayan has no dynamic members or extension methods in v0.1.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a member the type has. For strings, spans and lists the size is always <code>length</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let name = "bayan"
let n = name.size</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2003:</span> `size` is not a member of `string`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:14

  2 │ let n = name.size
    │              ^^^^
    │
  help: use `length` for the size of anything</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2004">BYN2004: unknown module</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A module is a directory of <code>.byn</code> files under the package root (the entry file's directory); <code>import a.b;</code> reads <code>a/b/</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Check the directory name, or create the directory next to the entry file.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">import mony</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2004:</span> no module `mony`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ import mony
    │        ^^^^
    │
  help: put the module's `.byn` files in `mony/` next to the entry file; a module is a directory</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2005">BYN2005: not visible</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Declarations are module-private by default; <code>private</code> members are visible only inside their struct. Other modules see only <code>public</code> declarations.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Mark the declaration <code>public</code> if other modules should use it, or call a public API instead.</p>

<h3 id="byn2006">BYN2006: duplicate declaration</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every name in a module, struct, parameter list or import list must be unique. Bayan has no overloading.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename one of them; for variants of a function, give each a descriptive name (<code>areaOfSquare</code>).</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IEnumerator {
    bool moveNext(ref this)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2006:</span> `IEnumerator` is declared twice
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ interface IEnumerator {
    │           ^^^^^^^^^^^ already declared as a built-in interface</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2007">BYN2007: local shadows another local</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A local hiding another local of the same name makes code ambiguous to read, so Bayan forbids it in nested scopes too. The one exception is a <code>let</code> in the same scope that gives the name a new type (<code>let count = count.length;</code>); with the same type it is warning BYN2028.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pick a different name.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total = 0
if total == 0 {
    let total = 1
    print($"{total}")
}
let count = "3"
let count = count.length
let size = 1
let size = 2
var step = 1
step += 1
var step = 2
step += 1
print($"{count} {size} {step}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2007:</span> `total` is already a local here
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │     let total = 1
    │         ^^^^^ already declared on line 1
    │
  help: pick a different name; Bayan does not let a local hide another one</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2008">BYN2008: type casing</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Casing carries meaning (spec §5.1): types are PascalCase, values and functions camelCase. Acronyms are capitalized like words (<code>HttpClient</code>, not <code>HTTPClient</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the suggested name from the fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct point {
    i64 x
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2008:</span> type name `point` must be PascalCase
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ struct point {
    │        ^^^^^
    │
  help: rename it `Point`
    │ 1 │ struct Point {
    │   │        +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2008</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2009">BYN2009: value casing</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Casing carries meaning (spec §5.1): locals, parameters, fields, functions and error variants are camelCase.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the suggested name from the fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let Total = 0</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2009:</span> `Total` must be camelCase
  --&gt; main.byn:1:5

  1 │ let Total = 0
    │     ^^^^^
    │
  help: rename it `total`; only types are PascalCase
    │ 1 │ let total = 0
    │   │     +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2009</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2010">BYN2010: import cycle</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Modules form a tree of dependencies so each can be compiled and understood on its own terms.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the declarations both modules need into a third module that both import.</p>

<h3 id="byn2011">BYN2011: circular type alias</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An alias is another name for an existing type; one defined in terms of itself names no type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Point one of the aliases at a concrete type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type Alpha = Beta
type Beta = Alpha
var x: Alpha</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2011:</span> type alias `Alpha` refers to itself
  --&gt; main.byn:1:6

  1 │ type Alpha = Beta
    │      ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2011</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2012">BYN2012: circular constant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Constants are evaluated at compile time; a cycle has no value.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Break the cycle by giving one constant a literal value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">const first: i64 = second + 1
const second: i64 = first
print($"{first}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2012:</span> constant `first` depends on itself
  --&gt; main.byn:1:7

  1 │ const first: i64 = second + 1
    │       ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2012</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2013">BYN2013: two entry points</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Top-level statements in the entry file are the program's <code>main</code>; a separate <code>void main()</code> would be a second one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Keep one: move the statements into <code>main</code>, or delete <code>main</code> and keep the top-level statements.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">print("a")
void main() {
    print("b")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2013:</span> the program has two entry points
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ print("a")
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^ `main` is also declared on line 2</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2013</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2014">BYN2014: no entry point</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A program starts at the top-level statements of the entry file, or at <code>void main()</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add top-level statements or a <code>main</code> function. To check a library, run <code>bayan test</code> on it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2014:</span> the program has no entry point
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2
    │ ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2014</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2015">BYN2015: statement outside the entry module</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Top-level statements form the program's <code>main</code>, so only the entry module may have them. Other modules contain declarations only.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the statements into a function and call it from the entry module.</p>

<h3 id="byn2017">BYN2017: instance member without an instance</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Fields, properties and non-static methods belong to a value. Static methods have no <code>this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Make the method an instance method, or pass the value as a parameter.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 count
    static i64 twice() =&gt; count * 2
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2017:</span> `count` needs an instance
  --&gt; main.byn:3:27

  3 │     static i64 twice() =&gt; count * 2
    │                           ^^^^^
    │
  help: call it on an instance; a `static` method has no `this`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2017</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2018">BYN2018: <code>this</code> outside a method</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>this</code> is the value a method was called on. Functions and static methods are not called on a value.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Take the value as a parameter.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 f() =&gt; this.x</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2018:</span> `this` is only available inside instance methods
  --&gt; main.byn:1:12

  1 │ i64 f() =&gt; this.x
    │            ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2018</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2019">BYN2019: not a value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Types and modules name things at compile time; they cannot be stored or passed. Neither can a function or method named without its call parentheses: there are no function values yet.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Construct a value (<code>Money { cents: 0 }</code>) or call a function from the module (<code>money.parse(s)</code>). For a method, add the parentheses: <code>items.copy()</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Money {
    i64 cents
}

let m = Money</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2019:</span> `Money` is a type, not a value
  --&gt; main.byn:5:9

  5 │ let m = Money
    │         ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2019</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2020">BYN2020: unknown label</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>break label;</code> and <code>continue label;</code> leave the enclosing loop with that label.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Label the loop (<code>outer: for (…)</code>) or fix the label's spelling.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for i in 0..3 {
    break outer
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2020:</span> no enclosing loop is labeled `outer`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:11

  2 │     break outer
    │           ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2020</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2021">BYN2021: unknown parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A named argument must match a parameter name of the function being called.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the parameter's name (the fix suggests the closest one).</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 area(i64 width, by i64 height) =&gt; width * height
print($"{area(widht: 3, by: 4)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2021:</span> `area` has no parameter `widht`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:15

  2 │ print($"{area(widht: 3, by: 4)}")
    │               ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2021</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2022">BYN2022: not supported in v0.1</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The syntax is part of Bayan, but this version of the compiler does not implement it yet. Reporting it keeps programs from silently meaning something else.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rewrite without the feature; the help line suggests an alternative when one exists.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = a?.b</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2022:</span> `?.` is not supported in v0.1
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let x = a?.b
    │         ^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2022</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2023">BYN2023: wrong number of type arguments</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Generic types have a fixed arity: <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> take one type argument, and a <code>struct Pair&lt;A, B&gt;</code> takes two. <code>ValidationError</code> takes one too: a bare <code>ValidationError</code> names no type, so there is no <code>T.Field</code> for its violations (revision 0011).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass exactly the type arguments the type takes: <code>fails ValidationError&lt;Order&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var xs: List&lt;i64, i64&gt;</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2023:</span> `List` takes 1 type argument(s), not 2
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ var xs: List&lt;i64, i64&gt;
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2023</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2024">BYN2024: nested optional</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>T??</code> would need two kinds of null, which hides a missing case. Use an error or a small struct to tell them apart.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Drop the extra <code>?</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type MaybeCount = i64?
let x: MaybeCount? = null</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2024:</span> `i64??` is already optional
  --&gt; main.byn:2:8

  2 │ let x: MaybeCount? = null
    │        ^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2024</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2025">BYN2025: void used as a value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>void</code> means a function returns nothing. It cannot be the type of a local, a field or a parameter.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the declaration, or give it a real type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var nothing: void</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2025:</span> `void` is not a value type
  --&gt; main.byn:1:14

  1 │ var nothing: void
    │              ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2025</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2027">BYN2027: <code>==</code> with <code>null</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>x == null</code>, <code>x != null</code> or <code>null == x</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Absence is tested with a pattern: <code>x is null</code>, <code>x is not null</code>. The pattern also narrows <code>x</code> in the branch where it is present.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the pattern (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">if name is not null {
    greet(name)
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let n: i32? = null
    let c = n == null
    let d = n != null
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2027:</span> compare with `is null` / `is not null`, not `==`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:13

  3 │     let c = n == null
    │             ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write `n is null`
    │ 3 │     let c = n is null
    │   │               ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2027</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2028">BYN2028: <code>let</code> shadows a local of the same type</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>let</code> redeclares a local in the same scope, and the new value has the same type as the old one (warning).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Redeclaring a name with <code>let</code> is for changing its type as a value moves through steps: <code>let count = count.length;</code> turns a <code>string</code> into an <code>i32</code>. When the type stays the same, the second <code>let</code> hides the first value without saying how it differs, and a reader cannot tell which one a later line means (HANDOFF §4).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Reuse the first one, or name the second one for what it is:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let size = readSize()
let doubled = size * 2</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total = 0
if total == 0 {
    let total = 1
    print($"{total}")
}
let count = "3"
let count = count.length
let size = 1
let size = 2
var step = 1
step += 1
var step = 2
step += 1
print($"{count} {size} {step}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN2028:</span> `size` shadows an earlier `size` of the same type `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:5

  9 │ let size = 2
    │     ^^^^ `size` is declared on line 8
    │
  help: use the first one, or rename this one to say how it differs</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2028</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2029">BYN2029: nested <code>it</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>it</code> names the parameter of the one-parameter lambda being written. Inside a lambda that already uses <code>it</code>, a second <code>it</code> could mean either parameter, so the shorthand stops at one level. A <code>requires</code> rule counts as that level, since its <code>it</code> is the field.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the inner lambda's parameter:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let found = first(xss, by: any(it, by: x =&gt; x &gt; 1))</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bool any(List&lt;i64&gt; xs, by (i64 -&gt; bool) p) =&gt; false
bool first(List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt; xss, by (List&lt;i64&gt; -&gt; bool) p) =&gt; p(xss[0])

void main() {
    let xss: List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt; = [[1, 2], [3]]
    let found = first(xss, by: any(it, by: it &gt; 1))
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2029:</span> `it` inside a lambda that already uses `it`; name one lambda
  --&gt; main.byn:6:44

  6 │     let found = first(xss, by: any(it, by: it &gt; 1))
    │                                            ^^
    │
  help: name the inner lambda's parameter: `x =&gt; …`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2029</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2031">BYN2031: <code>toString()</code> call</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A call to <code>toString()</code> or <code>ToString()</code>. Bayan has no such method.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Display has one protocol (HANDOFF §13): a type writes itself with <code>formatInto(ref StrBuf)</code>, and the allocating <code>format()</code> is derived from it. Interpolation calls <code>formatInto</code> directly, so <code>$"{x}"</code> never builds a temporary string. A <code>toString()</code> beside them would be a second, allocating way to do the same thing.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call <code>format()</code> (the machine fix renames the call), or interpolate the value where it is written:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let text = total.format()
print($"{total}")</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point { i64 x }

void main() {
    let n = 42
    let p = Point { x: 1 }
    print(n.toString())
    print(p.ToString())
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2031:</span> Bayan has no `toString()`; display is `format()` (derived from
`formatInto(ref StrBuf)`)
  --&gt; main.byn:6:13

  6 │     print(n.toString())
    │             ^^^^^^^^ not a Bayan method
    │
  help: call `format()`, or interpolate the value: `$"{x}"`
    │ 6 │     print(n.format())
    │   │             ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2031</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g21">Bindings and initialization (BYN21xx)</h2>
<p>Variables used before they have a value, assigned when they cannot be, or declared without enough information.</p>
<h3 id="byn2101">BYN2101: missing initializer</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>let</code> and <code>const</code> never change, so they must be given their value where they are declared.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Give it a value, or declare it with <code>var</code> (or a type, <code>i64 total;</code>) and assign it on every path before use.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2101:</span> `let total` needs a value
  --&gt; main.byn:1:5

  1 │ let total
    │     ^^^^^
    │
  help: give it a value, or declare it with `var` and assign it on every path before use</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2101</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2102">BYN2102: use before assignment</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A local declared without a value has none until it is assigned. Bayan checks every path, so reading it early is a compile error rather than garbage at run time.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign it on every path before the read, or give the declaration a value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var hi: i32
let n = 3
print($"{n in 0..=hi}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2102:</span> `hi` is used before it is assigned
  --&gt; main.byn:3:19

  3 │ print($"{n in 0..=hi}")
    │                   ^^ declared without a value on line 1
    │
  help: assign it on every path before this, or give the declaration a value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2102</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2103">BYN2103: <code>out</code> parameter not assigned</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The caller reads an <code>out</code> argument after the call, so the function must assign it before returning normally.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign the parameter before each <code>return</code> and before the end of the body.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">bool tryParse(string text, to out i64 value) {
    if text.isEmpty {
        return false
    }
    value = 1
    return true
}

bool tryFirst(string text, to out i64 value) {
    return false
}

var v: i64
print($"{tryParse("1", to: out v)} {tryFirst("", to: out v)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2103:</span> `out` parameter `value` is not assigned on every path
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │         return false
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: assign `value` before every `return`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2103</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2104">BYN2104: assignment to an immutable value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>let</code> locals, parameters, loop variables and pattern variables never change after they are bound; this makes data flow easy to follow.</p>
<p>Calling a <code>ref this</code> member on a <code>let</code> value, or on an element of a <code>let</code> collection (<code>list[0].record()</code>), is the same error: the call changes the value.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare it with <code>var</code> if it should change. For parameters, copy into a local <code>var</code>, or take the parameter by <code>ref</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total = 0
total += 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `total` is immutable
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ total += 1
    │ ^^^^^ declared with `let` on line 1
    │
  help: declare it with `var` if it should change
    │ 1 │ var total = 0
    │   │ +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2104</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2105">BYN2105: readonly field</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>readonly</code> field keeps the value it is given in a constructor or struct literal.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Create a new value (<code>id with { value: 2 }</code>), or drop <code>readonly</code> from the field.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Id {
    readonly i64 value
}

var id = Id { value: 1 }
id.value = 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2105:</span> field `value` is readonly
  --&gt; main.byn:6:1

  6 │ id.value = 2
    │ ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: set `readonly` fields only in a constructor or a struct literal</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2105</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2106">BYN2106: modifying a temporary or read-only value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only variables, fields of variables, and elements of lists, arrays and spans can be written. A call result is a temporary copy, and the bytes of a string are read-only.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Store the value in a <code>var</code> first, or copy the bytes into a buffer you own.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let s = "abc"
s.bytes[0] = 65</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2106:</span> cannot modify this value
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ s.bytes[0] = 65
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^ a `ReadOnlySpan` cannot be written through</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2106</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2107">BYN2107: getter modifies its value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Reading a property must not change the value; otherwise <code>x.count</code> in a <code>let</code> would be an error at a distance.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Make it a method: <code>i64 next() { … }</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 n
    List&lt;i64&gt; seen

    i64 latest =&gt; record()

    i64 record(ref this) {
        n += 1
        return n
    }
}

let c = Counter { n: 0, seen: List&lt;i64&gt;() }
print($"{c.latest}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2107:</span> property `latest` modifies the value
  --&gt; main.byn:5:19

  5 │     i64 latest =&gt; record()
    │                   ^^^^^^
    │
  help: make it a method if it needs to change the value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2107</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2108">BYN2108: required field with a default</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>required</code> means every struct literal must set the field; a default would never be used.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>required</code> or remove the default.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Limit {
    required i64 max = 10
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2108:</span> required field `max` has a default value
  --&gt; main.byn:2:18

  2 │     required i64 max = 10
    │                  ^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2108</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2109">BYN2109: missing fields</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A struct literal must set every field that is <code>required</code> or has no default, so no value is ever half-built.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Set the missing fields, or give them defaults in the struct.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
    i64 y
}

let p = Point { x: 1 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2109:</span> `Point` needs a value for `y`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:9

  6 │ let p = Point { x: 1 }
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: set it: `Point { y: … }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2109</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2110">BYN2110: field set twice</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each field gets one value in a struct literal; a second one would silently win.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove one of them.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
}

let p = Point { x: 1, x: 2 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2110:</span> field `x` is set twice
  --&gt; main.byn:5:23

  5 │ let p = Point { x: 1, x: 2 }
    │                       ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2110</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2111">BYN2111: struct literal bypasses a constructor</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A constructor establishes invariants; a struct literal from outside the struct would skip them.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call the constructor: <code>Ratio(1, 2)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Ratio {
    i64 num
    i64 den

    public Ratio(i64 num, to i64 den) {
        this.num = num
        this.den = den
    }
}

let r = Ratio { num: 1, den: 0 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2111:</span> `Ratio` has a constructor; call it instead of using a struct literal
  --&gt; main.byn:11:9

  11 │ let r = Ratio { num: 1, den: 0 }
     │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: call the constructor: `Ratio(num: …, den: …)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2111</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2112">BYN2112: no constructor</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Structs without a constructor are built with a struct literal, which names each field.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a struct literal: <code>Point { x: 1, y: 2 }</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
    i64 y
}

let p = Point(1, 2)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2112:</span> `Point` has no constructor
  --&gt; main.byn:6:9

  6 │ let p = Point(1, 2)
    │         ^^^^^
    │
  help: set the fields with a struct literal: `Point { x: …, y: … }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2112</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2114">BYN2114: not a struct</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Struct literals and <code>with</code> expressions build struct values; other types have no fields to set.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a struct type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
let m = n with { value: 1 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2114:</span> `i32` is not a struct
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ let m = n with { value: 1 }
    │         ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2114</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2115">BYN2115: method modifies <code>this</code> without <code>ref this</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A method assigns to a field of <code>this</code>, or calls a <code>ref this</code> member on <code>this</code>, but does not declare <code>ref this</code> as its first parameter.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Members are read-only by default (spec §9). <code>ref this</code> in the signature tells the reader, and the compiler, that a call changes its receiver, so calling it on a <code>let</code> is an error at the call site instead of a surprise inside the body. The compiler checks the declaration; it does not infer it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare <code>ref this</code> as the first parameter (the machine fix inserts it):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 n

    void bump(ref this) {
        n += 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>If the method should not change the value, return a new value instead of writing to <code>this</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    i64 n

    void bump() {
        n += 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2115:</span> `bump` modifies `this` but is not declared `ref this`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:9

  5 │         n += 1
    │         ^ `this` is modified here
    │
  help: declare it `ref this` as the first parameter
    │ 4 │     void bump(ref this) {
    │   │               ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2115</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2117">BYN2117: <code>ref this</code> is not the first parameter</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>ref this</code> appears in a parameter list after another parameter, or twice.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>ref this</code> is not an ordinary parameter: it marks the member as mutating (spec §9) and stands for the receiver, which is written before the <code>.</code> at the call site. Keeping it first makes every mutating signature read the same way.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move <code>ref this</code> to the start of the parameter list:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void add(ref this, i64 by) { n += by; }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void add(i64 by, ref this) { n += by; }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2117:</span> `ref this` must be the first parameter
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ void add(i64 by, ref this) { n += by; }
    │                  ^^^^^^^^ after other parameters
    │
  help: move `ref this` to the start of the parameter list</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2117</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g22">Types and expressions (BYN22xx)</h2>
<p>A value of the wrong type, an operator that does not apply, a conversion that is not allowed.</p>
<h3 id="byn2201">BYN2201: string indexer</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>string</code> is UTF-8. <code>s[i]</code> would have to pick between a byte, a code point and a grapheme, and either be wrong for some text or hide an O(n) scan. Bayan makes you choose.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Read a byte, slice, or take characters: <code>s.bytes[i]</code> for the byte at offset <code>i</code> (the machine fix), <code>s[a..b]</code> for the text between byte offsets, <code>s.runes.take(n)</code> for the first <code>n</code> runes, or <code>s.graphemes</code> for user-perceived characters.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let name = "bayan"
let first = name[0]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2201:</span> a `string` cannot be indexed
  --&gt; main.byn:2:17

  2 │ let first = name[0]
    │                 ^^^ strings have no indexer
    │
  help: read a byte, slice, or take characters; `string` is UTF-8, so position 0 is not a character:
          `name.bytes[0]`       the byte at offset 0 (applied below)
          `name[a..b]`          the text between byte offsets a and b
          `name.runes.take(n)`  the first n runes
          `name.graphemes`      user-perceived characters
    │ 2 │ let first = name.bytes[0]
    │   │                 ++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2201</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2202">BYN2202: type mismatch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan converts implicitly only when nothing can be lost: widening integers, <code>T</code> to <code>T?</code>. Everything else needs an explicit cast.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Convert explicitly with a cast (the fix inserts one), or change the declared type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let big: i64 = 5
let small: i32 = big
let flag: bool = 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2202:</span> expected `i32`, found `i64`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:18

  2 │ let small: i32 = big
    │                  ^^^
    │
  help: convert it if the value always fits; `i64` does not convert to `i32` without loss
    │ 2 │ let small: i32 = i32(big)
    │   │                  ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2202</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2203">BYN2203: literal out of range</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An integer literal takes its type from the context; a value outside that type's range would silently wrap.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a wider type or a smaller value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let b: u8 = 300
let c: i8 = -129</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2203:</span> 300 does not fit in `u8`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:13

  1 │ let b: u8 = 300
    │             ^^^
    │
  help: use a value from 0 to 255, the range of `u8`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2203</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2204">BYN2204: constant overflow</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The compiler evaluates constant expressions; one that overflows would panic every time it runs.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a wider type, or the wrapping operators (<code>*%</code>) if wrapping is intended.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">const big: i32 = 2_000_000_000 * 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2204:</span> constant arithmetic overflows `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ const big: i32 = 2_000_000_000 * 2
    │                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2204</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2205">BYN2205: division by zero</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Integer division by zero traps at run time; a constant divisor of zero always does.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Divide by a non-zero value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = 10 / 0</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2205:</span> division by zero
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let x = 10 / 0
    │         ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2205</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2206">BYN2206: invalid cast</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Casts convert between numeric types and between <code>rune</code> and integers. Other conversions are functions (<code>parse</code>, <code>formatInto</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call a conversion function instead.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = i64("42")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2206:</span> cannot convert `string` to `i64`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let n = i64("42")
    │         ^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2206</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2207">BYN2207: bad unary operand</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each operator works on specific types: <code>-</code> on signed numbers, <code>!</code> on <code>bool</code>, <code>~</code> on integers, <code>++</code>/<code>--</code> on integer variables.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use an operand of a type the operator supports, or cast first.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n: u32 = 5
let x = -n
let y = !n</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2207:</span> `-` cannot be applied to `u32`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ let x = -n
    │         ^^
    │
  help: convert to a signed type first; unsigned values cannot be negative</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2207</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2208">BYN2208: bad binary operands</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Arithmetic needs two numbers of compatible types; mixing signed and unsigned, or numbers and <code>bool</code>, needs an explicit cast so the intended type is visible.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Cast one operand so both have the same type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let a: i64 = 1
let b: u64 = 2
let c = a + b
let d = true + 1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2208:</span> `+` cannot be applied to `i64` and `u64`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │ let c = a + b
    │         ^^^^^
    │
  help: convert one side explicitly, e.g. `(u64)…`; mixed signedness never converts implicitly</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2208</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2209">BYN2209: string concatenation</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>+</code> (or <code>+=</code>) with <code>string</code> operands. Strings cannot be joined with <code>+</code>, not even constants.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>+</code> on strings would allocate behind your back, and in a loop it is quadratic. Bayan keeps allocation visible: interpolation writes straight into its output or a buffer. One rule for every operand, constant or not, keeps the language small (HANDOFF §12). A string pattern such as <code>"/api/" + var rest</code> is not concatenation and stays legal.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Interpolate the parts (the machine fix rewrites <code>a + b</code> to <code>$"{a}{b}"</code>, inlining literal text). Two literals become one literal, which stays a constant:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let greeting = $"Hello, {name}"
let path = "/usr/lib"</code></pre>
<p>To build text in a loop, append to a <code>StrBuf</code>: <code>buf.append($"{a}{b}")</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let greeting = "Hello, " + name
let path = "/usr" + "/lib"</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │ let path = "/usr" + "/lib"
    │            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write one literal: `"/usr/lib"`
    │ 2 │ let path = "/usr" + "/lib"
    │   │                 -----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2209</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2210">BYN2210: <code>??</code> on a non-optional value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>a ?? b</code> uses <code>b</code> when <code>a</code> is null; a value that cannot be null never uses <code>b</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>?? …</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5 ?? 0</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2210:</span> `??` needs an optional value on the left, found `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let n = 5 ?? 0
    │         ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2210</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2211">BYN2211: member of an optional value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An optional value must be checked before its members are used, so a null is handled where it can occur.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Check it first: <code>if (p is { } point) { … point.x … }</code>, or supply a default: <code>(p ?? origin).x</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
}

let p: Point? = null
let x = p.x</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2211:</span> `x` is not a member of `Point?`; the value may be null
  --&gt; main.byn:6:11

  6 │ let x = p.x
    │           ^
    │
  help: check it first: `if (p is { } value) { … value.x … }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2211</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2212">BYN2212: not indexable</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Indexing works on arrays, spans and lists.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Index a collection instead.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
let d = n[0]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2212:</span> `i32` cannot be indexed
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ let d = n[0]
    │         ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2212</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2213">BYN2213: constant index out of range</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A fixed array's length is known at compile time, so a constant index past the end is always a bug.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use an index from 0 to length − 1.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let xs: i64[3] = [1, 2, 3]
let x = xs[3]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2213:</span> index 3 is out of range for `i64[3]`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │ let x = xs[3]
    │            ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2213</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2214">BYN2214: <code>^</code> outside an index</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>^n</code> means "n from the end" of the collection being indexed; outside an index there is no end to count from.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use it as <code>xs[^1]</code>, or compute <code>xs.length - 1</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let i = ^1</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2214:</span> `^` is only allowed inside an index or slice
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let i = ^1
    │         ^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2214</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2215">BYN2215: open range</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Open ranges (<code>a..</code>, <code>..b</code>) are only meaningful as slices, where the collection supplies the missing end.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Give both ends: <code>0..n</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for i in 0.. { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2215:</span> a range needs both ends here
  --&gt; main.byn:1:10

  1 │ for i in 0.. { }
    │          ^^^
    │
  help: use open ranges (`a..`, `..b`) only inside `[ ]`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2215</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2216">BYN2216: branch type mismatch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Both branches of <code>?:</code> (and every arm of a <code>switch</code> expression) produce the expression's value, so they need a common type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Make the branches agree, converting one of them explicitly if needed.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = ok ? 1 : "no"</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2216:</span> branches have different types: `i32` and `string`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let x = ok ? 1 : "no"
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2216</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2217">BYN2217: cannot infer type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A local's type comes from its value; <code>null</code>, <code>[]</code>, and a declaration with no value say nothing about the type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the type: <code>i64? x = null;</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = null</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2217:</span> cannot infer the type of `null`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let x = null
    │         ^^^^
    │
  help: write the type: `i64? x = null`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2217</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2218">BYN2218: no target type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>.variant</code>, <code>{ … }</code> and <code>[ … ]</code> take their type from where they are used: a declared local, a parameter or a return type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the type out: <code>MoneyError.invalid</code>, <code>Point { … }</code>, or declare the local's type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let e = .invalid</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2218:</span> `invalid` needs a known type here
  --&gt; main.byn:1:9

  1 │ let e = .invalid
    │         ^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2218</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2219">BYN2219: not a constant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Constants, array lengths, parameter defaults and patterns are evaluated by the compiler. A spread <code>..xs</code> into a fixed array or <code>Span</code> literal must also have a compile-time length, because the literal's storage is sized on the stack.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use literals and other constants, or make it a <code>let</code>. For a spread, spread a fixed array <code>T[N]</code>, or make the target a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 compute() =&gt; 2

const n: i64 = compute()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2219:</span> a `const` must be a compile-time constant
  --&gt; main.byn:3:16

  3 │ const n: i64 = compute()
    │                ^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2219</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2220">BYN2220: not callable</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only functions, methods and types with a constructor (or built-ins such as <code>List&lt;T&gt;()</code>) can be called. Bayan has no function values in v0.1.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call a function, or for numbers convert with a cast: <code>(i64)x</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
n()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2220:</span> `i32` cannot be called
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ n()
    │ ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2220</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2221">BYN2221: not iterable</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>for</code> walks ranges, arrays, spans, lists, the characters of a string, and user types conforming to <code>IIndexable</code> or <code>IEnumerator</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Iterate a range (<code>0..n</code>) or a collection, or make the type conform to <code>IIndexable</code> (<code>i64 count { get; }</code> and <code>Item get(i64 index)</code>) or <code>IEnumerator</code> (<code>bool moveNext(ref this)</code> and <code>Item current { get; }</code>).</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for x in 5 { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2221:</span> `i32` cannot be iterated
  --&gt; main.byn:1:10

  1 │ for x in 5 { }
    │          ^
    │
  help: use `for` over ranges (`0..n`), spans, lists, arrays, strings, or a type conforming to
        `IIndexable` or `IEnumerator`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2221</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2222">BYN2222: array length mismatch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A fixed array <code>T[N]</code> holds exactly N elements; the literal must provide all of them.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Provide every element, or change the length.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let xs: i64[3] = [1, 2]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2222:</span> `i64[3]` needs exactly its length in elements, found 2
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ let xs: i64[3] = [1, 2]
    │                  ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2222</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2223">BYN2223: tuple name mismatch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Tuple element names are part of the type; a mismatched name usually means swapped values.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use the names of the target type, in order.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let p: (i64 x, i64 y) = (y: 1, x: 2)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2223:</span> tuple element `y` is named `x` in the target type
  --&gt; main.byn:1:26

  1 │ let p: (i64 x, i64 y) = (y: 1, x: 2)
    │                          ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2223</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2225">BYN2225: not formattable</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An interpolation hole holds a value with no textual form.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Interpolation formats primitives, errors, enums, optionals and tuples of them, and structs that define <code>formatInto(ref StrBuf)</code>. Structs have no default display (HANDOFF §13): the compiler will not guess a format for you. Spans, lists and arenas have no single textual form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>formatInto(ref StrBuf)</code> to the struct, or use <code>{x, as: .debug}</code> for the debug form (the struct-literal form, when every field formats):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
    i64 y

    void formatInto(ref StrBuf out) {
        out.append($"({x}, {y})")
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>For a collection, format the elements in a loop, or a summary such as <code>xs.length</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
print($"{xs}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2225:</span> `List&lt;i64&gt;` cannot be formatted
  --&gt; main.byn:2:10

  2 │ print($"{xs}")
    │          ^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2225</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2226">BYN2226: not disposable</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>using</code> releases a value's memory at the end of the scope; only <code>Arena</code> and <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> own memory in v0.1.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>using</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">using let n = 5</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2226:</span> `using` needs a value that owns memory, not `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ using let n = 5
    │           ^
    │
  help: use `using` only for values that own memory: `Arena` and `List&lt;T&gt;`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2226</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2227">BYN2227: ambiguous variant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> When a function can fail with several error sets, a bare <code>.variant</code> must name exactly one variant.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Qualify it: <code>MoneyError.invalid</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error MoneyError {
    invalid
}

union error ParseError {
    invalid
}

void check(i64 n) fails MoneyError, ParseError {
    fail .invalid if n &lt; 0
}

check(1) catch return</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2227:</span> `.invalid` is ambiguous; it exists in `MoneyError`, `ParseError`
  --&gt; main.byn:10:10

  10 │     fail .invalid if n &lt; 0
     │          ^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2227</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2228">BYN2228: unknown variant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An error enum lists its variants; others do not exist.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of the declared variants, or add the variant to the error enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error MoneyError {
    invalid
}

let e = MoneyError.overflow</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2228:</span> `overflow` is not a variant of `MoneyError`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:20

  5 │ let e = MoneyError.overflow
    │                    ^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2228</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2229">BYN2229: invalid const array</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A module-level <code>const</code> array is stored once as static data, so its element type must be a number type, <code>bool</code>, <code>rune</code> or <code>string</code>, its type a fixed-size <code>T[N]</code>, and every element a literal or constant. Structs, <code>List</code>s and computed elements have no static form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use only literals and constants, or build the collection at run time with <code>let</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">string label() =&gt; "x"
i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2

const names: string[2] = ["a", label()]
const computed: i64[3] = [1, twice(2), 3]
const list: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1, 2]
const table: i64[2] = [1, 2]

table[0] = 5</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2229:</span> module-level `const` array `names` must hold literal numbers, `bool`s, `rune`s or
`string`s
  --&gt; main.byn:4:32

  4 │ const names: string[2] = ["a", label()]
    │                                ^^^^^^^ not a constant
    │
  help: write `const T[N] names = [ … ]` with `T` a number type, `bool`, `rune` or `string`, and
        only literals or constants inside</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2229</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2230">BYN2230: renamed member</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A built-in member is used under its old name: <code>add</code> on a <code>List</code>, <code>asString()</code> on a <code>StrBuf</code>, <code>onPanic</code> on <code>Panic</code>, or a round-3 rename that changes the call's shape.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Round 2 gives each idea one name (HANDOFF §11, §12). Ordered collections <code>append</code> (unordered ones <code>add</code>). A <code>StrBuf</code> hands back its text with <code>finish()</code>, or lends it with <code>view</code>. The old names stay recognised so the compiler can tell you the new one. Sizes keep <code>length</code> (round 3 keeps length). Round 3 names the panic hook for its role, <code>Panic.handler</code>; spells ranges and lookups with particles (<code>remove(from:until:)</code>, <code>tryGet(at:)</code>); and drops the <code>toX</code>/<code>asX</code> members for a view noun (<code>span</code>) and the <code>string(x, as:)</code> conversion. Decoding bytes is a codec, <code>Utf8.decode</code>, and <code>string(x)</code> only formats.</p>
<p>| Old | New | |---|---| | <code>list.add(x)</code> | <code>list.append(x)</code> | | <code>buf.asString()</code> | <code>buf.finish()</code> | | <code>Panic.onPanic = f</code> | <code>Panic.handler = f</code> | | <code>list.removeRange(a, b)</code> | <code>list.remove(from: a, until: b)</code> | | <code>list.replaceRange(r, items)</code> | <code>list.replace(at: r, by: items)</code> | | <code>map.getOr(k, d)</code> | <code>(map.tryGet(at: k) ?? d)</code> | | <code>xs.asSpan()</code> | <code>xs.span</code> | | <code>n.toHex()</code>, <code>n.toBinary()</code>, <code>n.toOctal()</code> | <code>string(n, as: .hex)</code>, <code>.binary</code>, <code>.octal</code> | | <code>s.offset(x)</code> | <code>s.position(x)</code> | | <code>s.toUtf16()</code> | <code>s.utf16</code> | | <code>xs.toList()</code> | <code>xs.copy()</code> | | <code>array.toList()</code> | <code>array.span.copy()</code> | | <code>string.from(bytes)</code>, <code>string.fromUtf8(bytes)</code> | <code>Utf8.decode(bytes)</code> | | <code>string.tryFrom(bytes)</code> | <code>Utf8.tryDecode(bytes)</code> | | <code>try string(bytes)</code> | <code>try Utf8.decode(bytes)</code> |</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the new name (the machine fix does it):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
xs.append(1)
print(buf.finish())</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
xs.add(1)
var buf = StrBuf()
print(buf.asString())</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2230:</span> `add` is now `append`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:4

  2 │ xs.add(1)
    │    ^^^ the old name
    │
  help: rename it `append`
    │ 2 │ xs.append(1)
    │   │     ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2230</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2234">BYN2234: <code>while let</code> on a value that cannot be null</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>while let x = e { … }</code> loops while <code>e</code> is non-null and binds <code>x</code> to its value. Here <code>e</code> is not an optional (<code>T?</code>), so the loop would never stop on its own.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The loop's only exit test is "is the value null?"; a value that cannot be null makes the test meaningless and the loop endless.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Return <code>T?</code> from the source (<code>i64? next()</code>) so the loop ends at <code>null</code>, or loop with a condition: <code>while n &lt; 10 { … }</code>. To bind a value once, write <code>let n = next();</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 next() =&gt; 1

while let n = next() {
    print($"{n}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2234:</span> `while let` needs a `T?` value, got `i64`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:15

  3 │ while let n = next() {
    │               ^^^^^^
    │
  help: loop with `while` and a condition, or bind it once: `let n = …`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2234</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2235">BYN2235: spread of something that is not a sequence</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>..x</code> inside a collection literal adds every element of <code>x</code>, so <code>x</code> must be a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> or a fixed array <code>T[N]</code>. A number, a string or a map has no elements to add this way.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A spread copies a sequence's elements into the literal in order; anything else has no order or no elements of the literal's element type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> To add one value, drop the <code>..</code>; to add several, spread a list, span or fixed array.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
List&lt;i32&gt; xs = [n, 1]</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
let xs: List&lt;i32&gt; = [..n, 1]
let stock: Map&lt;string, i32&gt; = ["a": 1]
let ys: List&lt;i32&gt; = [1, ..stock]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2235:</span> `..n` spreads a `i32`, which is not a sequence
  --&gt; main.byn:2:22

  2 │ let xs: List&lt;i32&gt; = [..n, 1]
    │                      ^^^ not a `List`, `Span` or fixed array
    │
  help: drop the `..` to add `n` as one element</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2235</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2236">BYN2236: map literal with an element that is not <code>key: value</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A collection literal with a <code>key: value</code> element is a map literal, and then every element needs a key. An element without one (a plain value or a <code>..</code> spread) has no key to store it under.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>["a": 1, 2]</code> is neither a list nor a map; the compiler does not guess which one was meant.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Give every element a key, or remove the keys to make a list.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Map&lt;string, i32&gt; stock = ["A-1": 12, "B-2": 3]</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let stock: Map&lt;string, i32&gt; = ["A-1": 12, 3]
let headers = ["Accept", "Host": "x"]</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2236:</span> a map literal needs `key: value` in every element
  --&gt; main.byn:1:43

  1 │ let stock: Map&lt;string, i32&gt; = ["A-1": 12, 3]
    │                                           ^ no `key:` here
    │
  help: write every element as `key: value`, or remove the keys to make a list</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2236</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g23">Calls and arguments (BYN23xx)</h2>
<p>A call that does not match the function it names.</p>
<h3 id="byn2301">BYN2301: missing argument</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every parameter without a default needs an argument.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass the missing argument, or give the parameter a default.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 area(i64 width, by i64 height) =&gt; width * height
print($"{area(width: 3)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2301:</span> missing argument `by` for `area`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:10

  2 │ print($"{area(width: 3)}")
    │          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2301</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2302">BYN2302: too many arguments</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A call passes at most one argument per parameter.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the extra arguments.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2
print($"{twice(1, 2)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2302:</span> `twice` takes 1 argument(s)
  --&gt; main.byn:2:19

  2 │ print($"{twice(1, 2)}")
    │                   ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2302</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2303">BYN2303: argument passed twice</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each parameter receives one argument.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove one of them.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2
print($"{twice(x: 1, x: 2)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2303:</span> `x` is passed twice
  --&gt; main.byn:2:22

  2 │ print($"{twice(x: 1, x: 2)}")
    │                      ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2303</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2304">BYN2304: positional after named</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> After a named argument, positions no longer line up with parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the remaining arguments too.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 add(i64 a, to string b) =&gt; a
print($"{add(a: 1, "x")}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2304:</span> a positional argument cannot follow a named one
  --&gt; main.byn:2:20

  2 │ print($"{add(a: 1, "x")}")
    │                    ^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2304</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2305">BYN2305: argument must be named</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>f(true)</code> doesn't say what the argument means. Bayan requires the parameter name for <code>bool</code> parameters. An argument that is a variable or field with the parameter's name (<code>verbose</code> or <code>options.verbose</code> for <code>verbose</code>) counts as named. Parameters that share a type no longer need names: since design round 3 every required parameter besides the object carries a particle, and the call writes it (BYN5005).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name it: <code>show(verbose: true)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show(bool verbose) {
}

show(true)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2305:</span> argument for `verbose` must be named
  --&gt; main.byn:4:6

  4 │ show(true)
    │      ^^^^
    │
  help: write `verbose: …`; a `bool` argument says nothing at the call site
    │ 4 │ show(verbose: true)
    │   │      +++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2305</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2306">BYN2306: argument modifier mismatch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>ref</code> and <code>out</code> show at the call site that the callee may change the caller's variable; they must match the parameter.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the modifier the parameter declares: <code>bump(ref total)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void bump(ref i64 total) {
    total += 1
}

var t: i64 = 0
bump(t)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2306:</span> argument for `total` must be passed as `ref`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:6

  6 │ bump(t)
    │      ^
    │
    │ 6 │ bump(ref t)
    │   │      ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2306</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2310">BYN2310: span parameter is never written through</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> parameter promises the function may change the caller's elements, so callers can't pass a <code>string</code>, <code>string.bytes</code> or another read-only view. A function that only reads should say so with <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code>; a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> still converts to it implicitly.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the parameter <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; values</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 total(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    var sum: i64 = 0
    for v in values {
        sum += v
    }
    return sum + values[0] + values.length
}

void zero(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    values[0] = 0
}

void bump(ref i64 x) {
    x += 1
}

void bumpFirst(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    bump(ref values[0])
}

void forward(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    zero(values[1..])
}

void write(Span&lt;u8&gt; buffer) {
    var sb = StrBuf(buffer)
    sb.append("x")
}

i64 viaReadOnly(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) =&gt; sum(values)

i64 sum(ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; values) =&gt; values.length

void main() {
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN2310:</span> `values` is never written through; use `ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt;`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ i64 total(Span&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    │           ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: take a `ReadOnlySpan`; it also accepts strings and read-only views
    │ 1 │ i64 total(ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; values) {
    │   │           ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2310</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2311">BYN2311: <code>var</code> is never reassigned</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>var</code> says the value will change; a reader has to look for where. When nothing ever assigns it, increments it, passes it by <code>ref</code> or <code>out</code>, or mutates through it, the <code>var</code> is noise, and <code>let</code> says what is true. This applies to locals and to <code>var</code> pattern bindings alike (spec §7.1, §6.5). Parameters, <code>for</code> loop variables and <code>let</code> bindings are never reported.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare them with <code>let</code>: <code>let total = prices.length;</code> and <code>.circle(let r)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    xs.add(1)
    var sb = StrBuf()
    sb.append("hi")
    print(sb.asString())
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN2311:</span> `xs` is never reassigned; declare it with `let`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │     var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    │         ^^
    │
  help: declare it with `let` to tell the reader the value never changes
    │ 2 │     let xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    │   │     +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2311</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2320">BYN2320: <code>requires</code> clause is not <code>bool</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A function's <code>requires</code> clause has a type other than <code>bool</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>requires</code> clause is a condition the arguments must meet on entry; the function panics in every build mode when it is false. Only a <code>bool</code> can be true or false (HANDOFF §7).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the condition the argument must meet:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 half(i32 n) requires n &gt;= 0 {
    return n / 2
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 half(i32 n) expects n {
    return n / 2
}

print($"{half(4)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2320:</span> a `requires` clause must be `bool`, got `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:25

  1 │ i32 half(i32 n) expects n {
    │                         ^
    │
  help: write a condition the arguments must meet, such as `count &gt; 0`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2320</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2321">BYN2321: constant argument violates <code>requires</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A call passes a constant argument that makes one of the callee's <code>requires</code> clauses false.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>requires</code> clause is the caller's obligation. A violation is a bug and panics at run time in every build mode; when the argument is a constant, the compiler already knows the call would panic, so it reports it here instead (HANDOFF §7).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass a value that meets the clause:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let price = discount(total: 200, percent: 50)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id requires it &gt; 0
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
}

let order = Order.draft { id: -1 }
let paid = Order.paid { id: 0, payment: 5 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2321:</span> `-1` violates `requires it &gt; 0` of `Order`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:31

  9 │ let order = Order.draft { id: -1 }
    │                               ^^ fails the contract
    │
  help: pass a value that meets `requires it &gt; 0`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2321</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g24">Functions and returns (BYN24xx)</h2>
<p>Return values, control flow and function shape.</p>
<h3 id="byn2401">BYN2401: missing return</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function with a return type must produce a value; falling off the end would return garbage.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add a <code>return</code> (or <code>panic(…)</code>) on the paths that reach the end.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 sign(i64 n) {
    if n &gt; 0 {
        return 1
    }
}

print($"{sign(1)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2401:</span> `sign` must return `i64` on every path
  --&gt; main.byn:5:1

  5 │ }
    │ ^
    │
  help: add a `return` (or `panic(…)`) on the paths that reach the end</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2401</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2402">BYN2402: missing return value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function with a return type must produce a value on every <code>return</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Return a value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 f() {
    return
}

print($"{f()}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2402:</span> this function returns `i64`; `return` needs a value
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     return
    │     ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2402</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2403">BYN2403: return value in a void function</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>void</code> function produces no value, so a returned value would be lost.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare a return type, or remove the value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void log() {
    return 1
}

log()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2403:</span> `log` returns nothing, but `return` has a value
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │     return 1
    │            ^
    │
  help: declare a return type instead of `void`, or remove the value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2403</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2404">BYN2404: <code>never</code> function can return</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>never</code> promises the function does not return (it panics, exits or loops forever).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> End every path with <code>panic(…)</code> or a call to another <code>never</code> function.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">never halt() {
    print("x")
}

halt()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2404:</span> `halt` returns `never` but can reach its end
  --&gt; main.byn:3:1

  3 │ }
    │ ^
    │
  help: end every path with `panic(…)` or a call that never returns</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2404</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2405">BYN2405: unreachable code</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Code after <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, a failure or a call that returns <code>never</code> cannot run; it is usually a mistake.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the code or move it before the jump.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">return 1
print("done")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN2405:</span> unreachable code
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ print("done")
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2405</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2406">BYN2406: jump outside a loop</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>break</code> leaves a loop or a <code>switch</code>; <code>continue</code> starts a loop's next iteration.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use <code>return</code> to leave the function.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">break</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2406:</span> `break` outside a loop
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ break
    │ ^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2406</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2407">BYN2407: continue targets a switch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>continue</code> starts the next iteration of a loop; a <code>switch</code> has no iterations.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Label the enclosing loop and continue that.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = 1
pick: switch x {
    case 1:
        continue pick
    default:
        break
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2407:</span> `continue pick` refers to a `switch`, not a loop
  --&gt; main.byn:4:18

  4 │         continue pick
    │                  ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2407</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2408">BYN2408: label not on a loop</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Labels exist only for <code>break label;</code> and <code>continue label;</code>, which target loops and <code>switch</code> statements.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the label, or move it to a loop.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">done: print("x")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2408:</span> label `done` must be on a loop or `switch`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ done: print("x")
    │ ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2408</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2409">BYN2409: duplicate default</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only one section can handle the values that no other section matches.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove one of them.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let x = 1
switch x {
    default:
        break
    default:
        break
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2409:</span> `switch` has more than one `default` section
  --&gt; main.byn:5:5

  5 │     default:
    │     ^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2409</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2410">BYN2410: non-exhaustive switch</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>switch</code> expression must produce a value for every input, and a <code>switch</code> over an enum, an error enum or <code>bool</code> must name every case so adding a variant shows every place that needs updating.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the missing cases (listed in the label), or <code>_ =&gt; …</code> / <code>default:</code> for the rest. For a union declared in this package, the help names the missing arms and does not suggest <code>_</code>, since a <code>_</code> arm on such a union is the BYN2712 warning.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">enum Color {
    red
    green
    blue
}

i64 code(Color c) =&gt; c switch {
    .red =&gt; 1,
}

print($"{code(.blue)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2410:</span> `switch` does not handle every `Color`
  --&gt; main.byn:7:22

  7 │ i64 code(Color c) =&gt; c switch {
    │                      ^ not covered: .green, .blue
    │
  help: add the missing arms, or `_ =&gt; …` for the rest</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2410</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2411">BYN2411: pattern cannot match</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A pattern that can never match its input's type is always a bug.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the pattern, or match against the right type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let n = 5
if n is null { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2411:</span> `null` cannot match `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ if n is null { }
    │         ^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2411</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g26">Errors and failures (BYN26xx)</h2>
<p>Functions that fail, and code that does not handle the failure.</p>
<h3 id="byn2601">BYN2601: <code>try</code> is not allowed in <code>defer</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Cleanup runs while the function is already returning, possibly because of another error. A failure there would have to replace or drop the first error, so cleanup must not fail. This covers <code>defer</code> and <code>defer failing</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Handle the error inside the cleanup:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">defer flush(file) catch log("flush failed")</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">defer try flush(file)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2601:</span> `try` is not allowed in `defer`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:7

  1 │ defer try flush(file)
    │       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: handle the error here with `catch`; cleanup cannot fail</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2601</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2602">BYN2602: unhandled failure</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Errors are values that must be dealt with where they occur: passed on with <code>try</code>, or handled with <code>catch</code>. Ignoring one silently is not possible.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>try parse(text)</code> in a function that <code>fails</code>, or <code>parse(text) catch …</code> to handle it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    empty
}

void f() fails ParseError {
    defer {
        fail ParseError.empty
    }
}

f()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2602:</span> `f` can fail; handle the error or pass it on
  --&gt; main.byn:11:1

  11 │ f()
     │ ^^^
     │
  help: propagate the error with `try`, or handle it with `catch`
     │ 11 │ try f()
     │    │ ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2602</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2603">BYN2603: undeclared error</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function's signature lists every error set it can fail with, so callers know what to handle.</p>
<p>The message and the help name each set with its type arguments: a function that validates two types gets `<code> help: add it to the signature: </code>fails ValidationError&lt;Address&gt;, ValidationError&lt;Signup&gt;<code> </code>`.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the set to the <code>fails</code> clause, or handle the error with <code>catch</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    empty
}

i64 f(string s) fails ParseError {
    fail .missing if s.isEmpty
    return 1
}

print($"{f("x")}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2603:</span> `.missing` is not in the `fails` clause of `f`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:10

  6 │     fail .missing if s.isEmpty
    │          ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: name a case of `ParseError`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2603</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2604">BYN2604: <code>try</code> in a function that cannot fail</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>try</code> passes an error to the caller, which needs a <code>fails</code> clause to say so.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Handle the error with <code>catch</code>, or add <code>fails …</code> to the signature.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError {
    empty
}

i64 f(string s) {
    fail ParseError.empty if s.isEmpty
    return 1
}

print($"{f("x")}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2604:</span> `f` cannot fail, so it cannot pass on `ParseError`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:10

  6 │     fail ParseError.empty if s.isEmpty
    │          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: declare the error in a `fails` clause, or handle it here</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2604</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2605">BYN2605: nothing to handle</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code> apply to a call of a function that <code>fails</code>; on anything else they do nothing.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>try</code> / <code>catch</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2
let n = try twice(2)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2605:</span> `try` needs a call that can fail
  --&gt; main.byn:2:13

  2 │ let n = try twice(2)
    │             ^^^^^^^^
    │
    │ 2 │ let n = try twice(2)
    │   │         ----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2605</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2606">BYN2606: <code>defer failing</code> in a function that cannot fail</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>defer failing</code> runs only when the function fails. In a function without a <code>fails</code> clause it would never run.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>failing</code> so the cleanup runs on every exit, or add a <code>fails</code> clause if the function can fail:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void f() {
    defer cleanup()
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void f() {
    defer failing cleanup()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2606:</span> `defer failing` in a function that cannot fail
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     defer failing cleanup()
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: remove `failing` to run it on every exit, or add a `fails` clause to the function</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2606</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2607">BYN2607: public function with inferred errors</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A public function's error sets are part of its API; inferring them would let a change inside the body silently change what callers must handle.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> List the sets: <code>fails ParseError</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public i64 parse(string s) fails {
    return 1
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2607:</span> public function `parse` must list its errors
  --&gt; main.byn:1:28

  1 │ public i64 parse(string s) fails {
    │                            ^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2607</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2608">BYN2608: not an error set</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>fails</code> clause lists <code>error enum</code> types.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name an error enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 f() fails i64 { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2608:</span> `i64` is not an error enum
  --&gt; main.byn:1:15

  1 │ i64 f() fails i64 { }
    │               ^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2608</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2609">BYN2609: empty error set</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An error enum with no variants can never be produced, so failing with it means nothing.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the variants, or remove the enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error NoError {
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2609:</span> error enum `NoError` has no variants
  --&gt; main.byn:1:13

  1 │ union error NoError {
    │             ^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2609</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2610">BYN2610: jump out of defer</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Cleanup must run to completion; jumping out of a <code>defer</code> or <code>defer failing</code> body would skip the rest of the cleanup.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Restructure the cleanup so it ends normally.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void f() {
    defer {
        return
    }
}

f()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2610:</span> `return` cannot leave a `defer` or `defer failing` body
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │         return
    │         ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2610</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2611">BYN2611: catch block completes</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> When the call fails there is no value to use, so a <code>catch { … }</code> block on a call that returns a value has to leave: the code after it would otherwise read a value that was never produced. A call returning <code>void</code> has no value, so its block may simply end.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> End the block with <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code> or a panic, or give a value with the expression form: <code>parse(s) catch -1</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error E {
    bad
    worse
}

i64 parse(i64 n) fails E {
    fail .bad if n == 0
    return n
}

i64 handle(i64 n) =&gt; parse(n) catch switch {
    E.bad =&gt; { print("bad") },
    E.worse =&gt; return -1
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2611:</span> this `catch` block can complete normally; end it with `return`, `break`, `continue`
or a panic
  --&gt; main.byn:12:29

  12 │     E.bad =&gt; { print("bad") },
     │                             ^
     │
  help: leave the block instead of falling through; the call returns a value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2611</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2612">BYN2612: not allowed in a panic handler</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>defer panicking</code> body, or the function named by <code>Panic.handler</code>, uses <code>try</code>, <code>return</code>, <code>break</code>, <code>continue</code>, an assignment or an allocation.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A panic means the program is in a state it cannot trust, and handlers run on the way to aborting it (spec §7.6). Panic handlers can't recover: they cannot resume normal flow, fail, change state, or ask an allocator that may itself be broken for memory. They can only report, flush and release outside resources.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the report straight to <code>print</code>, <code>panic</code> or a <code>StrBuf</code> the handler was given, and read only <code>let</code> values:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void save(Db db) {
    let name = db.name
    defer panicking (p) print($"save to {name} failed: {p.message}")
    …
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void onCrash(Panic p) {
    var s = StrBuf.over(stackalloc u8[2])
    s.append("abcdefghij")
}

void main() {
    Panic.handler = onCrash
    print("ok")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2612:</span> allocation is not allowed in a panic handler
  --&gt; main.byn:2:13

  2 │     var s = StrBuf.over(stackalloc u8[2])
    │             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: remove it; panic handlers can't recover</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2612</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2613">BYN2613: panic handler reads a <code>var</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>defer panicking</code> body, or the function named by <code>Panic.handler</code>, reads a <code>var</code> declared outside it. <code>let</code> bindings, <code>is let</code> and <code>for</code> loop variables, and parameters are fine.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A panic can happen half way through an update, so a <code>var</code> may hold a torn or stale value when the handler runs. A <code>let</code> binding, a loop or pattern variable, or a parameter was fixed before the handler was registered, so the handler sees what it was written against (spec §7.6).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Copy the value into a <code>let</code> before the handler and read that, or leave it out of the report:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let total = items.length
defer panicking print($"writing {total} items failed")</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var done = 0
defer panicking print($"{done} items written")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2613:</span> a panic handler reads only `let` bindings and parameters
  --&gt; main.byn:2:26

  2 │ defer panicking print($"{done} items written")
    │                          ^^^^ `done` is not a `let` or a parameter
    │
  help: copy the value into a `let` before the handler and read that</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2613</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2614">BYN2614: <code>Panic.handler</code> set outside the start of <code>main</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>Panic.handler = f;</code> appears somewhere other than the first statement of the program's entry function, appears a second time, or appears in a library package.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The hook runs for every panic in the process, after the <code>defer panicking</code> handlers and before the trace is printed (spec §7.6). Setting it once, before anything else runs, means every panic sees the same hook and no library can replace the program's choice.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Make it the first statement of <code>main</code>, and set it only there:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    Panic.handler = report
    print("starting")
}</code></pre>
<p>A library cannot set it; the program that uses the library does.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    print("starting")
    Panic.handler = report
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2614:</span> `Panic.handler` may be set only as the first statement of `main`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:5

  3 │     Panic.handler = report
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not the first statement of `main`
    │
  help: set it once, as the first statement of `main`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2614</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2615">BYN2615: panic handler reads a local moved after it</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>defer panicking</code> body reads a local that is moved, on some path, after the handler is registered.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A panic handler runs at a panic anywhere in its scope, not only at the scope's exit. A panic after the move would run the handler on a value that has already been handed away and may be freed (spec §7.6).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the value before registering the handler, or read a <code>let</code> copied from it there:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let ys = List&lt;i64&gt;()
let n = ys.length
defer panicking print($"handler sees {n}")
sink(move ys)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// A panic handler can run at any later panic in its scope, so what it reads may not be moved after it (BYN2615).
void sink(move List&lt;i64&gt; ys) {
    print($"{ys.length}")
}

void main() {
    let ys = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    defer panicking print($"handler sees {ys.length}")
    sink(move ys)
    panic("stop")
}

void maybe(bool ok) {
    let zs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    defer panicking print($"{zs.length}")
    if ok {
        sink(move zs)
    }
}

// Moved before the handler is registered: the handler cannot see it, and moving the copy is fine.
void movedFirst() {
    let xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    let n = xs.length
    sink(move xs)
    defer panicking print($"{n}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2615:</span> a panic handler reads `ys`, which is moved after the handler is registered
  --&gt; main.byn:9:10

  9 │     sink(move ys)
    │          ^^^^^^^ the `defer panicking` on line 8 reads it after a later panic
    │
  help: move `ys` before the `defer panicking`, or read a `let` copied from it there</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2615</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g27">Structs, unions, enums and patterns (BYN27xx)</h2>
<p>Declarations of data types, and the patterns that take them apart.</p>
<h3 id="byn2700">BYN2700: unknown union variant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The name after the union, or after a target-typed <code>.</code>, must be one of the union's variants.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a declared variant, or add the variant to the union.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    none
}

void main() {
    let s = Shape.square(1)
    let t: Shape = .circle(2)
    if t is .Triangle(let x) {
        print("never")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2700:</span> `square` is not a variant of union `Shape`
  --&gt; main.byn:7:19

  7 │     let s = Shape.square(1)
    │                   ^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2700</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2701">BYN2701: duplicate union variant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each variant names one tag; two variants with the same name could not be told apart.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename or remove one of the variants.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    none
    circle(f32 radius)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2701:</span> union `Shape` already has a variant `circle`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:5

  4 │     circle(f32 radius)
    │     ^^^^^^ already declared on line 2</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2701</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2702">BYN2702: empty union</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A union with no variants has no values, so nothing can ever have its type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the variants, or remove the union.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Nothing { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2702:</span> union `Nothing` has no variants
  --&gt; main.byn:1:7

  1 │ union Nothing { }
    │       ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: add the variants, or remove the union</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2702</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2703">BYN2703: variant pattern arity</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A variant pattern lists one subpattern per payload field, in declaration order.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> List every field, with <code>_</code> for the ones you do not need: <code>.rect(let w, _)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape { rect(f32 w, f32 h) }

f32 width(Shape s) =&gt; s switch { .rect(let w) =&gt; w }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2703:</span> variant `rect` has 2 payload field(s), but the pattern lists 1
  --&gt; main.byn:3:34

  3 │ f32 width(Shape s) =&gt; s switch { .rect(let w) =&gt; w }
    │                                  ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: list every field: `.rect(let w, let h)`, or `_` to skip one</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2703</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2704">BYN2704: payload read without a pattern</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A union value may hold any of its variants, so a payload field is read only after matching the variant.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Match the variant and bind the field: <code>if s is .circle(let r) { … }</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    none
}

void main() {
    let s = Shape.circle(1)
    print(s.r)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2704:</span> the payload of a `Shape` is read with a pattern, not `.r`
  --&gt; main.byn:8:13

  8 │     print(s.r)
    │             ^
    │
  help: match the variant and bind the field, as in `case .Variant(let x):`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2704</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2705">BYN2705: variant used without its payload</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A variant with payload fields is constructed by calling it with a value for each field. The same holds for a <code>union error</code> case in <code>fail</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass the payload: <code>Shape.circle(1)</code>, <code>fail .timeout(after: 30)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    none
}

void main() {
    let s = Shape.circle
    let t: Shape = .circle
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2705:</span> variant `circle` carries a payload
  --&gt; main.byn:7:13

  7 │     let s = Shape.circle
    │             ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: pass it: `Shape.circle(r)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2705</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2706">BYN2706: unit variant called</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A variant without payload fields is a value on its own; it takes no arguments, and its pattern has no parentheses. The same holds for a <code>union error</code> case.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Drop the parentheses: <code>Shape.none</code>, <code>fail .closed</code>, or <code>.none</code> in a pattern.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    none
}

void main() {
    let s = Shape.none()
    let t: Shape = .none
    if t is .none() {
        print("none")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2706:</span> variant `none` has no payload
  --&gt; main.byn:7:13

  7 │     let s = Shape.none()
    │             ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write `Shape.none` without parentheses</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2706</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2708">BYN2708: borrowed union payload</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A union payload is stored by value; a span would borrow memory the union may outlive.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Store an owned <code>List</code> copied with <code>.copy(to: alloc)</code> instead.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Chunk {
    bytes(ReadOnlySpan&lt;u8&gt; data)
    empty
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2708:</span> a union payload cannot hold a borrowed `ReadOnlySpan&lt;u8&gt;`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:28

  2 │     bytes(ReadOnlySpan&lt;u8&gt; data)
    │                            ^^^^
    │
  help: store a `List` copied with `.copy(to: alloc)` instead</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2708</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2709">BYN2709: union case value is not an integer constant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A payload-free case may declare the integer it is stored as, for C interop: <code>union Mode { read = 1, write = 2 }</code>. The value becomes the case's tag, an <code>i32</code>, so it must be an integer literal (optionally negated) that fits in <code>i32</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the value as an integer literal, or remove it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Mode { read = 1, write = 2 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Mode {
    read = 1.5
    write = "w"
    big = 3000000000
    fine = -3
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2709:</span> a case value must be an integer constant
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │     read = 1.5
    │            ^^^ not an `i32` integer literal
    │
  help: write the value as an integer literal, or remove it</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2709</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2710">BYN2710: union case with data has a value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only payload-free cases can declare an integer value; a case that carries data is not a plain integer on the C side.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the value from the case that carries data:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape { circle(f32 r), none = 2 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r) = 1
    none = 2
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2710:</span> `circle` carries data and cannot have a value
  --&gt; main.byn:2:21

  2 │     circle(f32 r) = 1
    │                     ^ only a case without data can have a value
    │
  help: remove the value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2710</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2711">BYN2711: two union cases share a value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A case's value is its tag; two cases with the same tag could not be told apart. A case without a value takes one more than the case before it, so it can collide with a later explicit value.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Give each case its own value:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Mode { read = 1, write = 2 }
union Level { low = 1, mid, high = 3 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Mode {
    read = 1
    write = 1
}

union Level {
    low = 1
    mid
    high = 2
}

union Step {
    two = 2
    one = 1
    next
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2711:</span> `read` and `write` share the value 1
  --&gt; main.byn:3:13

  3 │     write = 1
    │             ^ already used
    │
  help: give each case its own value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2711</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2712">BYN2712: <code>_</code> arm on a union declared in this package (warning)</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>switch</code> on a union is exhaustive: when a case is added, every <code>switch</code> that does not handle it is an error, which points at the code to update. A <code>_</code> arm matches every case, including the ones added later, so it hides those errors. For a union declared in the same package, you can list its cases instead.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> List the remaining cases instead of <code>_</code> (the help lists them):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">f32 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
    .circle(let r) =&gt; r * r,
    .rect(_, _) or .none =&gt; 0,
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(f32 r)
    rect(f32 w, f32 h)
    none
}

f32 area(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
    .circle(let r) =&gt; r * r,
    _ =&gt; 0,
}

f32 width(Shape s) =&gt; s switch {
    .circle(let r) =&gt; r,
    .rect(let w, _) =&gt; w,
    .none =&gt; 0,
    _ =&gt; 0,
}

string label(Shape? s) =&gt; s switch {
    .none =&gt; "none",
    _ =&gt; "other",
}

print($"{area(.none)} {width(.none)} {label(null)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN2712:</span> `_` hides future cases of `Shape`, declared in this package
  --&gt; main.byn:9:5

  9 │     _ =&gt; 0,
    │     ^ matches every case added later
    │
  help: list the cases instead: .rect, .none</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2712</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2720">BYN2720: unknown enum member</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An enum lists its members; others do not exist. Besides its members, an enum type has only <code>count</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>fromOrdinal</code> and <code>parse</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of the declared members, or add the member to the enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">enum Color {
    red
    green
    blue
}

let c = Color.gren
let d: Color = .purple</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2720:</span> `gren` is not a member of `Color`
  --&gt; main.byn:7:15

  7 │ let c = Color.gren
    │               ^^^^
    │
  help: did you mean `green`?</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2720</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2721">BYN2721: duplicate enum member</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each member of an enum names one value, so a name can appear only once.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the repeated member, or rename it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">enum Color {
    red
    green
    red
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2721:</span> enum `Color` already has a member `red`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:5

  4 │     red
    │     ^^^ already declared on line 2</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2721</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2722">BYN2722: empty enum</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An enum with no members has no values, so nothing can ever have its type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the members, or remove the enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">enum Nothing { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2722:</span> enum `Nothing` has no members
  --&gt; main.byn:1:6

  1 │ enum Nothing { }
    │      ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: add the members, or remove the enum</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2722</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2723">BYN2723: enum argument is not constant</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The data of an enum member is stored once in a static table, indexed by ordinal, so every argument must be known at compile time: a literal, a <code>const</code>, an expression of those, or a member of a plain enum.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the value as a literal or a <code>const</code> (<code>large(1024)</code>), or compute it at run time from the member, for example in a function taking the enum.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 twice(i64 x) =&gt; x * 2

enum Size(i64 bytes) {
    small(1)
    large(twice(512))
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2723:</span> argument `bytes` of `Size.large` must be a compile-time constant
  --&gt; main.byn:5:11

  5 │     large(twice(512))
    │           ^^^^^^^^^^ computed at run time
    │
  help: use literals and `const`s only; enum data is static</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2723</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2724">BYN2724: invalid enum parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each parameter of a data enum becomes a static table with one entry per member, so its type must have a static form: a number type, <code>bool</code>, <code>rune</code>, <code>string</code> or a plain enum (one without data). Parameters are read-only values, so <code>ref</code>, <code>out</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>move</code> and default values are not allowed either.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Store the parts as separate scalar parameters (<code>enum Shape(i64 cornerX)</code>), and pass every argument explicitly.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point { i64 x }

enum Shape(Point corner, ref i64 sides, i64 depth = 1) {
    square(Point { x: 0 }, 4, 1)
}

enum Unit(f64 scale) { metre(1.0) }

enum Length(Unit scale) { cm(.metre) }

enum Label(string name, i64 ordinal) { a("a", 1) }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2724:</span> enum parameter `corner` must be a number, `bool`, `rune`, `string` or a plain enum
  --&gt; main.byn:3:12

  3 │ enum Shape(Point corner, ref i64 sides, i64 depth = 1) {
    │            ^^^^^ `Point` has no static form
    │
  help: use a number type, `bool`, `rune`, `string` or a plain enum; enum data is stored in static
        tables</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2724</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2740">BYN2740: cannot infer a type argument</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A call of a generic function reads its type arguments from the types of its arguments. When no argument mentions a type parameter (or every such argument is <code>null</code>), there is nothing to read it from; Bayan does not infer type arguments from the expected type of the call.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the type arguments out, as in <code>none&lt;i64&gt;()</code>, or pass an argument the type can be read from.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">T? none&lt;T&gt;() =&gt; null
var x = none()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2740:</span> cannot infer type argument `T` of `none`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ var x = none()
    │         ^^^^
    │
  help: pass an argument it can be read from, or write it out: `none&lt;T&gt;(…)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2740</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2741">BYN2741: conflicting type arguments</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every argument whose parameter mentions a type parameter fixes that type parameter. When two of them fix it to different types and neither converts to the other, the call has no single instantiation.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Make the arguments agree, convert one of them, or write the type arguments out, as in <code>pick&lt;string&gt;(…)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">T pick&lt;T&gt;(bool first, T a, T b) =&gt; first ? a : b
var x = pick(first: true, a: 1, b: "one")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2741:</span> type argument `T` of `pick` is inferred as both `i32` and `string`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:36

  2 │ var x = pick(first: true, a: 1, b: "one")
    │                                    ^^^^^
    │
  help: make the arguments agree, or write it out: `pick&lt;…&gt;(…)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2741</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2742">BYN2742: wrong number of type arguments in a call</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A generic function takes exactly as many type arguments as it declares type parameters; a function that is not generic takes none.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass one type argument per type parameter, or leave them all out and let the arguments decide.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">T pick&lt;T&gt;(bool first, T a, T b) =&gt; first ? a : b
var x = pick&lt;i64, string&gt;(first: true, a: 1, b: 2)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2742:</span> `pick` takes 1 type argument(s), not 2
  --&gt; main.byn:2:9

  2 │ var x = pick&lt;i64, string&gt;(first: true, a: 1, b: 2)
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2742</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2743">BYN2743: operator on a type parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Type parameters have no constraints, so the body of a generic function or type must be valid for every type it could be instantiated with. A value of a type parameter <code>T</code> can only be copied, compared with <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code>, passed, stored, returned and formatted; arithmetic, ordering (<code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>), logic and bitwise operators are not available on every type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the function for the concrete type (<code>i64 add(i64 a, i64 b)</code>), or pass the operation in as data the caller computes.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">T add&lt;T&gt;(T a, T b) =&gt; a + b</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2743:</span> `+` cannot be applied to a value of type parameter `T`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:23

  1 │ T add&lt;T&gt;(T a, T b) =&gt; a + b
    │                       ^^^^^
    │
  help: use a `T` only to copy, compare with `==` and `!=`, pass, store, return and format; type
        parameters have no constraints</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2743</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2744">BYN2744: member of a type parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A type parameter with no <code>where</code> clause has no known members: a field, property or method that one type argument has, another lacks. (With a <code>where T : I</code> clause, a missing member is [BYN2806](BYN2806.md).)</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>where T : I</code> naming an interface that declares the member, or take the concrete type the member belongs to.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 size&lt;T&gt;(T x) =&gt; x.length</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2744:</span> `length` is not a member of type parameter `T`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:23

  1 │ i64 size&lt;T&gt;(T x) =&gt; x.length
    │                       ^^^^^^
    │
  help: add `where T : …` naming an interface that declares `length`, or use a concrete type</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2744</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2760">BYN2760: invalid <code>Map</code> key</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>Map</code> hashes and compares its keys by their value, so a key must be a type with one obvious equality: an integer type, <code>rune</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>Symbol</code>, a plain enum (one without data), or a tuple or struct whose parts are all keys. Floats (where <code>0.0 == -0.0</code> and <code>NaN != NaN</code>), optionals, enums with data, unions, collections and <code>@noEquality</code> structs are not keys. A tuple or struct with a part that is not a key is [BYN2831](BYN2831.md), which names the part.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Key by a value of one of the allowed types, such as an integer id, a <code>string</code> or <code>Symbol</code> name, a plain enum, or a tuple or struct of those.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let byWeight = Map&lt;f64, string&gt;()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2760:</span> `f64` cannot be a `Map` key
  --&gt; main.byn:1:20

  1 │ let byWeight = Map&lt;f64, string&gt;()
    │                    ^^^
    │
  help: use an integer type, `rune`, `bool`, `string`, `Symbol`, a plain enum, or a tuple or struct
        of those as the key</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2760</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2761">BYN2761: type contains itself by value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Structs and unions are stored inline: a field or payload of type <code>T</code> holds a whole <code>T</code>. A type that contains itself by value, directly or through other structs, unions, optionals, arrays or tuples, would be infinitely large. The message lists the cycle, starting and ending at the reported type. <code>Owned&lt;T&gt;</code> is a pointer into an allocator and <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> holds its elements elsewhere, so either breaks the cycle.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Refer to the inner value through <code>Owned&lt;…&gt;</code>, built with <code>Owned(value)</code> from <code>context.allocator</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Expr {
    lit(i64 v),
    add(Owned&lt;Expr&gt; l, Owned&lt;Expr&gt; r),
}</code></pre>
<p>or keep the children in a <code>List&lt;…&gt;</code>, or refer to them by index.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Node {
    i64 value
    Node? next
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2761:</span> `Node` contains itself by value: Node → Node
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ struct Node {
    │        ^^^^
    │
  help: store the inner one as `Owned&lt;Node&gt;` (or in a `List&lt;Node&gt;`); a value cannot contain itself</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2761</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2770">BYN2770: unknown attribute</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Attributes change how a declaration compiles, so a misspelled or unsupported one is an error instead of being ignored. The attributes are <code>[ExitCode(n)]</code>, on an error variant, and <code>[Cases(...)]</code>, on a test that takes parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the attribute, or correct its name.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error StepError {
    @retry(3)
    /// The step timed out.
    timedOut
}

@inline
void step() { }

@exitCode(2)
void other() { }

void main() {
    step()
    other()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2770:</span> unknown attribute `retry`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:6

  2 │     @retry(3)
    │      ^^^^^
    │
  help: use `@exitCode(n)`; it is the only attribute an error variant takes</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2770</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2771">BYN2771: invalid <code>main</code> signature</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The runtime calls <code>main</code> itself, so it accepts only the shapes it knows how to call: return <code>void</code> or <code>i32</code> (the exit code), take nothing or <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args</code> (the command-line arguments without the program name), with or without <code>fails</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of <code>void main()</code>, <code>i32 main()</code>, <code>void main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)</code> or <code>i32 main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)</code>, optionally followed by <code>fails E</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">string main(i64 count) {
    return "done"
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2771:</span> `main` must return `void` or `i32` and take nothing or `ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ string main(i64 count) {
    │        ^^^^
    │
  help: write `void main()`, `i32 main()`, `void main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)` or
        `i32 main(ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; args)`, each optionally with `fails`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2771</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2772">BYN2772: invalid exit code</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>@exitCode(n)</code> sets the process exit code when a failing <code>main</code> ends with that variant. Exit codes outside 1 to 255 are truncated or mean success on common systems, and the code must be known at compile time.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass one integer constant from 1 to 255, such as <code>@exitCode(3)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ConfigError {
    @exitCode(0)
    /// Zero means success.
    zero
    @exitCode(300)
    /// Too large.
    large
    @exitCode
    /// No argument.
    missing
    @exitCode("x")
    /// Not an integer.
    text
    @exitCode(1, 2)
    /// Two arguments.
    two
    @exitCode(4)
    @exitCode(5)
    /// Repeated.
    repeated
}

void main() fails ConfigError {
    fail .zero
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2772:</span> `@exitCode` needs one integer constant from 1 to 255
  --&gt; main.byn:2:15

  2 │     @exitCode(0)
    │               ^
    │
  help: pick a code from 1 to 255; 0 means success, and exit codes above 255 do not survive the
        operating system</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2772</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2773">BYN2773: <code>@cases</code> value count does not match the parameters</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> One case of a <code>@cases(...)</code> attribute gives a different number of values than the test has parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each case runs the test once with its values bound to the parameters in order (spec §20). A missing or extra value has no parameter to go to, so the case cannot run.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Give every case one value per parameter, in parameter order:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases((0, false), (1, true))
test "quantity bounds" (i32 input, bool valid) {
    assert(valid == (input &gt; 0))
}</code></pre>
<p>A test with one parameter takes bare values: <code>@cases(1, 2, 3)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases((0, false), (1, true, 2))
test "quantity bounds" (i32 input, bool valid) {
    assert(valid == (input &gt; 0))
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2773:</span> `@cases` gives 3 values but the test takes 2 parameters
  --&gt; main.byn:1:20

  1 │ @cases((0, false), (1, true, 2))
    │                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: give each case one value per parameter, in order: `(input, valid)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2773</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2774">BYN2774: <code>@cases</code> value is not a constant</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A value in <code>@cases(...)</code> is not a constant literal: an integer, float, string, rune, <code>true</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>null</code> or a <code>.case</code> value.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Cases are fixed when the test is compiled and shown in its title, such as <code>quantity bounds (input: 101, valid: false)</code>, so each value must be known without running code. Cases from a named const list (<code>@cases(discountCases)</code>) are not supported yet.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write each value as a literal, one tuple literal per case when the test takes more than one parameter:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases(1, 2)
test "positive" (i32 n) {
    assert(n &gt; 0)
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@cases(limit(), 2)
test "positive" (i32 n) {
    assert(n &gt; 0)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2774:</span> each `@cases` value must be a constant
  --&gt; main.byn:1:8

  1 │ @cases(limit(), 2)
    │        ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write the value as a literal: a number, string, rune, `true`, `false`, `null` or a `.case`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2774</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2775">BYN2775: <code>assert</code> needs a <code>bool</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> The expression after <code>assert</code> is not a <code>bool</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>assert expr;</code> checks that <code>expr</code> is <code>true</code> and panics otherwise, showing the value of each sub-expression (spec §20). There is no truthiness in Bayan: a number, a string or an optional is not true or false by itself, so the comparison must be written out.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Compare the value with what you expect:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let count = items.length
assert count == 3</code></pre>
<p>For an optional, test it with <code>is not null</code>: <code>assert found is not null;</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let count = 3
assert count
assert "ready"
assert count == 3</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2775:</span> `assert` needs a `bool`, got `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:8

  2 │ assert count
    │        ^^^^^ this is not a `bool`
    │
  help: check the value against what you expect, e.g. `assert count == 3`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2775</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2780">BYN2780: implicit copy of a unique value</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "implicit copy of a unique value: {0}"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "write <code>move {0} with { … }</code> to give up <code>{0}</code>" (on a <code>with</code> that copies a <code>unique</code> value, <code>{0}</code> the copied variable).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A unique value owns storage: a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>StrBuf</code> or <code>Owned</code>, a <code>unique struct</code>, a type parameter inside a generic body, or anything that holds one of these by value (a struct field, a union payload, a tuple, array, optional, <code>Untrusted</code> or <code>Result</code>). Copying it would leave two values sharing that storage, and growing or freeing one would leave the other pointing at freed memory. So storing an existing place of a unique type is rejected: a <code>let</code> or <code>var</code> initializer, an assignment, a field in a struct literal or <code>with</code>, an element added to a collection, a tuple, array or optional, an argument to a <code>move</code> parameter, or a <code>return</code> of anything but a local or <code>move</code> parameter (returning those moves them). A plain parameter of unique type is a read-only borrow, so storing it is a copy too. Fresh values (calls, constructors, literals, <code>copy(to:)</code>, <code>move x</code>) are never copies, and reading in place (members, indexing, <code>for</code>, <code>switch</code>, comparison, formatting, passing to a plain or <code>ref</code> parameter) is fine. A field of unique type cannot stand for an interface getter either: the getter's result belongs to the caller, so reading the field in its place would copy it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Hand the value over with <code>move</code>, after which <code>a</code> cannot be used until it is assigned again:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var b = move a</code></pre>
<p>or make an independent copy with <code>a.copy(to: alloc)</code> (for a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> or <code>StrBuf</code>), or share the original by passing it by <code>ref</code>. In a function that should own a parameter, declare it <code>move p</code>.</p>
<p>A <code>with</code> that copies a unique value from a local hands it over the same way: <code>let copy = move tx with { label: "copy" }</code> is <code>(move tx) with { … }</code>, since <code>with</code> binds looser than every prefix operator. <code>x = x with { … }</code> is already a move and needs no <code>move</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var a = List&lt;i64&gt;()
a.append(1)
var b = a</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; main.byn:3:9

  3 │ var b = a
    │         ^
    │
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
    │ 3 │ var b = move a
    │   │         +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2780</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2781">BYN2781: use after move</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>move x</code> (or passing <code>x</code> to a <code>move</code> parameter) hands its value to a new owner, so <code>x</code> no longer holds anything. Reading it, calling a member on it or moving it again on any path after the move would touch storage that now belongs to someone else. A move in one branch of an <code>if</code> or <code>switch</code> makes the local unusable after the branches join, and a move inside a loop of a local declared outside it fails on the next iteration.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign a fresh value before using the local again:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var b = move a
a = List&lt;i64&gt;()
a.append(2)</code></pre>
<p>or move a copy instead, <code>move a.copy(to: alloc)</code>, and keep the original. In a loop, reassign the local before the next iteration.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var a = List&lt;i64&gt;()
var b = move a
a.append(2)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2781:</span> `a` is used after it was moved
  --&gt; main.byn:3:1

  3 │ a.append(2)
    │ ^ moved on line 2
    │
  help: assign `a` a new value before using it again, or move a `.copy(to: alloc)` of it instead</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2781</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2782">BYN2782: cannot move out of this</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>move</code> takes the value from an owner that then holds nothing, and the compiler has to know that owner is not used again. It can track that only for a local variable or a <code>move</code> parameter. Moving out of a field, an element, a plain or <code>ref</code> parameter would leave a hole that someone else still reads, and a call result or literal is already fresh, so there is nothing to move.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> When <code>bag</code> can be written (a <code>var</code> local or a <code>ref</code> parameter), take the value with <code>bag.items.take()</code>, which leaves an empty list in the field (it works for a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>StrBuf</code> or an optional of a unique type), copy it with <code>bag.items.copy(to: alloc)</code>, or read it in place through the field. For a parameter, declare it <code>move p</code> so the function owns it. For a fresh value, remove <code>move</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Bag {
    List&lt;i64&gt; items
}

void take(Bag bag) {
    var items = move bag.items
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2782:</span> cannot move out of field `items`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:22

  6 │     var items = move bag.items
    │                      ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: take it with `.take()`, or copy it with `.copy(to: alloc)`; its owner keeps it</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2782</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2783">BYN2783: a borrowed binding cannot be changed or moved</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>for</code> loop variable and a <code>switch</code> or <code>is</code> pattern binding of a unique type borrow from the value they were bound to: they name storage that the list, union or other subject still owns. This holds for <code>var</code> bindings too. Changing the binding (a mutating member call, an assignment to it or to its fields) or moving it would change or take storage behind the owner's back.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Change the element through its owner:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">for i in 0..lists.length { lists[i].append(1); }</code></pre>
<p>or copy the binding into a local of its own with <code>xs.copy(to: alloc)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// A loop or pattern binding of a unique type borrows from its subject, even with `var` (BYN2783).
union Slot {
    empty
    full(List&lt;i64&gt; items)
}

void main() {
    var lists = List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt;()
    lists.append(List&lt;i64&gt;())
    for xs in lists { xs.append(1) }
    for xs in lists {
        let stolen = move xs
    }
    let slot = Slot.full(List&lt;i64&gt;())
    if slot is .full(var items) { items.append(2) }
    for xs in lists { print($"{xs.length}") }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2783:</span> `xs` borrows from what it was bound to and cannot be changed or moved
  --&gt; main.byn:10:23

  10 │     for xs in lists { xs.append(1) }
     │                       ^^ `append` modifies it
     │
  help: change the subject itself, or work on a `.copy(to: alloc)`; a loop or pattern variable of a
        unique type borrows from its subject</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2783</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2784">BYN2784: a collection holding a unique value cannot be copied</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>copy(to:)</code> on a <code>List</code> or <code>Map</code> copies the elements, keys and values as they are. When those are themselves a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>StrBuf</code> or <code>Owned</code> (or a struct, union, tuple, array or optional holding one), the copy would share their storage with the original, and growing one would free memory the other still points to.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Build the copy with a loop that copies each element:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var copy = List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt;()
for inner in lists { copy.append(inner.copy(to: arena)); }</code></pre>
<p>or share the original by <code>ref</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var lists = List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt;()
let copy = lists.copy(to: arena)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2784:</span> a `List&lt;List&lt;i64&gt;&gt;` holding `List&lt;i64&gt;` cannot be copied: the copy would share its
storage
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │ let copy = lists.copy(to: arena)
    │            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: build the copy with a loop that copies each element, or share the original by `ref`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2784</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2785">BYN2785: <code>slot</code> needs a default value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>map.slot(key)</code> returns a reference to the key's value, inserting a default value first when the key is missing. That needs a default for the value type: numbers, <code>bool</code>, <code>rune</code>, <code>string</code>, optionals, and structs whose every field has an initializer. Enums, unions, lists, maps and other types have no default, so the inserted value would be made up.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write with <code>map[key] = value</code>, which inserts or updates, and read with <code>map[key]</code> (panics when missing) or <code>map.tryGet(at: key) ?? fallback</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">colors["sky"] = .red
let sky = colors.tryGet(at: "sky") ?? .green</code></pre>
<p>or give every field of a struct value type an initializer.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">enum Color {
    red
    green
}

struct Point {
    i64 x
    i64 y = 0
}

struct Origin {
    i64 x = 0
    i64 y = 0
}

void use() {
    var colors = Map&lt;string, Color&gt;()
    colors.slot("sky") = .red
    var points = Map&lt;i64, Point&gt;()
    points.slot(1).y += 1
    var lists = Map&lt;i64, List&lt;i64&gt;&gt;()
    lists.slot(1).append(2)
    var origins = Map&lt;i64, Origin&gt;()
    origins.slot(1).x += 1
    var names = Map&lt;i64, string?&gt;()
    names.slot(1) = "one"
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2785:</span> `slot` needs a default value, and `Color` has none
  --&gt; main.byn:18:5

  18 │     colors.slot("sky") = .red
     │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: read with `map[key]` or `map.tryGet(at: key) ?? fallback`, and write with `map[key] = value`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2785</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2786">BYN2786: an <code>Untrusted</code> value reaches a sink</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>Io.run</code> and <code>Io.runBatch</code> run commands. <code>Io.run</code> involves no shell, but an untrusted program path runs whatever the attacker names, and an untrusted argument can inject an option (<code>--upload-pack=…</code> to <code>git</code>, <code>-oProxyCommand=…</code> to <code>ssh</code>) that makes the program run attacker-chosen commands. <code>Io.runBatch</code> runs a batch file through <code>cmd.exe</code>, which interprets its arguments again. A value that came from outside the program (<code>Untrusted&lt;T&gt;</code>, or a list, span or array of them) must not reach either unchecked: the program or path and every argument must be ordinary values.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Check the value, then clear the mark with <code>name.trustUnchecked() because "checked against the allow list"</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() fails IoError {
    let program: Untrusted&lt;string&gt; = Untrusted("git")
    let name: Untrusted&lt;string&gt; = Untrusted("x")
    let _ = try Io.run(program, ["status"])
    let _ = try Io.run("git", ["log", name])
    let _ = try Io.run("git", ["log", name.trustUnchecked() because "checked against the branch list"])
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2786:</span> `Untrusted&lt;string&gt;` cannot be passed to `Io.run`: it is `Untrusted`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:24

  4 │     let _ = try Io.run(program, ["status"])
    │                        ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: check the value, then clear the mark with `x.trustUnchecked() because "…"`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2786</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2787">BYN2787: <code>trust</code> needs a reason</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>trust(x, reason: "…")</code> clears the <code>Untrusted</code> mark on a value, so the reason is the audit trail for why that is safe. It must be written at the call site as a non-empty string literal; a missing, empty, interpolated or variable reason says nothing a reviewer can search for.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Pass a literal that explains why the value is safe, such as <code>reason: "checked against the allow list"</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let u: Untrusted&lt;string&gt; = Untrusted("x")
print(trust(u))
print(trust(u, "checked"))</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2787:</span> `trust` needs a reason: a non-empty string literal
  --&gt; main.byn:2:7

  2 │ print(trust(u))
    │       ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write `x.trustUnchecked() because "why this value is safe"`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2787</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2788">BYN2788: <code>take()</code> needs a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>StrBuf</code> or an optional of a unique type</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>x.take()</code> moves the value out of a place and leaves that type's empty value behind, so the place stays usable. Only types with an obvious empty value support it: an empty <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code> or <code>StrBuf</code>, and <code>null</code> for an optional holding a unique type. Other types have no empty value to leave, and a plain value needs no <code>take()</code> because it is copied.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Copy a plain value, <code>let n = count;</code>. For a unique struct, move the whole local with <code>move x</code>, or <code>take()</code> the collection fields inside it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var count = 3
let n = count.take()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2788:</span> `take()` needs a `List`, `Map`, `StrBuf` or an optional of a unique type, not `i32`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:15

  2 │ let n = count.take()
    │               ^^^^
    │
  help: copy it, or move the whole local; only an owned collection or optional has an empty value to
        leave behind</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2788</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2789">BYN2789: <code>switch move …</code> needs a local or a <code>move</code> parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>switch move x</code> moves <code>x</code> into the switch so its pattern bindings own the parts they bind, and <code>x</code> is moved from afterwards. Only a value this function owns outright can be moved that way: a local or a <code>move</code> parameter. A field or element belongs to its owner, and a plain parameter is borrowed from the caller, so moving it into the switch would leave a half-moved value behind.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Drop <code>move</code> and the bindings borrow, as in <code>switch box.slot</code>. Declare a parameter <code>move</code> to own it. For a collection field, take it into a local first, <code>let items = box.items.take();</code>, and switch on that local.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// `switch move …` needs a local or a `move` parameter as its subject (BYN2789).
union Slot {
    empty
    full(List&lt;i64&gt; items)
}

struct Box {
    Slot slot
}

void inspect(Slot plain) {
    let box = Box { slot: Slot.empty }
    let slots = List&lt;Slot&gt;()
    switch move box.slot {
        default:
    }
    switch move slots[0] {
        default:
    }
    switch move plain {
        default:
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2789:</span> `switch move …` needs a local or a `move` parameter, not field `slot`
  --&gt; main.byn:14:17

  14 │     switch move box.slot {
     │                 ^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: drop `move` so the bindings borrow, declare the parameter `move`, or `.take()` a collection
        field into a local first</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2789</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2790">BYN2790: pattern binds the same storage more than once in <code>switch move …</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> In <code>switch move x</code> each pattern binding owns the part it binds. A binding for a whole value and another for a part of it, two bindings on both sides of an <code>and</code>, or two bindings of the same field in one property pattern (<code>{ items: let x, items: let y }</code>, at any nesting) would own the same storage twice, and a unique value may have only one owner (L006).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Bind the whole value or its parts, not both: <code>case let x:</code> or <code>case Ledger { items: let it }:</code>, and each field once. Bindings over disjoint parts, such as <code>{ items: let a, other: let b }</code> or <code>.pair(let a, let b)</code>, are accepted. Without <code>move</code>, bindings borrow and any of these patterns is fine.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// A pattern in `switch move …` may not bind the same storage twice (BYN2790).
struct Ledger {
    List&lt;i64&gt; items
}

union Box {
    full(Ledger ledger)
    empty
}

void keep(move List&lt;i64&gt; xs) { }

void inspect() {
    let a = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    switch move a {
        case let x and let y:
            keep(move x)
        default:
    }
    let l = Ledger { items: List&lt;i64&gt;() }
    switch move l {
        case Ledger { items: let it } whole:
            keep(move it)
        default:
    }
    let m = Ledger { items: List&lt;i64&gt;() }
    switch move m {
        case { items: let p, items: let q }:
            keep(move p)
            keep(move q)
        default:
    }
    let b = Box.full(Ledger { items: List&lt;i64&gt;() })
    switch move b {
        case .full({ items: let p, items: let q }):
            keep(move p)
            keep(move q)
        default:
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2790:</span> pattern `let x and let y` binds the same storage more than once in `switch move …`
  --&gt; main.byn:16:14

  16 │         case let x and let y:
     │              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: bind the whole value or its parts, not both</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2790</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2791">BYN2791: binding is mutated in the guard of <code>switch move …</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> In <code>switch move x</code> each pattern binding owns the part it binds, but only once its arm is selected. A <code>when</code> guard runs before that: when it fails, the value goes back to the switch and a later arm binds the same storage. A guard that changed the binding (grew a list, took a field, passed it by <code>ref</code> or <code>out</code>, moved it) would leave that later arm with a stale or freed copy. So inside the guard the arm's bindings and their parts are read-only.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Read the binding in the guard and change it in the arm's body: <code>case .full(var x) when x.items.length &gt; 49: x.grow(); …</code>. Without <code>move</code>, bindings borrow and this rule does not apply.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Bag {
    List&lt;i64&gt; items

    public i64 grow(ref this) uses alloc {
        this.items.append(1)
        return this.items.length
    }
}

union Slot {
    full(Bag b)
    empty
}

i64 pick&lt;T&gt;(move Slot s, T v) uses alloc, console {
    switch move s {
        case .full(var x) when x.grow() &gt; 5:
            print("call")
        default:
    }
    return 0
}

void usePick() uses alloc, console {
    let _ = pick(Slot.empty, 1)
}

void take(u8 x) {}

void rebound() {
    let k = Bag { items: List&lt;i64&gt;() }
    var v = 5
    let _ = k.grow()
    take(v)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2791:</span> `x` is mutated in the guard of `switch move …`; a guard that fails gives the value
back
  --&gt; main.byn:17:32

  17 │         case .full(var x) when x.grow() &gt; 5:
     │                                ^ `grow` modifies it
     │
  help: read it in the guard and change it in the arm's body</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2791</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g28">Interfaces and generics (BYN28xx)</h2>
<p>Interface declarations, conformance and generic type parameters.</p>
<h3 id="byn2800">BYN2800: interface name must start with <code>I</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An <code>interface</code> is named without the <code>I</code> prefix, or the <code>I</code> is not followed by an uppercase letter.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An interface is not a type on its own: it is listed after <code>:</code>, named in a <code>where</code> bound or used as <code>dyn ref I</code>. The <code>I</code> prefix tells the reader which names are interfaces at every one of those places (spec §5.1).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename it to <code>I</code> followed by the PascalCase name (the machine fix does):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    f32 area()
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface Shape {
    f32 area()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2800:</span> `Shape` must start with `I` and an uppercase letter
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ interface Shape {
    │           ^^^^^ an interface name
    │
  help: rename it to `IShape`
    │ 1 │ interface IShape {
    │   │           +</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2800</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2801">BYN2801: missing interface member</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct lists an interface after <code>:</code> but does not provide one of the interface's members that has no default body. A field can provide a getter (<code>i64 count { get; }</code>) but not a method: a field named like a required method is reported here.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Code written against the interface may call any of its members on the struct; a member without a body has nothing to run unless the struct provides it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the member with the signature the interface declares:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Square : IShape {
    i64 side

    i64 area() =&gt; side * side
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

struct Square : IShape {
    i64 side
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2801:</span> `Square` does not provide `area` required by `IShape`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:17

  5 │ struct Square : IShape {
    │                 ^^^^^^ needs `area`
    │
  help: add `i64 area()` to `Square`
    │ 7 │     i64 area() =&gt; todo("area")
    │   │ ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
    │ 8 │ }</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2801</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2802">BYN2802: interface member does not match</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct provides a member an interface requires, but its signature differs: the return type, a parameter's type or modifier, the kind (method or property), <code>static</code>, or the <code>fails</code> clause. A field standing for a getter must have the getter's type. It is also reported when a default from one base would stand for a member of the same name in another base: add the member to the struct to choose.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Callers use the interface's signature. A witness that returns another type, takes other parameters or fails differently cannot stand in for it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Change the member's signature to the interface's:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Digits : IParser {
    i64 parse(string text) fails ParseError =&gt; 0
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface ICount {
    i64 count
}

struct Box : ICount {
    i32 count
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2802:</span> `Box.count` is `i32 count` but `ICount` requires `i64 count { get }`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:9

  6 │     i32 count
    │         ^^^^^ does not match `ICount`
    │
  help: change the field's type to `i64`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2802</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2803">BYN2803: missing associated type</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An interface declares <code>type Item;</code>, and a struct listing it does not bind <code>type Item = …;</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The interface's members mention <code>Item</code>; without a binding the struct's members cannot be checked against them, and generic code cannot know what <code>T.Item</code> is.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Bind the associated type to the type the members use:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Digits : IIndexable {
    type Item = i64

    i64 count =&gt; 3

    i64 get(i64 index) =&gt; index * 10
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Digits : IIndexable {
    i64 count =&gt; 3

    i64 get(i64 index) =&gt; index * 10
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2803:</span> `Digits` does not bind `type Item` of `IIndexable`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:17

  1 │ struct Digits : IIndexable {
    │                 ^^^^^^^^^^ needs `type Item`
    │
  help: add `type Item = …` to `Digits`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2803</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2804">BYN2804: not an interface</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct's base list (after <code>:</code>) or a <code>where</code> bound names a type that is not an <code>interface</code>: a struct, union, enum or other type.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only interfaces can be conformed to or used as constraints. There is no struct inheritance; a struct that needs another struct's data holds it as a field.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> List an interface, or hold the other type as a field:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Circle {
    Point center
    i64 r
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Point {
    i64 x
}

struct Circle : Point {
    i64 r
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2804:</span> `Point` is not an interface
  --&gt; main.byn:5:17

  5 │ struct Circle : Point {
    │                 ^^^^^ a struct
    │
  help: list an `interface` here</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2804</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2805">BYN2805: interface listed twice</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct lists the same interface more than once after <code>:</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A struct conforms to an interface once; the second entry adds nothing and usually means another interface was meant.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the second entry, or replace it with the interface that was meant:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Square : IShape {
    i64 side

    i64 area() =&gt; side * side
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

struct Square : IShape, IShape {
    i64 side

    i64 area() =&gt; side * side
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2805:</span> `Square` lists `IShape` twice
  --&gt; main.byn:5:25

  5 │ struct Square : IShape, IShape {
    │                         ^^^^^^ already listed
    │
  help: remove the second `IShape`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2805</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2806">BYN2806: member not found in the constraints</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> Generic code uses a member on a type parameter, and no interface in the parameter's <code>where</code> clause declares it. Inside a default method, <code>this</code> stands for the interface's <code>Self</code>, whose only constraint is that interface.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Generic code is checked once, at its definition, not separately for each type it is called with. It can rely only on what its constraints promise. Otherwise a call with a new type could break code that has no error of its own.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the member in a constraining interface, or add an interface that declares it to the <code>where</code> clause:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
    i64 perimeter()
}

void show&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where T : IShape {
    print($"{shape.perimeter()}")
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

void show&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where T : IShape {
    print($"{shape.perimeter()}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2806:</span> `T` has no member `perimeter` in its constraints (`IShape`)
  --&gt; main.byn:6:20

  6 │     print($"{shape.perimeter()}")
    │                    ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: add an interface declaring `perimeter` to the `where` clause</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2806</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2807">BYN2807: type argument does not conform</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A call or a type names a type argument that does not meet its parameter's <code>where</code> constraint. The error is reported where the type is chosen: at the call, or at a generic's own call when it passes one of its type parameters along.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The generic code was checked against its constraints, so it may call any member they declare. A type that does not conform has no such member to call. Only structs conform to interfaces, and a type parameter conforms only to the interfaces in its own <code>where</code> clause.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Conform the type to the interface, or pass one that does. For a type parameter, add the interface to its <code>where</code> clause:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Square : IShape {
    i64 side

    i64 area() =&gt; side * side
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

struct Circle : IShape {
    i64 r

    i64 area() =&gt; 3 * r * r
}

struct Square {
    i64 side

    i64 area() =&gt; side * side
}

struct Box&lt;T&gt; where T : IShape {
    T item
}

i64 measure&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where T : IShape =&gt; shape.area()

i64 twice&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where T : IShape =&gt; measure(ref shape) * 2

i64 loose&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) =&gt; measure(ref shape)

void main() {
    var c = Circle { r: 1 }
    var s = Square { side: 2 }
    print($"{measure(ref c)} {measure(ref s)}")
    print($"{twice(ref s)}")
    var n = 4
    print($"{measure(ref n)}")
    let b: Box&lt;Square&gt; = Box&lt;Square&gt; { item: s }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2807:</span> `T` does not conform to `IShape`: missing `area`
  --&gt; main.byn:25:30

  25 │ i64 loose&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) =&gt; measure(ref shape)
     │                              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the generic code needs this of its type
     │                                                 arguments
     │
  help: add `where T : IShape` to the declaration of `T`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2807</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2808">BYN2808: interface cannot be used with <code>dyn</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>dyn ref I</code> names an interface that has a generic method or an associated type (<code>type Item;</code>). The diagnostic names the first such member.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>dyn ref</code> call goes through a table with one entry per member, built once for each conforming type. A generic method has no single entry (each type argument is a different function), and an associated type has no single type for callers to use: both depend on information only a constraint provides (spec 006, handoff Q93).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use a <code>where</code> constraint, which keeps the conforming type known at compile time:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void walk&lt;T&gt;(ref T c) where T : ICursor { }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface ICursor {
    type Item
    Item peek()
}

void walk(dyn ref ICursor c) { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2808:</span> `ICursor` cannot be used with `dyn`: `Item` is an associated type
  --&gt; main.byn:6:11

  6 │ void walk(dyn ref ICursor c) { }
    │           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ used with `dyn` here
    │
  help: use a `where` constraint instead</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2808</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2809">BYN2809: <code>dyn I</code> by value is not supported yet</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A type is written <code>dyn I</code> without <code>ref</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An owned dynamic value would need its size at run time and an allocation to hold it. Only the borrowed form <code>dyn ref I</code> is supported: it points at a value that lives elsewhere and never allocates (spec 006, non-goals).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Borrow the value with <code>dyn ref</code> (or <code>dyn ref readonly</code> when only members without <code>ref this</code> are called):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show(dyn ref IShape s) { }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    f32 area()
}

void show(dyn IShape s) { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2809:</span> `dyn IShape` is not supported yet
  --&gt; main.byn:5:11

  5 │ void show(dyn IShape s) { }
    │           ^^^^^^^^^^ an owned dynamic value
    │
  help: write `dyn ref IShape`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2809</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2810">BYN2810: <code>static</code> interface member</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An interface member is declared <code>static</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>static</code> interface member (<code>static T parse(string text)</code>, called as <code>T.parse(…)</code> through a constraint) is not supported yet: no part of the compiler port needs it, so it is left out of the first interface subset (spec 006).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>static</code> and call the member on a value, or make it a free function:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 parse(string text) {
    // …
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IParse {
    static i64 parse(string text)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2810:</span> `static` interface members are not supported yet
  --&gt; main.byn:2:16

  2 │     static i64 parse(string text)
    │                ^^^^^ declared `static`
    │
  help: remove `static` or make it a free function</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2810</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2811">BYN2811: <code>extension</code> conformance</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An <code>extension T : I</code> declaration adds a conformance to a type after the type is declared.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Retroactive conformance is not supported yet. A type's conformances are listed on its own declaration, so they are all in one place and a conformance cannot appear or disappear depending on which module is imported.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> List the interface on the type's declaration and move the members into it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Money : IShape {
    i64 cents

    i64 area() =&gt; 0
}</code></pre>
<p>If the type is not yours to change, wrap it in a struct that conforms.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Money {
    i64 cents
}

extension Money : IShape {
    i64 area() =&gt; 0
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2811:</span> `extension Money : IShape` is not supported yet
  --&gt; main.byn:5:1

  5 │ extension Money : IShape {
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ retroactive conformance
    │
  help: list `IShape` on `Money`'s declaration</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2811</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2812">BYN2812: <code>where</code> names an unknown type parameter</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>where</code> clause constrains a name that is not one of the declaration's own type parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>where T : I</code> clause restricts the types that can be passed for <code>T</code>, so <code>T</code> must be declared in the <code>&lt;…&gt;</code> of the same function, struct, union or interface. A misspelled parameter would otherwise leave the real one unconstrained.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name one of the declaration's type parameters, or remove the clause if there are none:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where T : IShape { }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where U : IShape { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2812:</span> `where` names `U`, which is not a type parameter
  --&gt; main.byn:1:33

  1 │ void show&lt;T&gt;(ref T shape) where U : IShape { }
    │                                 ^ not a type parameter of `show`
    │
  help: name one of `T`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2812</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2813">BYN2813: <code>ref this</code> member through <code>dyn ref readonly</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A member that takes <code>ref this</code> is called through a <code>dyn ref readonly</code> value. This includes default methods declared <code>ref this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>ref this</code> means the call may modify the value. A <code>dyn ref readonly</code> borrow promises that the value is not modified through it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Take a writable <code>dyn ref</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show(dyn ref ICounter c) {
    print($"{c.next()}")
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface ICounter {
    i64 next(ref this)
}

void show(dyn ref readonly ICounter c) {
    print($"{c.next()}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2813:</span> `next` takes `ref this`, but the receiver is `dyn ref readonly ICounter`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:16

  6 │     print($"{c.next()}")
    │                ^^^^ declared `ref this`
    │
  help: take `dyn ref ICounter`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2813</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2814">BYN2814: <code>ref this</code> does not match the interface</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct's member and the interface member it provides disagree on <code>ref this</code>: one takes it and the other does not.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>ref this</code> decides whether a call may modify the value it is called on. Callers through the interface rely on the interface's choice: a read-only member must not change the value, and a <code>ref this</code> member needs a mutable one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add or remove <code>ref this</code> to match the interface:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter : ICounter {
    i64 value

    i64 next(ref this) {
        value += 1
        return value
    }

    i64 peek() =&gt; value
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface ICounter {
    i64 next(ref this)
    i64 peek()
}

struct Counter : ICounter {
    i64 value

    i64 next() =&gt; value

    i64 peek(ref this) =&gt; value
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2814:</span> `Counter.next` must match `ICounter.next`, which takes `ref this`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:9

  9 │     i64 next() =&gt; value
    │         ^^^^ not declared `ref this`
    │
  help: add `ref this` on `Counter.next`
    │ 9 │     i64 next(ref this) =&gt; value
    │   │              ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2814</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2815">BYN2815: only structs can conform</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A union or an enum lists interfaces after <code>:</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Conformance is for structs only for now. Union and enum members are not in the language yet, so a union or enum has nowhere to provide an interface's members (spec 006, handoff Q100).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the union or enum in a struct that conforms, and switch on it inside the members:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union Shape {
    circle(i64 r),
    none,
}

struct AnyShape : IShape {
    Shape shape

    i64 area() =&gt; shape switch {
        .circle(let r) =&gt; 3 * r * r,
        .none =&gt; 0,
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    i64 area()
}

union Shape : IShape {
    circle(i64 r)
    none
}

enum Color : IShape {
    red
    green
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2815:</span> `Shape` is a union; only structs can conform yet
  --&gt; main.byn:5:15

  5 │ union Shape : IShape {
    │               ^^^^^^ listed here
    │
  help: wrap it in a struct that conforms</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2815</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2816">BYN2816: an interface is not a type</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> An interface name is used as a type on its own: a parameter, local, field, return or type argument.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An interface describes what a type provides; it has no size or layout of its own, so there is no value of type <code>IShape</code>. An interface may appear in a base list (<code>struct Circle : IShape</code>), in a <code>where</code> bound, or after <code>dyn ref</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Borrow any conforming value with <code>dyn ref</code>, or make the function generic with a constraint:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void show(dyn ref IShape s) { }

void draw&lt;T&gt;(ref T s) where T : IShape { }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IShape {
    f32 area()
}

void show(IShape s) { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2816:</span> `IShape` is an interface, not a type
  --&gt; main.byn:5:11

  5 │ void show(IShape s) { }
    │           ^^^^^^ an interface
    │
  help: write `dyn ref IShape` or add a `where` constraint</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2816</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2820">BYN2820: lambda parameter count</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A lambda takes its parameter types from the function type it is passed or assigned to, so it must name exactly as many parameters as that type has.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name one parameter per parameter of the function type:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let sum = apply((a, b) =&gt; a + b)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 apply((i64, i64) -&gt; i64 f) =&gt; f(1, 2)

void main() {
    let sum = apply(x =&gt; x)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2820:</span> this lambda takes 1 parameter(s) but `(i64, i64) -&gt; i64` takes 2
  --&gt; main.byn:4:21

  4 │     let sum = apply(x =&gt; x)
    │                     ^^^^^^
    │
  help: write 2 parameter name(s): `(a, b) =&gt; …`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2820</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2821">BYN2821: value a lambda cannot capture</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A lambda copies what it captures when it is made, so it captures only values that cannot change under it: <code>let</code> locals, <code>let</code> pattern bindings and plain parameters. A <code>var</code> could be reassigned after the copy, a <code>ref</code>, <code>mut</code> or <code>move</code> parameter is a borrow of the caller's storage, and <code>this</code> (with the fields read through it) is not captured at all.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Copy the value into a <code>let</code> first and capture that:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let current = step
let a = apply(x =&gt; x + current)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 apply((i64 -&gt; i64) f) =&gt; f(1)

void main() {
    var step = 2
    step = 3
    let a = apply(x =&gt; x + step)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2821:</span> `step` cannot be captured: it is a `var`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:28

  6 │     let a = apply(x =&gt; x + step)
    │                            ^^^^
    │
  help: copy it into a `let` first</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2821</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2822">BYN2822: function type not supported here</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function value borrows the frame that made it, so in 007 it lives only where that frame outlives it: a parameter, a local, or the target of a <code>type</code> alias naming one of those. A struct field, a return type, a type argument, or the element of an optional, span, list, map, array or tuple would carry it out of that frame.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Take the function as a parameter of the function that calls it: <code>Money total(Line line, Pricer pricer)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 inc(i64 n) =&gt; n + 1

void main() {
    let f: i64 -&gt; i64 = inc
    let xs = [f, f]
    let k = 10
    let g: i64 -&gt; i64 = n =&gt; n + k
    let ys = [g]
    let t = (1, g)
    let m = ["a": f]
    let u: (i64, i64 -&gt; i64) = (1, f)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2822:</span> a function type is not supported as an array element yet
  --&gt; main.byn:5:15

  5 │     let xs = [f, f]
    │               ^
    │
  help: take the function as a parameter instead</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2822</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2823">BYN2823: member where a lambda is expected</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A leading <code>.name</code> picks a member of the expected type, such as an enum member or an error variant. A function type has no such members, and <code>.name</code> never stands for a lambda that reads the member of its parameter.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Read the member through <code>it</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let text = label(user, by: it.name)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct User {
    string name
}

string label(User u, by (User -&gt; string) key) =&gt; key(u)

void main() {
    let user = User { name: "a" }
    let text = label(user, by: .name)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2823:</span> `.name` is a member, not a lambda
  --&gt; main.byn:9:32

  9 │     let text = label(user, by: .name)
    │                                ^^^^^
    │
  help: write `it.name`
    │ 9 │     let text = label(user, by: it.name)
    │   │                                ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2823</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2824">BYN2824: not a function value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only a module-level, non-generic function is a value of a function type, and only when its signature matches the type exactly. A built-in, a generic function, a method (static or instance), a constructor and a union variant are not values: a built-in has no single signature, a generic function has none until its type arguments are known, a method needs its receiver, and a constructor or variant builds a value rather than being one.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap it in a lambda that calls it: <code>measure(() =&gt; o.doubled())</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 total

    i64 doubled() =&gt; total * 2
}

i64 measure((() -&gt; i64) f) =&gt; f()

i64 twice(Order o) =&gt; measure(o.doubled)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2824:</span> `o.doubled` cannot be used as a value: it is a method
  --&gt; main.byn:9:31

  9 │ i64 twice(Order o) =&gt; measure(o.doubled)
    │                               ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: wrap it in a lambda: `() =&gt; o.doubled()`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2824</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2830">BYN2830: <code>HashMap</code> is not supported yet</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Bayan has one hash map, <code>Map&lt;K, V&gt;</code>. It hashes its keys like an unordered map would, and it also keeps insertion order, so iteration is the same on every run and every build. An unordered <code>HashMap</code> would iterate in an order that depends on the hash seed, which deterministic output cannot tolerate. <code>HashMap</code> is reported once and then read as <code>Map</code>, so nothing else is reported for it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>Map</code> with the same type arguments: <code>Map&lt;string, i32&gt; counts</code>. The fix does this; <code>bayan format</code> does not apply it, since the type checker reports it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Counter {
    HashMap&lt;string, i32&gt; counts
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2830:</span> `HashMap` is not supported yet: `Map` hashes the same way and keeps insertion order
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     HashMap&lt;string, i32&gt; counts
    │     ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write `Map&lt;string, i32&gt;`
    │ 2 │     HashMap&lt;string, i32&gt; counts
    │   │     ----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2830</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2831">BYN2831: a part of a <code>Map</code> key is not a key</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A tuple or struct is a <code>Map</code> key when every element or field is one: an integer type, <code>rune</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>Symbol</code>, a plain enum, or a tuple or struct of those. The map hashes and compares a composite key part by part, so one part that cannot be hashed (a float, an optional, an enum with data, a collection, a <code>@noEquality</code> struct) rules out the whole key. The diagnostic names the first such part and its type. In generic code it is reported where the type argument is chosen.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Key by a field that identifies the value, such as an integer id or a <code>Symbol</code> name, or change the part to a key type.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Weight {
    f64 grams
}

let byWeight = Map&lt;Weight, string&gt;()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2831:</span> `Weight` cannot be a `Map` key: field `grams` is a `f64`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:20

  5 │ let byWeight = Map&lt;Weight, string&gt;()
    │                    ^^^^^^
    │
  help: use a field that identifies the value as the key, such as an id or a `Symbol`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2831</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn2832">BYN2832: a <code>string</code> value is not a <code>Symbol</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Only a string literal becomes a <code>Symbol</code> by itself: its text is known when the program is compiled, so it is an entry of the program's literal table. Any other <code>string</code> (a local, a parameter, a slice, an interpolated string, a call result) is known only at run time, and turning it into a <code>Symbol</code> interns it, which allocates. Bayan keeps that visible.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Intern it: <code>let s: Symbol = Symbol.intern(name)</code>. A function that calls it declares <code>uses alloc</code>; <code>main</code> needs none.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let name: string = "total"
    let s: Symbol = name
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2832:</span> a `string` value is not a `Symbol`; only a string literal converts
  --&gt; main.byn:3:21

  3 │     let s: Symbol = name
    │                     ^^^^
    │
  help: convert it with `Symbol.intern(name)`
    │ 3 │     let s: Symbol = Symbol.intern(name)
    │   │                     +++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2832</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g30">Memory and borrowing (BYN30xx)</h2>
<p>Where a value lives and how long a borrow of it may last.</p>
<h3 id="byn3001">BYN3001: borrowed value in a struct field</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>string</code>, <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>StrBuf</code> points into memory owned elsewhere. Storing it in a struct would let the struct outlive that memory; v0.1 has no way to check that, so it is not allowed.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Storing borrows in structs arrives in a later version; copy the data instead (for example into a <code>List&lt;u8&gt;</code>), or store an index.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Window {
    Span&lt;u8&gt; bytes
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3001:</span> a struct field cannot hold a borrowed `Span&lt;u8&gt;`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     Span&lt;u8&gt; bytes
    │     ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: store a `List` copied with `.copy(to: alloc)` instead; storing borrows in structs arrives in
        a later version</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3002">BYN3002: borrow outlives its source</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>string</code>, <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> that points into a stack buffer, a local array or an arena is only valid until that storage is freed at the end of its block. Storing it in a variable (or list) declared in an outer block would leave the variable pointing at freed memory.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Move the storage out to the block where the value is used, or copy the data with <code>.copy(to: alloc)</code> into an allocator that lives long enough.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">var view = ""
{
    let bytes = stackalloc u8[16]
    var sb = StrBuf(bytes)
    sb.append("scratch")
    view = sb.finish()
}
print(view)</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3002:</span> the `stackalloc` buffer on line 3 does not live as long as `view`, which borrows it
  --&gt; main.byn:6:12

  6 │     view = sb.finish()
    │            ^^^^^^^^^^^ the `stackalloc` buffer on line 3 is freed at the end of its block
    │
  help: copy the data with `.copy(to: alloc)` into an allocator that lives long enough</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3003">BYN3003: returning a borrow of local storage</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function's locals, by-value parameters and <code>stackalloc</code> buffers are freed when it returns, so a span or string pointing into them would dangle. A returned borrow may only point into what the caller passed in (spec §9.4 rule 4).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Let the caller pass the buffer (a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> parameter) or an allocator in, and borrow or allocate from that.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Span&lt;i64&gt; scratch() {
    let buf = stackalloc i64[4]
    return buf[1..]
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3003:</span> cannot return a borrow of the `stackalloc` buffer on line 2, which is freed when
`scratch` returns
  --&gt; main.byn:3:12

  3 │     return buf[1..]
    │            ^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: let the caller pass the buffer or allocator in, and borrow or allocate from that</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3004">BYN3004: modified while borrowed</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Adding to or clearing a <code>List</code> can move its elements, clearing a <code>StrBuf</code> overwrites its text, and resetting an <code>Arena</code> frees everything allocated from it. A span, string or loop that still points into the old storage would read freed or changed memory (spec §9.4 rules 5 and 6).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Finish using the borrow before the change, or copy what it needs first. To grow a list you are iterating over, collect the new items in another list and add them after the loop.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    var sb = StrBuf()
    sb.append("a")
    let s = sb.finish()
    sb.clear()
    print(s)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3004:</span> `sb` is modified while `s` still borrows it
  --&gt; main.byn:5:5

  5 │     sb.clear()
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^ `s` is used again on line 6
    │
  help: finish using `s` first, or copy what it needs before changing `sb`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3005">BYN3005: <code>ref</code> and another use in one call</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>ref</code> argument may be changed by the callee while the callee also reads the same value through another argument, for example a list it is growing and a span over that list (spec §9.4 rule 5).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Copy the value into a local first and pass the copy, or split the work into two calls.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void grow(ref List&lt;i64&gt; items, from ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; source) {
    items.append(source.length)
}

void main() {
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    grow(items: ref xs, from: xs)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3005:</span> `xs` is passed by `ref` and again in the same call
  --&gt; main.byn:7:31

  7 │     grow(items: ref xs, from: xs)
    │                               ^^ `xs` is also passed by `ref`
    │
  help: copy the value into a local first, or make two calls</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3005</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3006">BYN3006: moved while borrowed</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Moving a value (<code>move s</code>, a <code>move</code> argument, <code>return move s</code>, <code>switch move s</code>, or <code>.take()</code> on a <code>List</code>, <code>Map</code>, <code>StrBuf</code> or optional) hands its storage to a new owner, which may grow, change or free it. A span or <code>dyn ref</code> that still points into the moved value would read freed or changed memory (spec §9.4 rules 5 and 6).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Finish using the borrow before the move, or copy what it needs first.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">// While finish() or view still lends a StrBuf's text, insert and take are refused (Q745).
void insertAfterFinish() {
    var sb = StrBuf()
    sb.append("a")
    let s = sb.finish()
    sb.insert("b", at: 0)
    print(s)
}

void takeAfterView() {
    var sb = StrBuf()
    sb.append("a")
    let s = sb.view
    let t = sb.take()
    print(s)
}

void main() {
    insertAfterFinish()
    takeAfterView()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3006:</span> `sb` is moved while `s` still borrows it
  --&gt; main.byn:14:13

  14 │     let t = sb.take()
     │             ^^^^^^^^^ `s` is used again on line 15
     │
  help: finish using `s` first, or copy what it needs before moving `sb`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g31">Manifests (BYN31xx)</h2>
<p>Problems in <code>package.byn</code>.</p>
<h3 id="byn3100">BYN3100: <code>package.byn</code> is data only</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The manifest is read by tools that never run Bayan code, so it holds values only: strings, integers, <code>.names</code>, <code>[lists]</code> and <code>Type { field: value }</code>. Calls, operators, <code>comptime</code> and statements are not allowed.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the value out: <code>name = "local/tally"</code>.</p>

<h3 id="byn3101">BYN3101: unknown manifest field</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>package.byn</code> is checked against the <code>Package</code> type (and <code>Dist</code> for <code>dist: Dist { … }</code>). A misspelled field would otherwise be ignored silently.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of the listed fields, or remove the line.</p>

<h3 id="byn3102">BYN3102: invalid manifest value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Each manifest field has a fixed shape: <code>name</code> is <code>namespace/name</code> in lowercase words joined by <code>-</code>, <code>version</code> is a semantic version, <code>edition</code> is <code>2027</code>, <code>kind</code> is <code>.app</code> or <code>.lib</code>, <code>dist.targets</code> is a list like <code>[.host]</code> and <code>dist.mode</code> is <code>.debug</code>, <code>.safe</code> or <code>.fast</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> <code>name = "local/tally"</code>, <code>version = "1.0.0"</code>.</p>

<h3 id="byn3103">BYN3103: missing manifest field</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every package needs a <code>name</code> and a <code>version</code>: outputs, <code>dist/</code> directories and provenance records are named after them.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>version = "0.1.0"</code>.</p>

<h3 id="byn3104">BYN3104: <code>=</code> in <code>package.byn</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>field = value</code> in <code>package.byn</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The manifest is Bayan data, and Bayan literals name their fields with <code>:</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>:</code> (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Package {
    name: "local/shop",
}</code></pre>

<h2 id="g32">Values, units and overloads (BYN32xx)</h2>
<p>Unused values, distinct types and overloads that cannot be told apart.</p>
<h3 id="byn3201">BYN3201: an unused value</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A statement ends at the newline (spec §2.5), so a line split in the wrong place can leave a value on a line of its own: <code>let x = a</code> followed by <code>-b</code> is two statements, and <code>-b</code> does nothing. Making an unused non-<code>void</code> value an error turns every such mis-split line into a diagnostic.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Join the lines, assign the value, or remove the expression:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void main() {
    let a = 1
    let x = a - 2
    print(x)
}</code></pre>
<p>There is no automatic fix: whether the value belongs to the line above or is not needed is a choice.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 one() =&gt; 1

void main() {
    one()
    let _ = one()
    var xs = List&lt;i64&gt;()
    xs.append(1)
    xs.removeLast()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3201:</span> the value of this `one()` is unused
  --&gt; main.byn:4:5

  4 │     one()
    │     ^^^^^ value dropped
    │
  help: assign it with `let _ = …`, or remove the expression</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3201</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3202">BYN3202: duplicate error case in a composition</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A composed <code>union error</code> is flattened: <code>union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code> has every case of both sets. When two of them have a case with the same name, <code>.rejected</code> in a <code>fails ShipError</code> function or a <code>catch switch</code> arm could mean either.</p>
<p>Different instantiations of one generic set are not a clash: <code>union error FormError = ValidationError&lt;Signup&gt;, ValidationError&lt;Address&gt;</code> is valid, and a <code>catch switch</code> matches them by type (<code>ValidationError&lt;Signup&gt; e =&gt; …</code>). The message names each set with its type arguments: `<code> </code>ValidationError&lt;Signup&gt;<code> and </code>SignupError<code> both have a case </code>invalid<code> </code>`.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename one of the cases (<code>carrierRejected</code>), or compose fewer sets.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error CarrierError {
    timeout
    rejected
}
union error StockError {
    outOfStock
    rejected
}
union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3202:</span> `CarrierError` and `StockError` both have a case `rejected`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:1

  9 │ union error ShipError = CarrierError, StockError
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^ two cases `rejected`
    │
  help: rename one case, or compose fewer sets</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3202</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3204">BYN3204: bare literal for a distinct type</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>{0}</code> is not a <code>{1}</code>"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "wrap it: <code>{1}({0})</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A bare literal is used where a distinct type is expected.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A distinct type is a new type, not an alias: its <code>requires</code> are checked when a value is built with <code>T(raw)</code>, and a bare literal would skip that check.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the literal (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    let i: Id = Id(7)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Id = i64 requires it &gt; 0

void main() {
    let i: Id = 7
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3204:</span> `7` is not a `Id`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:17

  4 │     let i: Id = 7
    │                 ^
    │
  help: wrap it: `Id(7)`
    │ 4 │     let i: Id = Id(7)
    │   │                 +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3204</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3206">BYN3206: rule target is not a field</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>at .{0}</code> names no field of <code>{1}</code>"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "name a field of <code>{1}</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct-level <code>requires … at .name</code> names something that is not a field of the struct.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>at .field</code> says which field a failed rule is reported against; it must name a field the struct declares.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name a field of the struct:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    requires low &lt;= high at .high</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Range {
    i64 low
    i64 high
    requires low &lt;= high at .top
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3206:</span> `at .top` names no field of `Range`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:30

  4 │     requires low &lt;= high at .top
    │                              ^^^ `.top`
    │
  help: name a field of `Range`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3206</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3207">BYN3207: literal of a checked struct needs <code>try</code></h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "a literal of <code>{0}</code> can fail its <code>requires</code>; write <code>try {0} { … }</code>"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "add <code>try</code> before the literal and handle the <code>ValidationError&lt;Order&gt;</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A struct literal builds a struct whose <code>requires</code> the compiler cannot decide, and nothing handles the failure.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A struct's <code>requires</code> are checked when the value is built. A literal that may break them fails with <code>ValidationError</code>, which must be propagated or caught like any other error.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>try</code> (the machine fix does this), or <code>catch</code> the error:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Order order(i64 n) fails ValidationError&lt;Order&gt; =&gt; try Order { quantity: n }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 quantity requires it &gt; 0
}

Order order(i64 n) fails ValidationError&lt;Order&gt; =&gt; Order { quantity: n }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3207:</span> a literal of `Order` can fail its `requires`; write `try Order { … }`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:52

  5 │ Order order(i64 n) fails ValidationError&lt;Order&gt; =&gt; Order { quantity: n }
    │                                                    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: add `try` before the literal and handle the `ValidationError&lt;Order&gt;`
    │ 5 │ Order order(i64 n) fails ValidationError&lt;Order&gt; =&gt; try Order { quantity: n }
    │   │                                                    ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3207</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3208">BYN3208: field of a checked struct assigned one at a time</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "fields of <code>{0}</code> cannot be assigned one at a time; its <code>requires</code> are checked at construction"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "build a new value with <code>with</code>: <code>try x with { f: v }</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A method of a struct with <code>requires</code> assigns one of its fields.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The rules are checked when the value is built. Writing a field afterwards could break them unseen, so a checked struct changes only by building a new value.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Build a new value with <code>with</code>, which checks the rules again:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    Range widen() fails ValidationError&lt;Range&gt; =&gt; try this with { high: this.high + 1 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Range {
    i64 low
    i64 high
    requires low &lt;= high at .high

    void widen(ref this) {
        this.high = this.high + 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3208:</span> fields of `Range` cannot be assigned one at a time; its `requires` are checked at
construction
  --&gt; main.byn:7:9

  7 │         this.high = this.high + 1
    │         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: build a new value with `with`: `try x with { f: v }`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3208</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3209">BYN3209: <code>unique</code> value declared <code>const</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>const</code> is a compile-time value that every use copies, while a <code>unique</code> type has exactly one owner and is never copied (spec §3.11). A <code>struct unique</code>, or a struct holding one, therefore cannot be <code>const</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare it with <code>let</code> (the machine fix does this):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    let f = File { fd: 1 }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct unique File {
    i64 fd
}
void main() {
    const f = File { fd: 1 }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3209:</span> a `unique` type cannot be `const`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:5

  5 │     const f = File { fd: 1 }
    │     ^^^^^ `File` is `unique`
    │
  help: declare it with `let`
    │ 5 │     let f = File { fd: 1 }
    │   │     ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3209</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3214">BYN3214: <code>rest</code> parameter stored</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>{0}</code> is borrowed for the call and cannot be stored"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "copy it first: <code>{0}.copy()</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>rest</code> parameter is returned, or stored somewhere that outlives the call: a <code>ref</code> or <code>out</code> parameter, or a field of <code>this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The values of a <code>rest</code> tail live in the caller's stack frame for the one call, so passing them allocates nothing. Once the call returns, the slots are reused, and a stored span would point at whatever comes next.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Copy the values into a list that owns them. In v0.1, <code>copy</code> takes the allocator to copy into:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">List&lt;i64&gt; keep(ref Arena arena, rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; xs) {
    return xs.copy(to: arena)
}</code></pre>
<p>Reading <code>xs</code> and binding it to a local inside the function are fine. The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; keep(rest ReadOnlySpan&lt;i64&gt; xs) {
    return xs
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3214:</span> `xs` is borrowed for the call and cannot be stored
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │     return xs
    │            ^^
    │
  help: copy it first: `xs.copy()`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3214</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3216">BYN3216: field written from outside its struct</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>{0}.{1}</code> can be written only by <code>{0}</code>'s own methods or with <code>with</code>: <code>{0}</code> {2}"</p>
<p><code>{2}</code> is "has <code>requires</code>", "has states" or "is <code>@sealed</code>".</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "add a <code>ref this</code> method to <code>{0}</code>, or build a new value with <code>with</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> Code outside a struct assigns one of its fields, and the struct has a <code>requires</code> rule, a <code>states</code> block or <code>@sealed</code>. Plain data structs allow direct field writes everywhere.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Such a struct owns its fields: only its own methods change them, so it can keep its rules and states, and a <code>@sealed</code> struct can change its representation without breaking callers. Other code builds a new value instead; for a struct with <code>requires</code>, <code>with</code> checks the rules again.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Build a new value with <code>with</code>, or move the write into a <code>ref this</code> method of <code>HttpClientOptions</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void tune(ref HttpClientOptions options) {
    options = options with { retries: 5 }
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@sealed
public struct HttpClientOptions {
    i64 timeout
    i32 retries
}

void tune(ref HttpClientOptions options) {
    options.retries = 5
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3216:</span> `HttpClientOptions.retries` can be written only by `HttpClientOptions`'s own methods
or with `with`: `HttpClientOptions` is `@sealed`
  --&gt; main.byn:8:5

  8 │     options.retries = 5
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: add a `ref this` method to `HttpClientOptions`, or build a new value with `with`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3216</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3218">BYN3218: rule under a field reads other fields</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "this <code>requires</code> reads <code>it</code> and other fields"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "split it: an <code>it</code> rule under the field, and a struct-level rule with <code>at .field</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>requires</code> written under a field reads both <code>it</code> and another field of the struct.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A rule under a field checks that field alone. A rule that relates several fields is a struct-level rule, and <code>at .field</code> says which field a failure is reported against.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Split the rule:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Range {
    i64 low
    i64 high requires it &gt; 0
    requires low &lt;= high at .high
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Range {
    i64 low
    i64 high requires it &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; it &gt;= low
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3218:</span> this `requires` reads `it` and other fields
  --&gt; main.byn:3:14

  3 │     i64 high requires it &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; it &gt;= low
    │              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: split it: an `it` rule under the field, and a struct-level rule with `at .field`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3218</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn3219">BYN3219: not a mutation operator</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>{0}</code> is not a mutation operator"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "use one of <code>boundary</code>, <code>relational</code>, <code>logical</code>, <code>condition</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>arithmetic</code>, <code>literal</code>, <code>negation</code>, <code>statement</code>, <code>emptyReturn</code>, <code>variant</code>, or no argument for all"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> The argument of <code>@unobservable(operator)</code> names no mutation operator.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An operator argument narrows a mark to one kind of mutant. The names are the operators of the mutation-testing table; a misspelled one would silently mark nothing.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of the listed operators, or drop the argument to cover every operator in the statement.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 f(i32 x) {
    @unobservable(bogus) because "x is clamped by the caller"
    return x
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN3219:</span> `bogus` is not a mutation operator
  --&gt; main.byn:2:19

  2 │     @unobservable(bogus) because "x is clamped by the caller"
    │                   ^^^^^ not an operator
    │
  help: use one of `boundary`, `relational`, `logical`, `condition`, `bool`, `arithmetic`,
        `literal`, `negation`, `statement`, `emptyReturn`, `variant`, or no argument for all</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN3219</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g40">Toolchain (BYN40xx)</h2>
<p>Failures that are not in your program.</p>
<h3 id="byn4001">BYN4001: internal compiler error, the C compiler rejected the generated C</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> <code>bayan build</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>test</code> or <code>dist</code> produced C that the C compiler (zig cc, <code>$BAYAN_CC</code> or <code>cc</code>) refused. The C compiler's own messages follow the error.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A program that <code>bayan check</code> accepts must compile; a C compiler error that reaches you is a bug in the Bayan compiler, not in your code (spec §24). The generated C carries <code>#line</code> directives, so the C compiler's messages name the <code>.byn</code> file and line of the statement it rejected.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Report it with the C text and the <code>.byn</code> lines it names. <code>bayan build --emit-c</code> prints the generated C; a project's last build keeps it in <code>out/&lt;mode&gt;/&lt;target&gt;/c/main.c</code>. Meanwhile, rewriting the named statement differently may avoid the bug.</p>

<h2 id="g50">Naming rules (BYN50xx)</h2>
<p>Names that break the naming grammar. These apply to all code.</p>
<h3 id="byn5001">BYN5001: <code>-ed</code> member that takes <code>ref this</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A member is named with the <code>-ed</code> form of a verb (<code>sorted</code>, <code>trimmed</code>, <code>joined</code>) but takes <code>ref this</code>, so it changes <code>this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The <code>-ed</code> name is reserved (spec §5.2, [grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) tree 1 and 2): it names a changed copy or a view of <code>this</code> and never changes it. A member that acts on <code>this</code> is a plain verb on <code>ref this</code> (<code>sort</code>, <code>trim</code>). The <code>-ed</code> form does not need a plain verb to exist: an immutable type such as <code>string</code> has <code>trimmed()</code> with no <code>trim</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> changes <code>this</code>; name it <code>{1}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> name the member that changes <code>this</code> with the plain verb, and keep <code>-ed</code> for the copy or view</p>
<p><strong>What counts as <code>-ed</code>.</strong> One lowercase word of five letters or more ending in <code>-ed</code>, on a member that takes <code>ref this</code> and does not return <code>bool</code>. The plain verb offered in the message is found by dropping <code>-ed</code> (<code>sorted</code> → <code>sort</code>), dropping only the <code>d</code> (<code>reversed</code> → <code>reverse</code>), undoing a doubled consonant (<code>trimmed</code> → <code>trim</code>) or <code>-ied</code> (<code>copied</code> → <code>copy</code>). Not participles: words ending in <code>-eed</code> (<code>need</code>, <code>speed</code>, <code>seed</code>), shorter words (<code>red</code>, <code>bed</code>, <code>used</code>), and <code>hundred</code>, <code>embed</code>, <code>shred</code>, <code>naked</code>, <code>wicked</code>, <code>sacred</code>, <code>kindred</code>. An irregular form declared in <code>forms</code> in the project dictionary counts too (<code>forms: ["regrind": "reground"]</code>; see [BYN5014](BYN5014.md)). Not checked: math vocabulary (<code>sqrt</code>, <code>radians</code>, …), and typestate states (<code>states { paid, shipped }</code>), which are not members. A <code>bool</code> member named <code>closed</code> is a state: see [BYN5102](BYN5102.md).</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the member that changes <code>this</code> with the plain verb, and keep <code>-ed</code> for the copy or the view:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Words {
    string text

    void sort(ref this) { … }
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Words {
    string text

    // `-ed` names the changed copy; the member that changes `this` is `sort`.
    void sorted(ref this) {
        text = text
    }

    // A copy or a view may be `-ed` with no `ref this` verb beside it.
    string joined() =&gt; text

    Words reversed =&gt; Words { text: text }

    // `trimmed` undoes the doubled consonant: the verb is `trim`.
    void trimmed(ref this, i64 times) {
        text = text
    }

    // Words that only end in `-ed` and math vocabulary are not participles.
    void need(ref this) {
        text = text
    }

    f64 sqrt =&gt; 0.0

    states {
        drafted
        shipped
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5001:</span> `sorted` changes `this`; name it `sort`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:10

  5 │     void sorted(ref this) {
    │          ^^^^^^
    │
  help: name the member that changes `this` with the plain verb, and keep `-ed` for the copy or view</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5002">BYN5002: state member modifies <code>this</code></h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A member with no arguments named <code>isX</code>, <code>hasX</code> or <code>canX</code> is declared <code>ref this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>is</code>, <code>has</code> and <code>can</code> name a state (spec §5.2, [grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) tree 3): a reader expects <code>if q.isEmpty</code> to look, not to change <code>q</code>, and a state member is callable on a <code>let</code>. A member that changes <code>this</code> is an action, named with a verb on <code>ref this</code> (tree 1). A member with arguments (<code>hasRoomFor(n)</code>) is not checked.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>ref this</code> so the member only reads:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Queue {
    i64 count

    bool isEmpty() =&gt; count == 0
}</code></pre>
<p>If it must change <code>this</code>, name the action with a verb: <code>bool drain(ref this)</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Queue {
    i64 count

    bool isEmpty(ref this) {
        count = 0
        return true
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5002:</span> `isEmpty` reads as state but modifies `this`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:10

  4 │     bool isEmpty(ref this) {
    │          ^^^^^^^ declared `ref this`
    │
  help: remove `ref this` so it only reads, or name the action with a verb</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5003">BYN5003: <code>tryX</code> with the wrong return type</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A hand-written <code>tryX</code> returns something other than <code>T?</code> or <code>bool</code>, and the type has an <code>x()</code> that <code>fails</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>tryX</code> is reserved (spec §5.2, [grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) tree 4): it is the attempt form of <code>x() fails</code>, derived from it, and it returns <code>T?</code> (the value or <code>null</code>) or <code>bool</code> (whether it worked). A <code>tryX</code> with another return type reads as an attempt but does not report failure the way every other <code>tryX</code> does.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove <code>tryParse</code> and call <code>try parse()</code>, or return <code>i64?</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    i64? tryParse() =&gt; null</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">union error ParseError { empty }

struct Reader {
    string text

    i64 parse() fails ParseError {
        return 0
    }

    i64 tryParse() =&gt; 0

    // `T?` and `bool` are the two shapes of an attempt.
    i64? tryParseOrNull() =&gt; null
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5003:</span> `tryParse` must return `i64?` or `bool`; it is derived from `parse() fails`
  --&gt; main.byn:10:5

  10 │     i64 tryParse() =&gt; 0
     │     ^^^ returns `i64`
     │
  help: change it to return `i64?` or `bool`, or remove `tryParse` and call `try parse()`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5004">BYN5004: too many unlabeled parameters</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A particle is part of a function's name: <code>copy(from: a, to: b)</code> says what each argument is for, where <code>copy(a, b)</code> leaves the reader to guess. A function has at most one unlabeled required parameter, the object; every other one starts with a particle (<code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>as</code>). The receiver of a method never counts as the object. <code>bool</code> parameters, parameters with a default, <code>extern</code> declarations, operators, union and state payloads and laws are exempt.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> has {1} unlabeled parameters; give each but one a particle</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> keep one unlabeled parameter, the object</p>
<p>Each parameter besides the object is also reported as BYN5006.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Keep the object unlabeled and give the others a particle by meaning:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void transfer(i64 amount, from i64 payer, to i64 payee) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void transfer(i64 amount, i64 payer, i64 payee) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5004:</span> `transfer` has 3 unlabeled parameters; give each but one a particle
  --&gt; main.byn:1:6

  1 │ void transfer(i64 amount, i64 payer, i64 payee) {
    │      ^^^^^^^^ 3 unlabeled parameters
    │
  help: keep one unlabeled parameter, the object</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5005">BYN5005: argument passed without its particle</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A particle is part of the function's name, so the call writes it: <code>width(2, until: 5)</code> reads as the declaration does. An argument for a parameter with a particle, passed without it, is reported; this also catches a swapped pair, which the same-type naming rule (BYN2307, retired) used to.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is passed without its particle <code>{1}:</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> add <code>{1}:</code> before the argument</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the particle: <code>width(2, until: 5)</code>. The diagnostic carries this fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 width(i64 start, until i64 end) =&gt; end - start

print($"{width(2, 5)}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5005:</span> `end` is passed without its particle `until:`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:19

  3 │ print($"{width(2, 5)}")
    │                   ^ needs `until:`
    │
  help: add `until:` before the argument
    │ 3 │ print($"{width(2, until: 5)}")
    │   │                   +++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5005</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5006">BYN5006: parameter needs a particle</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function has one unlabeled required parameter, the object; every other required parameter starts with a particle that names its role (BYN5004). This code marks each parameter that needs one. The receiver of a method is not the object, so <code>record(ref this, i64 amount, i64 fee)</code> has two unlabeled parameters.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is a required parameter besides the object and needs a particle</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> label it with <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>as</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Choose the particle by meaning: a source or start takes <code>from</code>, a destination <code>to</code>, an exclusive end <code>until</code>, an inclusive end <code>through</code>, a position <code>at</code>, a replacement <code>by</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    void record(ref this, i64 amount, by i64 fee) {
        total += amount - fee
    }</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Ledger {
    i64 total

    void record(ref this, i64 amount, i64 fee) {
        total += amount - fee
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5006:</span> `fee` is a required parameter besides the object and needs a particle
  --&gt; main.byn:4:43

  4 │     void record(ref this, i64 amount, i64 fee) {
    │                                           ^^^ needs a particle
    │
  help: label it with `from`, `to`, `until`, `through`, `at`, `by`, `for` or `as`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5007">BYN5007: a log level used as the verb</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>Log.{0}</code> names a level, not the act; write <code>log(…, as: .{0})</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> call <code>log</code> and pass the level <code>as: .{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A call such as <code>Log.debug("cache miss")</code> names the level where the act should be.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>log</code> is the verb; the level says how the message is logged, so it is an argument with the particle <code>as</code> ([grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) §10): <code>log("cache miss", as: .debug)</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Call <code>log</code> and pass the level:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">log("cache miss", as: .debug)</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">Log.debug("cache miss")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5007:</span> `Log.debug` names a level, not the act; write `log(…, as: .debug)`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ Log.debug("cache miss")
    │ ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: call `log` and pass the level `as: .debug`
    │ 1 │ log("cache miss", as: .debug)
    │   │ ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5008">BYN5008: not a particle</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A parameter may start with a particle that names its role: <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code> or <code>as</code>. Any other word in that position, followed by a passing mode or a type and a name, is not one.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is not a particle</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> choose one of <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>at</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code></p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Choose one of the eight particles, or remove the word:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void copy(from Buf src, to Buf dst) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void copy(over Buf src, to Buf dst) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5008:</span> `over` is not a particle
  --&gt; main.byn:1:11

  1 │ void copy(over Buf src, to Buf dst) {
    │           ^^^^ not a particle
    │
  help: choose one of `from`, `to`, `until`, `through`, `at`, `by`, `for`, `as`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5008</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5009">BYN5009: a particle as a member name</h3>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A member is named with a particle (<code>at</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>by</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>through</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>as</code>), or an instance member spells a conversion with <code>to</code> or <code>as</code> (<code>toList</code>, <code>toString</code>, <code>asSpan</code>). The checker runs on all code, public or not.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is a particle; name the member with a verb or a noun</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> rename the member, for example <code>tryGet(at:)</code> instead of <code>at(…)</code></p>
<p>For an instance <code>toX</code> or <code>asX</code> member:</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> spells a conversion with <code>{1}</code>; write <code>{2}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> use a view noun, <code>copy()</code>, or a <code>T(x)</code> conversion</p>
<p><code>{2}</code> is the form to write instead: the view noun for <code>asX</code> (<code>asCount</code> → <code>count</code>), and the conversion <code>X(x)</code> for <code>toX</code>, with a primitive type in lowercase (<code>toString</code> → <code>string(shelf)</code>, <code>toList</code> → <code>List(shelf)</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Members are verbs or nouns ([grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) §1); a particle labels an argument (<code>tryGet(at: k)</code>) and says nothing on its own. A conversion already has a form: a view is a noun (<code>xs.span</code>), an owned copy is <code>copy()</code>, and a conversion is <code>T(x)</code> (<code>List(seq)</code>, <code>string(x)</code>). Static factories keep <code>from</code> (<code>T.fromJson(…)</code>, <code>T.tryFrom(x)</code>) and are not checked, nor is any static member.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the member with a verb or a noun, and write a conversion as <code>T(x)</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Shelf {
    i64 count

    i64 tryGet(at i64 index) =&gt; count + index
}

let text = string(shelf)</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Shelf {
    i64 count

    i64 at(i64 index) =&gt; count + index

    string toString() =&gt; ""
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5009:</span> `at` is a particle; name the member with a verb or a noun
  --&gt; main.byn:4:9

  4 │     i64 at(i64 index) =&gt; count + index
    │         ^^
    │
  help: rename the member, for example `tryGet(at:)` instead of `at(…)`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5009</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5011">BYN5011: <code>expects</code> reads one parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "this <code>expects</code> reads only <code>{0}</code>; a type with <code>requires</code> checks it once"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "declare a type with <code>requires</code> for <code>{0}</code>"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A function's <code>expects</code> clause tests a single parameter and nothing else. This is a warning.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A condition on one value is a fact about that value, not about the call. A type with <code>requires</code> checks it once, when the value is built, and every function that takes the type can rely on it; an <code>expects</code> checks it again at each call and leaves other functions unprotected.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare a type that carries the rule, and take it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Percent = i64 requires it in 0..=100

i64 percent(Percent value) =&gt; value.value</code></pre>
<p>An <code>expects</code> that relates two parameters (<code>lo &lt;= hi</code>) is not reported. The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 percent(i64 value) expects value in 0..=100 {
    return value
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN5011:</span> this `expects` reads only `value`; a type with `requires` checks it once
  --&gt; main.byn:1:24

  1 │ i64 percent(i64 value) expects value in 0..=100 {
    │                        ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: declare a type with `requires` for `value`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5011</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5013">BYN5013: particle used twice</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A particle names one role in a signature, and a call is read by its particles: with two <code>from</code> parameters, <code>copy(from: a, from: b)</code> no longer says which is which. A particle appears at most once per declaration. Calls need no check, since such a declaration cannot exist.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> uses the particle <code>{1}</code> twice</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> group the values in a struct, or choose another particle</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Group the values in a struct, or choose another particle for one of them:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Sources {
    string src
    string backup
}

void copy(from Sources sources) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void copy(from string src, from string backup) {
    return
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN5013:</span> `copy` uses the particle `from` twice
  --&gt; main.byn:1:28

  1 │ void copy(from string src, from string backup) {
    │                            ^^^^ used again
    │
  help: group the values in a struct, or choose another particle</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5013</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5014">BYN5014: unknown word in a member name</h3>
<p>A warning: it never blocks a build.</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A word of a member name is not in the checker's built-in word list or in the project dictionary, so the checker cannot tell whether it is a verb, a noun or an adjective. Each word of a name is checked (<code>upsertRow</code> → <code>upsert</code>, <code>row</code>), on all code, public or not.</p>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is not a known verb or noun</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> add it to <code>grammar</code> in package.byn: <code>verbs: ["{0}"]</code>, <code>nouns: ["{0}"]</code> or <code>adjectives: ["{0}"]</code></p>
<p><strong>What counts as known.</strong> A word in the built-in list (<code>src/Bayan.Semantics/Lexicon.cs</code>; its verbs are also the verbs a help text may start with), a particle, or a word declared in <code>grammar</code> in <code>package.byn</code>, compared in lowercase. Regular forms of a known word are known too: <code>-s</code>, <code>-es</code>, <code>-ies</code>, <code>-ed</code>, <code>-ing</code>, <code>-er</code>, <code>-or</code>, <code>-est</code>, <code>-ly</code>, with a doubled consonant undone, and <code>un-</code> or <code>re-</code> before a known word. An irregular form is declared in <code>forms</code>. Not checked: math vocabulary (<code>sqrt</code>, <code>log2</code>, <code>radians</code>, …), single letters and digits (<code>x</code>, <code>v2</code>), names with letters outside ASCII, and typestate states.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The grammar rules ([grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) §1) depend on the class of each word: a <code>ref this</code> member is named with a verb, a property with a noun. A domain word (<code>upsert</code>, <code>sku</code>, <code>kyc</code>) is declared once for the project rather than suppressed member by member.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the word in <code>package.byn</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">grammar: Grammar {
    verbs: ["upsert"],
}</code></pre>
<p>The fix that adds the word to <code>package.byn</code> is not offered yet: a fix edits the file of the diagnostic, and this one edits another file (handoff Question 272).</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Catalog {
    i64 count

    void upsertRow(ref this) {
        count += 1
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN5014:</span> `upsert` is not a known verb or noun
  --&gt; main.byn:4:10

  4 │     void upsertRow(ref this) {
    │          ^^^^^^
    │
  help: add it to `grammar` in package.byn: `verbs: ["upsert"]`, `nouns: ["upsert"]` or
        `adjectives: ["upsert"]`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5014</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g51">Public API names (BYN51xx)</h2>
<p>Names that break the rules for a package's public API.</p>
<h3 id="byn5101">BYN5101: noun on a <code>ref this</code> member</h3>
<p>A lint: it never blocks a build. It checks the public API only, a <code>public</code> member of a <code>public</code> struct; a name the module keeps to itself is not checked (grammar guide §1, checker scope).</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>ref this</code> member's name starts with a noun from the built-in word list: <code>size</code>, <code>length</code>, <code>total</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>values</code>, <code>data</code>, <code>item</code>, <code>items</code>, <code>element</code>, <code>elements</code>, <code>result</code>, <code>results</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>content</code>, <code>contents</code>, <code>capacity</code>, <code>amount</code>, <code>number</code>, <code>key</code>, <code>keys</code>, <code>entry</code>, <code>entries</code>, <code>node</code>, <code>nodes</code>. Only the first word of the name is checked (<code>sizeUp</code> → <code>size</code>). The list holds words that are rarely verbs; <code>count</code>, <code>list</code> and <code>set</code> are not on it.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A member that changes <code>this</code> is an act, named with a verb (spec §5.2, [grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) tree 1): <code>c.add(2)</code> says what happens to <code>c</code>; <code>c.total(2)</code> does not.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the action:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public struct Counter {
    i64 n

    public void add(ref this, i64 by) {
        n += by
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public struct Counter {
    i64 n

    public void total(ref this, i64 by) {
        n += by
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN5101:</span> `total` is a noun on a `ref this` member; name the action
  --&gt; main.byn:4:17

  4 │     public void total(ref this, i64 by) {
    │                 ^^^^^
    │
  help: rename it with the verb for what it does to `this`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5101</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn5102">BYN5102: <code>bool</code> state without <code>is</code>, <code>has</code> or <code>can</code></h3>
<p>A lint: it never blocks a build. It checks the public API only, a <code>public</code> member of a <code>public</code> struct; a name the module keeps to itself is not checked (grammar guide §1, checker scope).</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A <code>bool</code> property, or a <code>bool</code> method with no arguments that is not <code>ref this</code>, is named without the <code>is</code>, <code>has</code> or <code>can</code> prefix. Not reported: names starting with <code>contains</code>, <code>equals</code>, <code>matches</code>, <code>startsWith</code>, <code>endsWith</code> (verbs that ask a question) or <code>try</code> (an attempt, tree 4); <code>ref this</code> members (an action that reports success, such as <code>bool advance(ref this)</code>); fields.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A boolean state reads as a question with <code>is</code> / <code>has</code> / <code>can</code> (spec §5.2, [grammar guide](../bayan-grammar.md) tree 3): <code>if door.isLocked</code> says what is asked; <code>if door.locked</code> could be an action or a copy.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the prefix (the machine fix renames the declaration to <code>isX</code>; rename the callers with it):</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public struct Door {
    bool open

    public bool isLocked() =&gt; !open
}</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public struct Door {
    bool open

    public bool locked() =&gt; !open
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">warning BYN5102:</span> `locked` is a `bool` state; prefix with `is`, `has` or `can`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:17

  4 │     public bool locked() =&gt; !open
    │                 ^^^^^^
    │
  help: rename it `isLocked`
    │ 4 │     public bool isLocked() =&gt; !open
    │   │                 +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN5102</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g60">Effects (BYN60xx)</h2>
<p>A function does something its signature does not declare.</p>
<h3 id="byn6001">BYN6001: <code>noalloc</code>, <code>nosuspend</code> and <code>suspends</code> are removed</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A function without <code>uses</code> is already pure: it cannot allocate or suspend. What a function needs is named in <code>uses</code>, so the marker words add nothing. Writing one is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the word:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 twice(i32 n) =&gt; n * 2</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it. It is reported in signature position only: right after the parameters and again after <code>uses</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 twice(i32 n) noalloc =&gt; n * 2</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6001:</span> `noalloc` is removed; a function without `uses` is already pure
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ i32 twice(i32 n) noalloc =&gt; n * 2
    │                  ^^^^^^^ removed
    │
  help: remove `noalloc`
    │ 1 │ i32 twice(i32 n) noalloc =&gt; n * 2
    │   │                 --------</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn6002">BYN6002: a function uses an effect it does not declare</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A public function's signature is a contract: callers in other packages read its effects there, and no <code>uses</code> clause means pure. A function with a <code>uses</code> clause, public or not, promises the same. Private functions without <code>uses</code> infer their effects, and so do tests and scripts. The entry function is exempt whatever its visibility, because nothing calls it.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the effect:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public void greet(string name) uses console {
    print($"hello {name}")
}</code></pre>
<p>The effects come from <code>print</code> and the <code>Io</code> streams (<code>console</code>), <code>Io.readFile</code>, <code>Io.readText</code>, <code>Io.writeFile</code> and <code>Io.listDir</code> (<code>fs</code>), <code>Io.run</code> and <code>Io.runBatch</code> (<code>process</code>), <code>Io.monotonicNanos</code> (<code>clock</code>), and the allocating built-ins (<code>alloc</code>), and from calls of other functions. <code>log</code>, <code>panic</code> and <code>todo</code> are not effects.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">public void greet(string name) {
    print($"hello {name}")
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6002:</span> `greet` uses `console` but does not declare it
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     print($"hello {name}")
    │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ uses `console`
    │
  help: add `console` to the `uses` clause, or remove the call
    │ 1 │ public void greet(string name) uses console {
    │   │                               +++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn6003">BYN6003: not an effect</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The effect list is closed: <code>alloc</code>, <code>suspend</code>, <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>process</code> and <code>console</code>. A <code>uses</code> clause may also name an <code>effect</code> alias declared in the same module. An alias that refers to itself is reported here too.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the primitive effect, or declare an alias:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">effect disk = fs

void save() uses disk { }</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void save() uses disk { }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6003:</span> `disk` is not an effect
  --&gt; main.byn:1:18

  1 │ void save() uses disk { }
    │                  ^^^^ not an effect or an `effect` alias of this module
    │
  help: choose one of `alloc`, `suspend`, `net`, `fs`, `clock`, `random`, `env`, `process`,
        `console`, or a declared effect alias</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn6004">BYN6004: the package does not declare the capability</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>capabilities</code> in <code>package.byn</code> is an upper bound on what the package may do. A package without <code>.net</code> cannot contain a function that uses <code>net</code>, directly or through a call. Only <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>process</code> and <code>env</code> need a capability; the other effects are always allowed. A manifest with no <code>capabilities</code> field has no bound.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add the capability, or remove the use:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    capabilities: [.net, .fs],</code></pre>
<p>It is reported where the effect starts: at the built-in call, not at each function that calls it.</p>

<h3 id="byn6005">BYN6005: an implementation uses more than its interface allows</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An interface method's <code>uses</code> clause is an upper bound for every implementation, and no clause means pure. Code that calls through the interface relies on the bound, so an implementation may use fewer effects but not more.</p>
<p>The <code>uses</code> clause of a function type works the same way for a lambda or function stored in a local of that type: the call through the local relies on the bound. (A function-typed parameter is different: its effects are those of whatever the caller passes.)</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Remove the use, or widen the bound on the interface:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IPricer {
    i64 price(i64 cents) uses console
}</code></pre>
<p>A lambda stored in a local of a function type:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void stored() uses console {
    let f: i64 -&gt; void = x =&gt; print($"{x}")
    f(1)
}</code></pre>
<p>Widen the function type's bound to match:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">let f: i64 -&gt; void uses console = x =&gt; print($"{x}")</code></pre>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">interface IPricer {
    i64 price(i64 cents)
}

struct Logged : IPricer {
    public i64 price(i64 cents) uses console {
        print($"pricing {cents}")
        return cents
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6005:</span> `price` uses `console`, beyond the bound `pure` declared by `IPricer.price`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:16

  6 │     public i64 price(i64 cents) uses console {
    │                ^^^^^ uses `console`
    │
  help: declare `console` on `IPricer.price`, or pass a function without it</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6005</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn6006">BYN6006: <code>context (…) { }</code> is now <code>provide … { }</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A block that supplies a value to the code inside it is written <code>provide name: value { }</code>, without parentheses. <code>context (…) { }</code> is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write <code>provide</code> and drop the parentheses:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    provide allocator: arena {
        print("x")
    }</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void run(Arena arena) {
    context (allocator: arena) {
        print("x")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6006:</span> `context (…) { }` is now `provide … { }`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:5

  2 │     context (allocator: arena) {
    │     ^^^^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: write `provide allocator: arena { }` without the parentheses
    │ 2 │     provide allocator: arena {
    │   │     ++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn6007">BYN6007: not a handler</h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>provide</code> installs handlers, and the handler names are the effects plus <code>log</code>: <code>alloc</code>, <code>net</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>clock</code>, <code>random</code>, <code>env</code>, <code>process</code>, <code>console</code> and <code>log</code>. <code>suspend</code> parks the fiber itself and has nothing to intercept, so it has no handler.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the handler:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">    provide alloc: arena {
        print("x")
    }</code></pre>
<p><code>allocator</code> is still accepted for <code>alloc</code>. v0.1 lowers only <code>alloc</code>; the other handler names are BYN2022.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void run(Arena arena) {
    provide memory: arena {
        print("x")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN6007:</span> `memory` is not a handler; `provide` takes `alloc`, `net`, `fs`, `clock`, `random`,
`env`, `process`, `console` or `log`
  --&gt; main.byn:2:13

  2 │     provide memory: arena {
    │             ^^^^^^ not a handler
    │
  help: choose one of the handler names; `suspend` has no handler</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN6007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g70">States (BYN70xx)</h2>
<p>Use of a value in a state the compiler cannot prove.</p>
<h3 id="byn7001">BYN7001: value not known to be in the state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> needs <code>{1}</code> to be <code>.{2}</code>, but it is <code>{3}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> check the state with <code>is</code> or <code>switch</code> first</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A method with <code>this is .s</code>, or a parameter of a state type <code>T.s</code>, may run only on a value known to be in that state. Widening is free (<code>Order.paid</code> passes where <code>Order</code> is wanted), but the struct itself, or a state type listing other states, is not known to be in the one the callee needs.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Match the state with <code>is</code> or <code>switch</code> before the call, or pass a value whose type already names the state.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
        shipped
    }
    i64 receipt(this is .paid) =&gt; id
    i64 total(this is .draft) =&gt; receipt()
}

i64 refund(Order.paid order) =&gt; order.id

i64 archive(Order order is .paid or .shipped) =&gt; order.id

let order = Order.draft { id: 1 }
let any: Order = order
let refunded = refund(any)
let archived = archive(order)
let receipt = order.receipt()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7001:</span> `receipt` needs `this` to be `.paid`, but it is `Order.draft`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:34

  9 │     i64 total(this is .draft) =&gt; receipt()
    │                                  ^^^^^^^
    │
  help: check the state with `is` or `switch` first</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7002">BYN7002: payload read without knowing the state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> is a payload of <code>.{1}</code>; <code>{2}</code> is not known to be <code>.{1}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> match <code>.{1}</code> with <code>is</code> or <code>switch</code> before reading <code>{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A payload field exists only while the value is in a state that declares it. Reading it from a value that may be in another state would read storage that holds something else. A read is allowed when the value's type, an <code>is</code> test, a <code>switch</code> arm or a <code>becomes</code> call guarantees a state that has the field; with several states, each must have it with the same type.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Test the state first: <code>if order is .paid { … order.payment … }</code>, or take the state type <code>Order.paid</code> as the parameter.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
}

i64 paidAmount(Order order) =&gt; order.payment</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7002:</span> `payment` is a payload of `.paid`; `order` is not known to be `.paid`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:32

  9 │ i64 paidAmount(Order order) =&gt; order.payment
    │                                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: match `.paid` with `is` or `switch` before reading `payment`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7002</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7003">BYN7003: <code>become</code> the signature does not declare</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>become</code> needs <code>becomes .{0}</code> on <code>{1}</code> (with another target: <code>become .{0}</code> is not declared; <code>{1}</code> becomes <code>.{2}</code>; for a <code>becomes</code> call on <code>this</code>: <code>{0}</code> calls <code>{1}</code>, which changes the state; declare <code>becomes</code> on <code>{0}</code>)</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> add <code>becomes .{0}</code> to the signature of <code>{1}</code> (with another target: change the target to <code>.{2}</code>, or declare <code>becomes .{0}</code>; for a <code>becomes</code> call on <code>this</code>: add <code>becomes .{2}</code> to the signature of <code>{0}</code>)</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A transition is part of the method's signature: callers rely on <code>becomes .s</code> to know the state after the call. A <code>become</code> the signature does not declare would change the state behind their back. The same holds for a call on <code>this</code> to a method that <code>becomes .t</code>: it changes the caller's state, so the caller declares <code>becomes .t</code> too, and the call counts as its <code>become .t</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Declare the transition: <code>void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid { … }</code>. With no <code>becomes</code>, the diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix that adds the clause. With another target, change the <code>become</code> or the <code>becomes</code> so they agree. For a <code>becomes</code> call on <code>this</code> (<code>void erase(ref this) { reset() }</code>), declare the callee's target on the caller: <code>void erase(ref this) becomes .draft { reset() }</code>; the fix adds the clause when the caller has no <code>becomes</code>. On a <code>move this</code> or plain <code>this</code> method, the fix also makes the receiver <code>ref this</code>, since BYN7010 rejects <code>becomes</code> without it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
        shipped
    }
    void pay(move this is .draft, i64 amount) {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
    void ship(ref this is .paid) becomes .shipped {
        become .paid(1)
    }
    void settle(move this is .draft, i64 amount) expects amount &gt; id {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
    void charge(move this is .draft, i64 amount) fails {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7003:</span> `become` needs `becomes .paid` on `pay`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:9

  9 │         become .paid(amount)
    │         ^^^^^^
    │
  help: add `becomes .paid` to the signature of `pay`
    │ 8 │     void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
    │   │              +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7003</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7004">BYN7004: switch over states misses a state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> the switch does not handle state <code>.{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> add a <code>case .{0}</code> arm</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A <code>switch</code> on a value with states must handle every state the value may be in, as a <code>switch</code> on a union must handle every variant. A state type such as <code>Order is .paid or .shipped</code> needs only the states it lists. A <code>default</code> section or an irrefutable arm covers the rest.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Add a section for each missing state. In a statement <code>switch</code> the fix-it inserts an empty <code>case .s:</code> section after the last one.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
        shipped
    }
}

void show(Order order) {
    switch order {
        case .draft:
            print("draft")
        case .paid p:
            print($"{p.payment}")
    }
}

i64 rank(Order order) =&gt; order switch { .draft =&gt; 0, .paid(_) =&gt; 1 }

void shipped(Order order is .paid or .shipped) {
    switch order {
        case .paid:
            print("paid")
    }
}

void all(Order order) {
    switch order {
        case .draft:
            print("draft")
        default:
            print("other")
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7004:</span> the switch does not handle state `.shipped`
  --&gt; main.byn:11:5

  11 │     switch order {
     │     ^^^^^^
     │
  help: add a `case .shipped` arm
     │ 15 │             print($"{p.payment}")
     │ 16 │         case .shipped:
     │    │ ++++++++++++++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7004</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7005">BYN7005: literal of a struct with states names no state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> has states; a literal must name one: <code>{0}.{1} { … }</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> name the state the value starts in</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A struct with states is built as <code>T.state { fields…, payload… }</code>. No state is a safe default, so a plain <code>T { … }</code> or <code>T()</code> does not say which state the value starts in.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Name the state: <code>let order = Order.draft { id: 1 }</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
}

let order = Order { id: 1 }
let blank = Order()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7005:</span> `Order` has states; a literal must name one: `Order.draft { … }`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:13

  9 │ let order = Order { id: 1 }
    │             ^^^^^
    │
  help: name the state the value starts in</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7005</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7006">BYN7006: one state written inline</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0} {1} is .{2}</code> is an input form; write <code>{0}.{2} {1}</code> (without a name, in a local annotation or an alias: <code>{0} is .{2}</code> is an input form; write <code>{0}.{2}</code>)</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> write the state type <code>{0}.{2}</code></p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A value known to be in one state has the state type <code>T.s</code>. The inline form <code>T name is .a or .b</code> is for two or more states; with one state it is an input form.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Write the state type: <code>void ship(Order.paid order) { … }</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix, and <code>bayan format</code> applies it.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">void ship(Order order is .paid) { … }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7006:</span> `Order order is .paid` is an input form; write `Order.paid order`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:23

  1 │ void ship(Order order is .paid) { … }
    │                       ^^ one state
    │
  help: write the state type `Order.paid`
    │ 1 │ void ship(Order.paid order) { … }
    │   │                +++++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7007">BYN7007: unknown state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> has no state <code>.{1}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> check the states declared in <code>{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A state type <code>T.s</code>, a receiver <code>this is .s</code>, a <code>becomes .s</code> clause, a <code>become .s</code> or a state literal <code>T.s { … }</code> must name a state from the struct's <code>states { … }</code> block.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Use one of the declared states, or add the state to the struct's <code>states</code> block.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
    void ship(ref this is .lost) becomes .gone {
        become .gone
    }
}

void refund(Order.refunded order) {
}

void archive(Order order is .paid or .archived) {
}

let order = Order.missing { id: 1 }</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7007:</span> `Order` has no state `.lost`
  --&gt; main.byn:7:28

  7 │     void ship(ref this is .lost) becomes .gone {
    │                            ^^^^
    │
  help: check the states declared in `Order`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7007</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7008">BYN7008: state with the name of a static member</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> state <code>.{0}</code> has the same name as static member <code>{1}.{0}</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> rename the state or the static member</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>T.s</code> names a state of <code>T</code>, and <code>T.m</code> names a static member of <code>T</code>. A state and a static member with the same name would make <code>T.empty</code> mean two things.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Rename one of them, for example the static member to <code>makeEmpty</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Cart {
    i64 count
    states {
        empty
        full
    }
    static Cart.empty empty() =&gt; Cart.empty { count: 0 }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7008:</span> state `.empty` has the same name as static member `Cart.empty`
  --&gt; main.byn:4:9

  4 │         empty
    │         ^^^^^
    │
  help: rename the state or the static member</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7008</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7009">BYN7009: return without <code>become</code></h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> declares <code>becomes .{1}</code> but returns here without <code>become</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> add <code>become .{1}(…)</code> before returning, or fail</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A signature with <code>becomes .s</code> promises that the receiver is in state <code>.s</code> when the function returns normally, and callers rely on that state after the call. A <code>return</code>, or the end of the body, reached on a path that ran no <code>become</code> would leave the receiver in its old state. Only a failure (<code>fail</code>, a propagated error) or a call that never returns may leave without <code>become</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Run <code>become .paid(…)</code> on every path before the return, or fail on the paths that cannot make the transition: declare <code>fails</code> and <code>fail</code> instead of <code>return</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
    void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
        if amount &lt;= 0 {
            return
        }
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7009:</span> `pay` declares `becomes .paid` but returns here without `become`
  --&gt; main.byn:9:13

  9 │             return
    │             ^^^^^^
    │
  help: add `become .paid(…)` before returning, or fail</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7009</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7010">BYN7010: <code>becomes</code> without <code>ref this</code></h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> declares <code>becomes .{1}</code> but takes <code>{2}</code>; a transition needs <code>ref this</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> change the receiver of <code>{0}</code> to <code>ref this</code>, or return the new state as a value</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A transition only means something to a caller that still holds the value afterwards. A <code>move this</code> receiver ends the caller's ownership, and a plain <code>this</code> is a copy the caller never sees again, so <code>becomes</code> on either would change a state nobody can observe. <code>move this is .s</code> and <code>this is .s</code> stay valid for methods without <code>becomes</code>: they read or consume a value in a known state. A <code>become</code> in a method rejected here reports nothing more.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Take the receiver as <code>ref this</code>, keeping its state: <code>void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid { … }</code>. The diagnostic carries a machine-applicable fix that replaces <code>move</code> (or adds <code>ref</code> before a plain <code>this</code>). A method that ends the value's life should drop <code>becomes</code> instead.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
    void pay(move this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7010:</span> `pay` declares `becomes .paid` but takes `move this is .draft`; a transition needs
`ref this`
  --&gt; main.byn:7:14

  7 │     void pay(move this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
    │              ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: change the receiver of `pay` to `ref this`, or return the new state as a value
    │ 7 │     void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
    │   │              +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7010</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7011">BYN7011: <code>becomes</code> call on a <code>ref</code> parameter</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> changes the state of <code>{1}</code>, a <code>ref</code> parameter; a state changes in place only through <code>ref this</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> take <code>{1}</code> by <code>move</code> and return the new state as a value</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every place a value's state can change is visible in a signature, so a caller trusts what it knows about a value's state without reading any method body. A function that calls a <code>becomes</code> method on one of its <code>ref</code> parameters changes the caller's value behind a signature that says nothing about states. A transition in place happens only through the receiver of a <code>ref this</code> method that declares <code>becomes</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Consume the value and return the new state, with the transition in the signature (<code>Order.paid charge(move Order.draft order)</code>), or make the transition a <code>becomes</code> method of <code>Order</code> and call it on the caller's value.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
    void pay(ref this is .draft, i64 amount) becomes .paid {
        become .paid(amount)
    }
}

void charge(ref Order.draft order) {
    order.pay(2)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7011:</span> `pay` changes the state of `order`, a `ref` parameter; a state changes in place only
through `ref this`
  --&gt; main.byn:13:5

  13 │     order.pay(2)
     │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: take `order` by `move` and return the new state as a value</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7011</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn7012">BYN7012: assignment that changes the state</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> <code>{0}</code> would change state here; a state changes only through <code>become</code></p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> assign a value in the same state, or move the transition into a <code>becomes</code> method</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A state changes in place only through <code>become</code>, so every change shows in a <code>becomes</code> signature. A whole-value assignment to a <code>ref</code> parameter (or to <code>this</code>) whose type has states could swap the caller's value for one in another state without the caller knowing. It is allowed only when the target's type names exactly one state and the new value is in that state: <code>ref Order o</code> and <code>ref Order o is .paid or .shipped</code> can't be reassigned, since the callee does not know which state the caller had; <code>ref Order.paid o</code> can take another <code>Order.paid</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Assign a value in the state the parameter names (<code>ref Order.paid o</code> takes <code>Order.paid { … }</code>), write fields through a <code>ref this</code> method of the struct, or move the transition into a <code>ref this</code> method that declares <code>becomes .draft</code> and runs <code>become .draft</code>.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Order {
    i64 id
    states {
        draft
        paid(i64 payment)
    }
}

void put&lt;T&gt;(move T v, to ref T x, at ref Order o) {
    o = Order.paid { id: 1, payment: 5 }
    x = move v
}

void main() {
    var a: i64 = 1
    var o = Order.draft { id: 1 }
    put(2, to: ref a, at: ref o)
    put(3, to: ref a, at: ref o)
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN7012:</span> `o` would change state here; a state changes only through `become`
  --&gt; main.byn:10:5

  10 │     o = Order.paid { id: 1, payment: 5 }
     │     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
     │
  help: assign a value in the same state, or move the transition into a `becomes` method</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN7012</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="g80">Bypasses and trust (BYN80xx)</h2>
<p>Places where a safety check is turned off, or untrusted data is used.</p>
<h3 id="byn8001">BYN8001: a safety bypass without <code>because</code></h3>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Some constructs switch off a check the compiler would otherwise make: <code>@allow</code>, <code>@json(expose)</code>, a call to a member ending in <code>Unchecked</code>, and <code>unsafe</code>. Each one names its reason in a <code>because "…"</code> clause on its line, so a reviewer and <code>bayan audit</code> can see why (spec §4.4). An empty reason is rejected too.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Explain the bypass in a <code>because</code> clause:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@allow(unusedValue) because "warming only fills the cache"
void warm(Cache cache) {
    cache.load()
}</code></pre>
<p>There is no automatic fix: the reason is human text. This version reports <code>@allow</code>, <code>@json(expose)</code> and calls to a member ending in <code>Unchecked</code> without a reason, and any empty reason; <code>unsafe</code> is checked once <code>unsafe</code> blocks parse.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">@allow(unusedValue)
void warm(Cache cache) {
    cache.load()
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN8001:</span> `@allow` bypasses a safety rule and needs `because "…"`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:1

  1 │ @allow(unusedValue)
    │ ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ bypasses a safety rule
    │
  help: explain the bypass in a `because` clause</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN8001</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="byn8006">BYN8006: <code>expects</code> reads untrusted data</h3>
<p><strong>Message.</strong> "<code>expects</code> reads <code>{0}</code>, which is <code>Untrusted</code>; a violation would trap on outside data"</p>
<p><strong>Help.</strong> "validate <code>{0}</code> with a type that has <code>requires</code> before the call"</p>
<p><strong>What.</strong> A function's <code>expects</code> clause reads a parameter of type <code>Untrusted&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A violated <code>expects</code> traps in every build mode. Outside data can break any rule, so checking it with <code>expects</code> lets whoever sends the data stop the program. Outside data is validated where it enters, with a <code>requires</code> type whose construction fails with a <code>ValidationError</code> the caller handles.</p>
<p><strong>Fix.</strong> Validate the value into a type with <code>requires</code> before the call, and take that type:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">type distinct Name = string requires it.length &lt;= 64

i64 fit(Name name, by i64 limit) =&gt; limit</code></pre>
<p>The diagnostic has no machine-applicable fix.</p>
<p>A program that triggers it:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i64 fit(Untrusted&lt;string&gt; name, by i64 limit) expects name.length &lt;= limit {
    return limit
}</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN8006:</span> `expects` reads `name`, which is `Untrusted`; a violation would trap on outside data
  --&gt; main.byn:1:55

  1 │ i64 fit(Untrusted&lt;string&gt; name, by i64 limit) expects name.length &lt;= limit {
    │                                                       ^^^^
    │
  help: validate `name` with a type that has `requires` before the call</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN8006</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="retired">Retired and reserved codes</h2>
<p>These codes are no longer reported, or are not reported yet. The numbers stay reserved so they are never reused.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>BYN1030</strong>: retired: the rewrite moved to <code>bayan format</code> <code>style.primitives</code>; never reuse</li>
<li><strong>BYN1032</strong>: retired, <code>if !x</code> is no longer rewritten</li>
<li><strong>BYN1049</strong>: retired, merged into BYN1007</li>
<li><strong>BYN2026</strong>: declaration as a statement body</li>
<li><strong>BYN2113</strong>: recursive struct</li>
<li><strong>BYN2116</strong>: operator with <code>ref this</code></li>
<li><strong>BYN2224</strong>: interpolation not allowed here</li>
<li><strong>BYN2307</strong>: arguments in the wrong order</li>
<li><strong>BYN2322</strong>: retired, <code>requires</code> is checked at construction</li>
<li><strong>BYN2707</strong>: recursive union</li>
<li><strong>BYN2762</strong>: a <code>Map</code> cannot be copied</li>
<li><strong>BYN3213</strong>: overlapping overloads</li>
<li><strong>BYN3215</strong>: reference to an overloaded name</li>
<li><strong>BYN3217</strong>: borrow crosses a fiber</li>
</ul>
